{[%evp 0,60,19,38,40,41,41,32,50,22,15,22,86,84,64,14,37,27,14,97,80,69,62,52,51,20,0,-124,-102,-124,-140,-144,-141,-138,-138,-128,-82,-153,-156,-156,-145,-147,-153,-146,-148,-146,-135,-185,-164,-195,-175,-175,-175,-175,-175,-176,-149,-232,-201,-251,-249,-645,-660] This year the final match of the Magnus Carlsen Invitational was without the World Champion himself. However, the battle between Giri and Nepo for the top honour was both exciting and entertaining. Despite only two decisive rapid games, we saw nine Sicilians (!) out of 10 games (only in the last blitz game Ian went 1.b3) and some pretty sharp fights. All in all Anish deserved his victory, we'll check his best performance.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 ({Nepo relied on the Paulsen/Taimanov, but when he was trailing 3:4 and needed to win to stay in the match, he opted for} 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 e6 4. Bxc6 bxc6 5. d3 Ne7 6. Nbd2 (6. h4 h5 7. e5 d6 8. exd6 Ng6 9. Nfd2 Bxd6 10. Nc4 Be7 11. Nc3 Ba6 12. Qf3 (12. Bd2 $142 $5 {} Bxc4 13. dxc4 Qd4 14. Qe2 Nxh4 15. g3 Ng6 16. f4 $1 O-O-O 17. O-O-O $44 {[%csl Rc5,Rc6]  Landa}) 12... Bxc4 13. Qxc6+ Kf8 14. dxc4 Nxh4 15. O-O Nf5 16. Ne2 $6 (16. Qe4 $5) 16... Rc8 17. Qa4 $6 (17. Qe4 $1) 17... Rc7 18. Bf4 Rd7 19. c3 g5 20. Rad1 Rxd1 21. Rxd1 Qa8 $19 {, Giri,A (2779)-Carlsen,M (2875) Zagreb 2019}) 6... Ng6 7. h4 h5 8. e5 d6 $5 $146 ({A novelty, Black aims for a similar position as Carlsen.} 8... f6 9. Qe2 Qc7 10. Nc4 Ba6 11. Qe4 $1 Kf7 12. Bf4 $14 {/ retains the e5 and is advantageous for White; Black's  present a rather sorry picture here.}) 9. exd6 e5 {White played passively and} 10. Nc4 (10. Ng5 $5 $13 {[%cal Yd1f3]}) 10... Bg4 11. Bg5 f6 12. Bd2 Bxd6 13. Qe2 Bc7 14. O-O-O O-O 15. Kb1 Rb8 16. Rdg1 Nf4 17. Bxf4 exf4 18. Re1 Qd5 19. Qd1 Rfe8 20. b3 Kf7 21. a4 Rbd8 22. Ka2 Kg6 23. Rhg1 Kh6 24. Rh1 Qf7 25. Kb1 Be6 26. Nfd2 Bd5 27. Rhg1 Qg6 28. Rxe8 Rxe8 29. f3 (29. Qf1 Qg4 $17) 29... f5 $1 {[%csl Rh4][%cal Rg6g3,Rc7d8] gradually landed him in great trouble:} 30. Qf1 Qg3 31. Rh1 Bd8 32. Nd6 Rf8 33. N2c4 Bxh4 34. Ne5 Rf6 35. c4 Bg8 36. Nb7 Re6 37. Rh3 Rxe5 38. Rxg3 Bxg3 {0-1, Giri,A (2776)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2789) chess24.com INT 2021}) 3. d4 ({However, he tested also} 3. c3 d5 4. exd5 ({The first blitz game saw transposition to an Advance French} 4. e5 Nc6 5. d4 Bd7 6. Be2 Nge7 7. Na3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Ng6 9. Nc2 f6 10. exf6 gxf6 11. O-O Bd6 12. g3 h5 13. Bd3 Nce7 14. Ne3 h4 15. Ng4 Kf7 16. Re1 Qf8 $13 17. Nfe5+ $2 (17. Bg5 $5 fxg5 18. Nxg5+ Kg8 19. Nxe6 Qf7 20. Ng5 Qf8 $11) 17... fxe5 18. dxe5 Bc5 19. Bg5 hxg3 20. hxg3 Bc6 21. Rc1 d4 22. Be4 Bxe4 23. Rxe4 Nf5 24. Qa4 Rc8 25. Bf6 Rh7 $2 (25... Rg8 26. Qd7+ Be7 $17) 26. Qd7+ Nge7 27. Bxe7 Qxe7 28. Qxc8 $18 {Giri,A (2776)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2789) chess24.com INT 2021}) 4... Qxd5 5. d4 Nf6 6. Na3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nb5 Qd8 9. Nbxd4 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Be7 11. Bd3 O-O 12. Qc2 h6 13. O-O-O Qa5 14. Kb1 $14 {Giri,A (2776)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2789) chess24.com INT 2021}) 3... cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. Nc3 a6 (5... Qc7 6. Be3 a6 7. g4 ({Then a couple of years ago White came up with a different setup, preparing  castling with} 7. Qf3 {Here after} Ne5 8. Qg3 {the most recent idea is} Bd6 $5 (8... b5 {and especially}) (8... h5 $5 {, introduced at the top level by Giri himself, are considered to be the main lines.}) 9. f4 Ng6 10. e5 Bb4 {Po} 11. O-O-O (11. Bd2 d6 12. Nf3 N8e7 13. Bd3 dxe5 14. fxe5 Nf5 15. Qg4 Be7 (15... Bxc3 $5 16. Bxc3 Bd7 $132) 16. O-O-O O-O 17. Rde1 (17. Qh5 $5 $36 {[%cal Rg2g4]}) 17... b5 18. h4 Bb7 19. Bxf5 exf5 20. Qxf5 b4 21. Nd1 $6 (21. Ne4 $1 $13) 21... Rfd8 22. h5 Nf8 23. Ne3 Rac8 24. Ng5 Bxg5 25. Qxg5 h6 26. Qf5 Ne6 $44 {, Andreikin,D (2725)-Chigaev,M (2631) Chess.com INT 2021}) 11... Bxc3 12. bxc3 N8e7 13. Bd3 (13. c4 b5 $1 14. cxb5 O-O 15. h4 h5 16. Bd3 axb5 17. Nxb5 Qa5 18. a4 Qxa4 $40 {[%csl Rc1] 0-1 (86) Erdogdu,M (2396)-Chigaev,M (2631) Chess.com INT 2021}) 13... b5 14. Rhf1 Nd5 15. Bd2 Bb7 16. Rde1 Nge7 17. f5 O-O-O 18. fxe6 fxe6 19. Rf7 Kb8 20. Qf2 Qa5 21. Kb2 b4 22. c4 Qa3+ 23. Ka1 Nc3 24. Bxc3 Qxc3+ 25. Kb1 Nc8 26. Rf1 Nb6 27. Ne2 Qxe5 28. Qxb6 Qxh2 29. Be4 Qc7 30. Qxb4 g6 31. Nd4 Rhe8 32. Bxb7 Qxb7 33. Nc6+ {1-0 (33) So,W (2772)-Artemiev,V (2704) chess24.com INT 2021}) (7. Qd2 Nf6 8. O-O-O {or} Be7 $5 {was popular in the previous decade and leads to a complex struggle.} (8... Bb4 $13) 9. f3 b5 10. g4 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Bb7 12. Kb1 O-O 13. h4 b4 14. Na4 d5 15. Nb6 Rad8 16. e5 Ne4 17. fxe4 dxe4 18. Qe2 Rxd4 19. Rxd4 Qxb6 20. Rd7 Bc5 21. Bg2 Bd5 22. Bxe4 Qc6 23. Rxd5 exd5 24. Bf3 Qe6 25. Rd1 Rd8 26. g5 Kh8 27. Qc4 Bf8 28. Qf4 d4 29. Bg4 Qd5 30. h5 Be7 31. h6 gxh6 32. gxh6 Rg8 33. e6 fxe6 34. Qf7 Qg5 35. Qxe6 Rg6 36. Qc8+ Rg8 37. Qc7 Bf6 38. Be6 Rf8 39. Qd6 Qxh6 40. Qxb4 d3 41. c3 Bg7 42. Bc4 d2 43. Bd3 Qe3 44. Kc2 Rd8 45. Bxa6 Qf3 46. Rxd2 Rxd2+ 47. Kxd2 Qd5+ 48. Kc2 Qxa2 49. Qb8+ Qg8 50. Qxg8+ Kxg8 51. b4 Kf7 52. c4 Ke6 53. c5 Bf8 54. Bb7 Ke5 55. Kd3 h5 56. Kc4 h4 57. Bc8 Kf6 58. b5 Ke7 59. b6 Kd8 60. Bh3 Bh6 61. c6 Bf4 62. Kb5 Be5 63. Ka6 Bf4 64. Kb7 Be5 65. c7+ {1-0 (65) So,W (2772)-Artemiev,V (2704) chess24.com INT 2021}) (7. Be2 b5 8. Nxc6 Qxc6 9. e5 Bb7 10. Bf3 Qc7 11. Bxb7 Qxb7 12. Qd3 Ne7 (12... Bb4 13. O-O Bxc3 14. Qxc3 Ne7 15. Bc5 Rc8 16. b4 (16. Qa3 Nd5 17. b4 f5 18. Qg3 Kf7 $132 19. a4 $6 a5 $1 $15 {Gara,A (2340)-Horvath,G (2364) Hungary 2011}) 16... O-O 17. a4 Rfe8 18. axb5 axb5 19. Qd3 Nd5 20. f4 Ra8 21. Rxa8 (21. Ra5 Rxa5 22. bxa5 Ra8 $15 {[%csl Ra5,Rf4]}) 21... Rxa8 22. f5 f6 $1 23. exf6 Nxf6 24. Bd4 Rf8 25. fxe6 dxe6 26. Qg3 Nd5 27. Rxf8+ Kxf8 28. Bc5+ Kg8 29. Qe5 Qd7 30. Qd4 Nf6 $11 {Giri,A (2776)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2789) chess24.com INT 2021} 31. c4 bxc4 32. Qxc4 Qd1+ 33. Qf1 Qc2 34. Qf2 Qb1+ 35. Qf1 Qc2 36. Qf2 Qb1+ 37. Qf1 Qc2 {½-½ (37) Giri,A (2776)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2789) chess24.com INT 2021}) 13. Bc5 Qxg2 14. O-O-O Qc6 15. Ne4 Nf5 16. Bxf8 Rxf8 17. Rhe1 Rc8 18. Kb1 f6 19. f4 fxe5 20. fxe5 h6 21. Rg1 Qd5 22. Nd6+ Ke7 23. Nxc8+ Rxc8 24. Qxd5 exd5 25. Rxd5 Ke6 26. Rd3 g5 27. a4 bxa4 28. Rg4 Rc6 29. Rxa4 h5 30. Rda3 g4 31. Kc1 Kxe5 32. Rxa6 h4 33. Rxc6 dxc6 34. Kd1 h3 35. Ke2 g3 36. Kf3 gxh2 37. Ra1 Nd4+ 38. Kg3 Nxc2 39. Rc1 Nb4 40. Kxh2 Kd4 41. b3 c5 42. Kxh3 Nc6 43. Rc4+ Kd5 44. Kg2 Ne5 45. Rc1 c4 46. bxc4+ Nxc4 47. Rxc4 Kxc4 {½-½ (47) Carlsen,M (2847)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2792) chess24.com INT 2021}) (7. f4 b5 8. e5 Bb7 9. Be2 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Nh6 11. Bf3 Nf5 12. Bf2 Bb4 13. O-O Bxc3 14. bxc3 O-O 15. Bxb7 Qxb7 16. a4 Qc6 17. axb5 axb5 18. Qd3 Ra4 19. Rxa4 bxa4 20. c4 h5 21. c5 Rb8 22. Rd1 Ra8 23. Ra1 Ne7 24. h3 Qd5 25. Qe2 a3 26. c4 Qb7 27. Qxh5 Qb2 28. Qd1 Nc6 29. h4 a2 30. h5 Rb8 31. Be1 Qb1 32. Bc3 Qxd1+ 33. Rxd1 Rb1 34. Rf1 Nb4 35. g4 Nd3 {0-1 (35) Dubov,D (2710)-Caruana,F (2820) chess24.com INT 2021}) 7... b5 (7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 b5 9. O-O-O Bb7 10. Kb1 Nf6 11. g5 Ng4 12. Bf4 e5 13. Nd5 O-O-O 14. f3 exd4 15. Bxc7 Re8 16. Bg3 Bxd5 17. exd5 Ne3 18. Rxd4 Nf5 19. Rd1 Nxg3 20. hxg3 Kc7 21. a4 Rb8 22. axb5 axb5 23. Bd3 h6 24. gxh6 Rxh6 25. Rxh6 gxh6 26. Rh1 b4 27. Rh5 Rb6 28. Rf5 f6 29. Bc4 Bd6 30. f4 Bc5 31. d6+ Bxd6 32. Rxf6 Bc5 33. Rf5 Bd4 34. Rh5 Rg6 35. Rh3 Bf2 36. f5 Rxg3 37. Rxh6 Be3 38. Ra6 Bc5 39. f6 Rf3 40. f7 Rf5 41. Ka2 Kb7 42. Rh6 d5 43. Rh7 Kc6 44. Bd3 Rf6 45. Kb3 Bf8 46. Be2 Kc5 47. Bh5 Rh6 48. Rxh6 Bxh6 49. c3 bxc3 50. Kxc3 Bg7+ 51. Kb3 Kd6 52. Kc2 Ke7 53. b4 Bd4 54. Kd3 Ba7 55. Ke2 d4 56. Kd3 Kf8 57. Ke4 Ke7 58. b5 Kf8 59. Bg6 Bb6 60. Kd3 Ba7 61. Kc4 Bb6 62. Bh5 Ba7 63. Kb4 Bb6 64. Bg6 Ke7 65. Kc4 Kf8 66. Kd3 Ba7 67. Ke4 Bb6 68. Bh5 Ba7 69. Kd5 d3 70. Kc6 d2 71. b6 Bxb6 72. Kxb6 d1=Q 73. Bxd1 Kxf7 {½-½ (73) Nepomniachtchi,I (2792)-Caruana,F (2820) chess24.com INT 2021}) (7... h6 $146 {[%mdl 8] '!?' Although this is a novelty, it follows in Garri's footsteps - Black insists on playing f6 despite White's last move. We'll check the alternatives: A novelty, but the computers don't dislike this move too much and it didn't come as a shocker. The main move here is the standard 7...b5. After 7...h6 Black wants to slow down the g5 push.  Eine Neuerung, aber den Computern missfällt dieser Zug nicht allzu sehr, und er kam nicht als ein Schocker. Der Hauptzug hier ist das standardmäßige 7...b5. Nach 7...h6 will Schwarz den g5-Vorstoß verlangsamen.} 8. h4 {Preparing g5 slowly but surely. Bereitet langsam, aber sicher g5 vor.} Nf6 {Alternatively it is also possible to go for 8...b5 here. Alternativ ist es auch möglich, hier 8...b5 zu wählen.} 9. Nxc6 {White needs to prepare 9.g5 and this, followed by f3 serves that exact purpose. Weiß muss 9.g5 vorbereiten, und dies, gefolgt von Df3, dient genau diesem Zweck.} ({The more standard Sicilian idea of Die standardmäßigere Sizilianisch-Idee} 9. Rg1 {would be well met with würde gut mit} Bb4 $1 {when Black appears to get quite some counterplay in the centre, as the e4-pawn would be hanging. beantwortet, wonach Schwarz offenbar einiges Gegenspiel im Zentrum bekommt, da der e4-Bauer hinge.}) 9... bxc6 (9... Qxc6 10. Bg2 Ba3 11. Bc1 $5 $14 {and what now? White is already prepared to go g5,} Bb4 12. Bd2) 10. Qf3 {Protecting the h1 rook and threatening g5, but also preparing 0-0-0. Deckt den h1-Turm und droht g5, bereitet aber auch 0-0-0 vor.} d5 {It is a common question in such positions after a xc6 bxc6 trade- to start with d5 or b8 first. Very nuanced.  Es ist eine häufige Frage in solchen Stellungen nach einem Tausch Sxc6 bxc6 - mit ...d5 beginnen oder erst Tb8. Sehr nuanciert.} ({The text move is more natural than} 10... h5 11. gxh5 $5 (11. g5 Ng4 $132 {}) 11... Nxh5 12. O-O-O ({or} 12. Bc4 $5 $36 {White's attacking chances are more realistic due to his more active pieces, his  can also shield the  from b3.})) 11. g5 hxg5 12. hxg5 Rxh1 13. Qxh1 Ng4 {A tempo move. Ein Tempozug.} 14. Bd2 {I was prepared this far, but from here on I hadn't studied this position in too much depth, as both sides had plenty of deviations earlier on as well. The move Ian played here, set me thinking. So weit war ich vorbereitet, aber von hier an hatte ich die Stellung nicht sonderlich eingehend studiert, da beide Seiten auch vorher schon reichlich Abweichungen hatten. Der Zug, den Ian hier spielte, brachte mich zum Nachdenken.} Bb7 {Natural, preparing long castling and reinforcing the d5-pawn. I recalled the variations with bishop going to d7, which is far less human, obviously.  Natürlich, die lange Rochade wird vorbereitet und der Bauer d5 gefestigt. Ich erinnerte mich an die Varianten, in denen der Läufer nach d7 geht, was offensichtlich weit weniger menschlich ist.} (14... Bc5 $2 15. Qh8+ Ke7 16. O-O-O {[%csl Re7] } Nxf2 17. Qxg7 Qg3 (17... Nxd1 18. g6 $18) 18. Re1 $16 {/+- The position with the exposed e7 is unplayable.}) ({For this reason Black also spurns} 14... Rb8 15. b3 $16 {and prepares 0-0-0 instead.}) 15. Qh3 {First of all White has to chase the g4 away. I had seen this idea in similar positions, the queen is doing a fine job, controlling the third rank and pointing at the e6 pawn. Diese Idee hatte ich in ähnlichen Stellungen gesehen, die Dame leistet gute Arbeit, sie kontrolliert die dritte Reihe und zeigt auf den e6-Bauern.} (15. Be2 $5 {also deserves attention, after was my first instinct but after the knight retreat I didn't find the good follow up. war mein erster Instinkt, aber nach dem Springerrückzug fand ich die gute Fortsetzung nicht.} Ne5 16. Be3 {'!' is a very strong move pointed out by the engine, as indeed 16.f4?! g6! and 16.0-0-0 d4! give Black adequate counterplay. Now White just stops d4 and wants to castle long with a great position, as Black can't start advancing his central pawns with c5-d4 and is thus left without any follow up here. in einer sehr starker Zug, der von der Engine angezeigt wird, denn 16.f4?! Sg6! und 16.0-0-0 d4! gibt Schwarz in der Tat ausreichendes Gegenspiel. Nun verhindert Weiß einfach d4 und will lang rochieren, mit großartiger Stellung, da Schwarz seine Zentralbauern nicht mit c5-d4 in Gang setzen kann und somit hier um eine Fortsetzung verlegen ist.} (16. f4 $6 Ng6 $1) (16. O-O-O d4 $1) 16... O-O-O 17. O-O-O $14 {[%csl Ra6,Rc6][%cal Yf2f4,Yh1g1,Yc3a4] White has a more pleasant position due to his safer c1.}) 15... Ne5 16. O-O-O ({I coudn't get Nicht zum Funktionieren bringen konnte ich} 16. f4 Ng6 17. f5 exf5 18. exf5 {to work, due to a queen check on e5. wegen eines Damenschachs auf e5.}) 16... d4 17. Nb1 {I liked 17.a4 more, optically, but in some lines I didn't want my knight to be attacked with c5/c6. Now I want to push f4-f5 and Black has to play really well for his position not to crumble. 17.Sa4 gefiel mr optisch besser, aber in manchen Varianten wollte ich nicht, dass mein Springer mit c5/Lc6 gegriffen wird. Jetzt plane ich den Vorstoß f4-f5, und Schwazr muss richtig gut spielen, damit seine Stellung nicht einstürzt.} c5 {'?!' Black had to do some preventive thinking and should have taken the threat of f4 more seriously. Schwarz hätte ein wenig Präventivdenken vornehmen und die Drohung f4 ernster nehmen sollen.} ({More prudent was} 17... Ng6 $5 {'!' , but Black still can't claim full equality after doesn't look exciting, but having stopped f4 push, Black gets to keep his important e6 pawn intact. sieht nicht so spannend aus, aber nachdem er den f4-Vorstoß gestoppt hat, schafft es Schwarz, seinen wichtigen e6-Bauern intaktzuhalten.} 18. Na3 $14 c5 {I would be quite happy with the c4 square here for my knight, but Black has the important f4 square under his firm control and his position is very solid. Mit dem c4-Feld für meinen Springer wäre ich hier ganz zufrieden, aber Schwarz hat das wichtige Feld f4 fest im Griff, und seine Stellung ist ziemlich solide.}) (17... O-O-O $2 18. f4 Ng6 19. f5 $36 exf5 20. Qxf5+ Qd7 21. Ba5 $1 Qxf5 22. exf5 Rd5 23. fxg6 Rxa5 24. Re1 $1 $18) 18. f4 Nc6 $2 {We have reached the critical position and this serious error will cost Black dearly. Black goes for the "better" looking square, but this is just losing. The solution at this point was barely findable, though someone as gifted as Ian, could perhaps pull it off on a better day, had he taken some time here. Schwarz wählt das "besser" aussehende Feld, aber dies verliert einfach. Die Lösung an diesem Punkt war allerdings kaum zu finden, wenngleich jemand, der so begabt ist wie Ian, es an einem besseren Tag, hätte er sich hier etwas Zeit genommen, vielleicht schaffen könnte.} (18... Nd7 $2 {is not much better due to} 19. g6 $1 Bxe4 (19... c4 $3 {would still keep the game exciting here. würde die Partie hier noch immer spannend halten.}) 20. gxf7+ Kxf7 21. Bc4 $40 Bd5 22. Bxd5 exd5 23. Qh5+ $1 {This side check and not 23.Qf5+? Nf6! is important. The d5 pawn is falling and White wins. Dies Seitenschach und nicht 23.Df5+? Sf6! ist wichtig. Der d5-Bauer fällt und Weiß gewinnt.} (23. Qf5+ $2 Nf6 $1)) ({Black has to counter the g6 break, much stronger and practically necessary was} 18... Ng6 {'!' Black is anyway losing the battle for the light squares, so he should at least fight for the dark ones.  Den Kampf um die hellen Felder verliert Schwarz sowieso, daher sollte er zumindest um die dunklen kämpfen.} 19. f5 Ne5 $1 {This is counterintuitive for humans, as the e6 pawn seemed key, but actually it was neccessary to dig deeper here. Für Menschen ist dies kontraintuitiv, da der e6-Bauer ja der Schlüssel zu sein schien, tatsächlich aber war es nötig, hier tiefer zu schürfen.} (19... Nf4 $143 20. Qg4 e5 21. Na3 $16 {[%csl Gc4,Rf4][%cal Ra3c4,Rc4e5]}) (19... exf5 $143 20. exf5 Ne5 21. f6 $1 {[%csl Re8]} gxf6 22. Re1 $1 $40) 20. Bf4 $5 {seems to be the best:} (20. fxe6 {gives Black a lot of dark-square play after in view of the reply, this may not even be the best move. Angesichts der Erwiderung mag dies nicht einmal der beste Zug sein.} Bc8 $1 {'!!' Beautiful idea. For the pawn, Black has regained control over key squares - e5 and very importantly c4 as well. Wunderbare Idee. Für den Bauern hat Schwarz die Kontrolle über Schlüsselflder zurückerlangt - e5 und, ganz wichtig, auch c4.} (20... Bxe4 $2 21. Re1 $18 {[%cal Re1e8]}) 21. exf7+ Qxf7 22. Qa3 (22. Qg3 Bd6 {with a dynamic equality. White had some better options on move 20 though. mit dynamischem Ausgleich. Weiß hatte allerdings im 20. Zug bessere Optionen.}) 22... Bd6 23. Be2 Be6 $44 24. Rf1 Qd7 $44 {[%csl Rb1][%cal Yc5c4] , e.g.} 25. Rh1 Qc6 26. Rh8+ Ke7 27. Rxa8 Qxa8 $132) (20. g6 exf5 21. Bf4 (21. exf5 O-O-O 22. f6+ Kb8 $11) 21... fxg6 22. exf5 O-O-O 23. fxg6+ Kb8 $13 {[%cal Yf8d6]}) 20... Nd3+ (20... exf5 21. Qxf5 Bd6 22. Bxe5 $5 Bxe5 23. Bc4 Bc8 24. Qh7 $16) 21. Bxd3 Qxf4+ 22. Nd2 exf5 23. g6 $1 (23. exf5 Qxg5 $13 24. Re1+ Kd8) {White is playing on the light squares and} 23... f6 (23... fxg6 24. exf5 $14) ({Deserving a closer look was also} 23... fxe4 $5 24. Rf1 Qe3 25. gxf7+ Kd8 26. Qg2 Rb8 27. Bxe4 Bxe4 28. Qxe4 Qxe4 29. Nxe4 {[%csl Gf7] with a good endgame for White, but Black still has drawing chances after} c4 $14) 24. exf5 O-O-O 25. Kb1 $14 {is more pleasant for him, but cannot be compared to what happens in the game.}) 19. g6 $1 {After this blow the engine indicates Black is already fighting for a lost cause - the price for a mistake in a sharp Sicilian. The e6 pawn is doomed now. Black tries now to get his king to safety at least. Jetzt ist der e6-Bauer verloren. Schwarz versucht, zumindest seinen König in Sicherheit zu bringen.} O-O-O {This loses material.} ({However, playing with the  in the middle is no bed of roses after} 19... Nd8 20. gxf7+ Qxf7 21. Re1 $16 {/+- , for example} e5 22. Na3 $1 Qxa2 23. Bc4 Qa1+ 24. Nb1 $40 {[%csl Re8]}) 20. gxf7 Qxf7 21. Bc4 Kb8 {The king does remain quite safe on the queenside, but having lost a pawn for nothing and with no control over e5 and c4 squares, Black's position is hopeless. Der König steht am Damenflügel zwar recht sicher, aber nach dem ersatzlosen Verlust eines Bauern und ohne Kontrolle über die Felder e5 und c4 ist die Stellung von Schwarz hoffnungslos.} (21... Re8 {gives White a pleasant choice between} 22. Qf1 ({or the more direct} 22. f5 {} Nd8 23. fxe6 Nxe6 24. Rf1 $18)) 22. Bxe6 $18 {[%mdl 4]} Qc7 23. Bc4 ({If we want to be very strict,} 23. Na3 $5 {would have probably spared White a tempo or two in comparison with the game. However, Giri's choice is safe enough and doesn't give his opponent any realistic chances.}) 23... Ka7 {Here was the last important moment in the game. I started thinking for a while and then realized that I should stop wasting my time, as every move is just equally winning and I should just avoid time trouble and miracles. Hier war der letzte wichtige Moment in der Partie. Ich begann, eine Weile zu überlegen, und merkte dann, dass ich aufhören sollte, meine Zeit zu vergeuden da einfach jeder Zug gleichermaßen gewinnt und ich bloß Zeitnot und Wunder vermeiden sollte.} 24. Na3 {The knight will eventually get to c4. Der Springer wird am Ende nach c4 kommen.} Nb4 {Knight sits nicely on b4, but on its own it can offer no counterplay. Der Springer steht schön auf b4, aber allein kann er kein Gegenspiel bieten.} 25. Re1 Be7 26. Kb1 {Black is helpless. Schwarz ist hilflos.} Re8 27. e5 Bd8 28. Bb3 {Now the knight finally makes his grand entrance via c4. Black is dead lost. Jetzt hat der Springer endlich seinen großen Auftritt auf c4. Schwarz steht völlig pleite.} g5 29. Nc4 {[%csl Gb3,Gc4,Gd2,Ge5] White has an extra central , his pieces have excellent coordiantion - the end is approaching... The knight is heading towards d6. Der Springer strebt nach d6.} gxf4 30. Bxf4 {As some Soviet commentators used to say, White has the pawn and the compensation. Wie manche Sowjet-Kommentatoren zu sagen pflegten, Weiß hat den Bauern und die Kompensation.} Nd5 31. Bd2 Qg7 32. Nd6 Rh8 (32... Rxe5 33. Rxe5 Qxe5 34. Nxb7 Kxb7 35. Qd7+ $18) 33. Qe6 Bh4 {Trying some last tricks, but first of all 34.xb7 complications work, but also White can just move the rook over. Versucht ein paar letzte Tricks, aber einmal funktionieren nicht nur alle 34.Sxb7-Komplikationen, sondern Weiß kann den Turm auch einfach wegziehen.} (33... Qg2 34. Qf7 Nc7 35. Bc1 $18 {and the counterattack is over.}) 34. Rc1 Nc7 35. Qc4 {[%csl Rc5] The c5 pawn is now a target too. Jetzt ist auch der Bauer c5 ein Ziel.} Qxe5 {The only way to protect the c5, but it drops material.} 36. Nf7 Qh2 37. Qxc5+ Kb8 38. Qxd4 {It's a matter of taste now, as everything wins. Jetzt ist es eine Frage des Geschmacks, denn alles gewinnt.} (38. Nxh8 $18) ({or} 38. Ba5 $5 $18 {are also winning with ease.}) 38... Rf8 39. Bf4 Bg3 40. Bxg3 Qxg3 41. Qb4 {[%cal Rb4f8,Rf7d6] The d6 motif will net White at least an exchange. 1-0 (41) Giri,A (2776)-Nepomniachtchi,I (2789) chess24.com INT 2021 CBM 202 Extra [Giri, Stohl,I] Black is two pawns down with a weak king, but also there is a concrete issue of not being able to deal with d6. Schwarz hat zwei Bauern weniger bei schwachem König, aber es gibt auch das konkrete Problem, Sd6 nichts entgegenzusetzen zu haben.}) ({The bishop sortie} 7... Bb4 $2 {is not ideal due to} 8. Nxc6 Bxc3+ (8... bxc6 9. Qd4 $16) 9. bxc3 dxc6 (9... Qxc6 10. Qd4 Nf6 {runs into} 11. g5 $1 $16 {} Qxe4 $2 12. gxf6 Qxh1 13. fxg7 Rg8 14. Qf6 $18 {[%cal Re3g5,Rf6h6,Rh6h7]}) 10. Bc5 $1 {[%csl Gc5][%cal Ga3f8]} Nf6 11. f3 h5 12. g5 Nd7 13. Bd6 Qa5 14. Qd2 Ne5 15. Be2 Bd7 16. Rb1 O-O-O 17. Qd4 {[%csl Ra7,Re5] 1-0, Ponkratov,P (2582)-Matsenko,A (1993) Khanty-Mansiysk 2010}) ({Going for the Scheveningen} 7... d6 8. g5 $14 ({or} 8. Qe2 $5 {seems pleasant and promising for White, who can develop his forces more easily.})) 8. Nxc6 (8. Bg2 Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Ne7 10. O-O-O Bb7 11. Kb1 Nc6 12. Qb6 Qxb6 13. Bxb6 Ne5 14. f3 Rc8 15. Bd4 f6 16. Ne2 Bc5 17. Bxc5 Rxc5 18. Nf4 Rc7 19. Rhf1 Ke7 20. Rf2 g5 21. Nh5 Ng6 22. Bf1 Nf4 $1 23. Nxf4 gxf4 24. Bd3 Rc5 {[%csl Gb7,Rd3,Re4,Rf3,Rg4]} 25. Rfd2 Bc6 26. b4 Re5 27. h4 h5 $17 {Horvath,T (2368)-Ivanisevic,I (2547) Interlaken 2001}) 8... Qxc6 9. Qd2 (9. a3 Bb7 10. Qd2 Rc8 (10... b4 11. axb4 Bxb4 12. Qd4 Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 Nf6 14. f3 (14. g5 Nxe4 15. Qxg7 Qxc3+ 16. Qxc3 Nxc3 17. Rg1 O-O $13) 14... O-O 15. c4 d6 $6 (15... d5 $5 $13 {}) 16. Be2 Rfb8 17. g5 e5 18. Qd2 Ne8 19. Kf2 Qc7 20. Ra5 f6 21. Rd1 Rd8 22. Qb4 Qe7 23. c5 $18 {Le Page,C (2361)-Richards,J (1845) email 2010}) 11. f3 Nf6 (11... Ne7 $5) 12. g5 Nh5 13. Bh3 b4 14. axb4 Bxb4 15. Bg4 g6 16. Qd4 Bxc3+ 17. Qxc3 (17. bxc3 O-O 18. Bxh5 gxh5 19. Ra3 d6 $13) 17... Qxc3+ 18. bxc3 O-O 19. Kd2 (19. Bxh5 $142 $1 gxh5 20. Kd2 {[%csl Rb7,Ge3,Ge4,Gf3]} f5 21. gxf6 $14 {[%csl Rh5,Rh7]}) 19... f5 $1 20. exf5 exf5 21. Bxh5 gxh5 $11 {[%csl Gb7][%cal Ga8h1] Mekhitarian,K (2570)-Barbosa,E (2462) Buenos Aires 2015}) (9. Bg2 Bb7 10. a3 Ne7 11. Qd2 $2 (11. h4 $13) 11... Ng6 12. f4 Nh4 13. O-O-O b4 14. axb4 Bxb4 15. Bd4 Nxg2 16. Qxg2 O-O 17. Rhe1 a5 18. Qe2 Ba6 19. Qe3 a4 20. Bxg7 a3 $1 $40 21. Bf6 axb2+ 22. Kb1 Bc5 23. Bd4 Bc4 24. Bxc5 Rfb8 25. Ba7 Qa6 {0-1, Hernandez Gonzalez,W (2481)-Matlakov,M (2685) chess.com blitz INT 2020}) 9... b4 10. Ne2 Qxe4 11. Rg1 Qc4 12. Nf4 Qc7 13. O-O-O Nf6 14. Bg2 Bb7 15. g5 Ne4 16. Qd4 Nd6 17. Qxb4 Bxg2 18. Nxg2 Rb8 19. Qa4 a5 20. Bd2 Nc4 21. Bc3 Bb4 22. Bxg7 Rg8 23. Bf6 Be7 24. Bc3 Bxg5+ 25. Ne3 Nxe3 26. Rxg5 Rxg5 27. fxe3 Rbb5 28. Qd4 Rbf5 29. e4 Rf3 30. Kb1 f6 31. a4 Rg7 32. h4 h5 33. Qd2 Qf4 34. Qe2 Qg4 35. Qb5 Re7 36. Bxa5 Rf5 37. exf5 Qxd1+ 38. Ka2 exf5 39. Bb4 Rg7 40. Qxf5 Qg4 41. Qxf6 Qxb4 42. Qxg7 Qxa4+ 43. Kb1 Qxh4 44. Qe5+ Kd8 45. Qb8+ Ke7 46. Qe5+ Kd8 47. Qb8+ {½-½ (47) Karjakin,S (2758)-Rapport,R (2760) Stavanger 2021}) ({A short historic throwback:} 5... d6 6. g4 $5 h6 7. h4 a6 8. Bg2 Be7 9. Be3 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 e5 11. Qd1 Be6 12. Nd5 Rc8 13. c3 Nf6 14. Nxe7 Qxe7 15. g5 hxg5 16. hxg5 Rxh1+ 17. Bxh1 Ng4 18. Bd2 Qf8 $1 19. Qf3 Qh8 20. Bg2 Qh4 21. b3 d5 22. Qg3 Qxg3 23. fxg3 Rd8 $132 {=, Karpov,A (2720)-Kasparov,G (2700) Wch Moscow 1985 This was game 14 of the match, in which Kasparov became World Champion, its significance will soon become apparent.}) 6. Be3 Nf6 (6... Nge7 7. Nb3 Ng6 (7... Na5 8. Na4 Nec6 9. Nb6 Rb8 10. Nxa5 Nxa5 11. Qd2 Nc6 12. a3 d6 13. f4 Be7 14. O-O-O O-O 15. h4 Qc7 16. h5 Rd8 17. h6 g6 18. Qc3 Bf8 19. Be2 Bd7 20. f5 Be8 21. Rhf1 Qe7 22. fxg6 fxg6 23. Rf6 Ne5 24. Rdf1 Bc6 25. Bg5 Qc7 26. Rxe6 Re8 27. Nd5 {1-0 (27) Naiditsch,A (2734)-Hoffmann,U (2255) Baden-Baden 2019}) (7... b6 8. Qd2 Ng6 9. O-O-O Be7 10. f4 O-O 11. g3 Qc7 12. h4 Rd8 13. h5 Nf8 14. g4 Na5 15. h6 Nxb3+ 16. axb3 Bc5 17. hxg7 Kxg7 18. f5 Bxe3 19. Qxe3 f6 20. Qh6+ Kf7 21. fxe6+ dxe6 22. Qxh7+ {1-0 (22) Romero Holmes,A (2518)-Corral Blanco,J (2436) Linares 2013}) (7... d6 8. a4 b6 9. Be2 Ng6 10. O-O Be7 11. f4 O-O 12. g3 Bb7 13. Bf3 Rc8 14. Qd2 Nb4 15. Qf2 Rc6 16. Rfd1 Qc7 17. Rd4 d5 18. exd5 exd5 19. Rd2 Bf6 20. Bd4 Rd8 21. Rad1 Ne7 22. Bxf6 Rxf6 23. Rd4 a5 24. R4d2 h6 25. Nb5 Qc8 26. c3 Na6 27. Re2 Ng6 28. h4 Qc4 29. N3d4 Nc5 30. h5 Nf8 31. Re7 Nfd7 32. Be2 Qxa4 33. Qe1 Qa2 34. Bg4 Nf8 35. b4 axb4 36. cxb4 Bc8 37. Bxc8 Rxc8 38. bxc5 bxc5 39. Nf3 Qb3 40. Qe2 d4 41. Kh2 Ra8 42. Rd2 Rb6 43. Rb2 Qa4 44. Qe4 Qa1 45. Re2 Ra2 46. Re8 Rxe2+ 47. Qxe2 Rb7 48. Nd6 Rb1 49. Rxf8+ {1-0 (49) Rudzinska,M (2222)-Toma,K (2289) Szklarska Poreba 2021}) (7... b5 8. Be2 Bb7 9. O-O Ng6 10. Nc5 Qc8 11. Nxb7 Qxb7 12. f4 Be7 13. Bf3 Nh4 14. e5 Nxf3+ 15. Qxf3 O-O 16. Ne4 f5 17. exf6 Bxf6 18. Rad1 d5 19. Nc5 Qe7 20. Nd3 Rad8 21. Kh1 Nd4 22. Qf2 Nf5 23. Bc5 Nd6 24. c3 Rc8 25. Rfe1 Bh4 26. Bxd6 Bxf2 27. Bxe7 Bxe1 28. Bxf8 Rxf8 29. Rxe1 Kf7 30. g3 Kf6 31. Kg2 Rb8 32. Kf3 a5 33. a3 a4 34. Nc5 Rc8 35. Nd3 h6 36. g4 g6 37. Re3 h5 38. g5+ Ke7 39. Ne5 Rc5 40. Nxg6+ Kd6 41. Nf8 Rc4 42. Rxe6+ Kc5 43. Nd7# {1-0 (43) Prraneeth,V (2253)-Lauridsen,J (2215) Chess.com INT 2021}) 8. Be2 (8. f4 b5 9. a3 (9. Qd2 Be7 10. O-O-O d6 11. Qf2 (11. Kb1 Bb7 12. Qf2 Qc7 13. Bb6 Qb8 14. h4 Nf8 15. Bxb5 axb5 16. Nxb5 Nd7 17. Bc7 Qc8 18. Nxd6+ Bxd6 19. Bxd6 Na5 20. Nxa5 Rxa5 21. Qd4 Nf6 22. Rhe1 Qa8 23. a3 Bxe4 24. Qb4 Bc6 25. g4 Rd5 26. g5 Ne4 27. Rxd5 Bxd5 28. c4 Bc6 29. Be5 Qb7 30. Bxg7 Rg8 31. Bc3 Qxb4 32. axb4 Kd7 33. b5 Bb7 34. Rd1+ Kc8 35. Be5 h5 36. gxh6 f6 37. Bd4 Kc7 38. f5 e5 39. Be3 Nd6 40. b6+ Kc6 41. Rd5 Nxf5 42. Rc5+ Kd6 43. Bf2 Nxh6 44. Rc7 Bc6 45. b4 Be4+ 46. Kc1 Nf5 47.