{[%evp 0,6,19,21,53,0,6,-33,9]} 1. e4 {This course is about the King's Gambit, historically one of the main weapons against 1...e5 . As Wikipedia informs us, 'The King's Gambit is one of the oldest documented openings, appearing in one of the earliest chess books, Luis Ramírez de Lucena's Repetición de Amores y Arte de Ajedrez (1497).'   A lot of water has flown under the bridge since it was really sharp and popular. These days, this weapon is gathering dust in the museum. You can occasionally meet it in faster time controls, but even there it gives the impression of rusty and unreliable junk. I've tried to harden, sharpen and polish it to a shine. In the days of countless Italian and Spanish games it is always tempting to find something not overused. As they say, everything new is merely the overlooked or forgotten old.} e5 2. f4 {Our first opening tabia. Most of the gambits are simple and forthright and the King's Gambit is not an exclusion. We hit the center. Unlike, say, the Queen's Gambit, the stakes increase, because our king is immediately under attack. Black has three main options. While working on the course, I found myself in an unusual situation. There is practically no modern theory! So I feel like a pioneer in a way. In this part I'll try to highlight the most frequent directions of the King's Gambit.} exf4 {The most obvious and the strongest reply. Whenever your opponent sacrifices you have to take the material! - an unwritten rule of the romantic era of chess.  2...d5  is the Falkbeer Countergambit. An excellent positional choice for those who prefer to sacrifice themselves or at least do not want to mess with gambits. 3.exd5  Compared to the other lines, the game is not too sharp here. Again Black has three paths. Each is is covered in the relevant section .   After 2...Bc5  ( Chapter 2 ) or 2...d6  ( Chapter 3 ), or 2...Nc6  ( Chapter 4 ) or even 2...c6  ( Chapter 1 ), and many other developing moves, we ignore the pawn and play as if nothing had happened.  White just develops pieces.    3.Nf3  Here's one of the tricks I'd like to show you: 3...Nc6 4.Nxe5 Nxe5 5.d4±  But jokes aside! There's a lot to learn from the respective chapters. The main continuation. White needs to be on the lookout and sometimes choose the lesser of evils.} (2... d5 {The Falkbeer Countergambit, which is normally played to deviate from all the sharp forced lines.} 3. exd5 e4 $6 {This is the old main line. The idea is to restrict White's knight from developing to f3 .} (3... c6 4. Ne2 {This is uncharted territory. An interesting way to develop the kingside while preparing the f4 - e5 capture.} cxd5 (4... exf4 5. Nxf4 cxd5 6. d4 {Now we can focus on the isolated pawn.} Nf6 7. Bb5+ Nc6 8. O-O Bg4 9. Be2 Bxe2 10. Nxe2 {White has obvious targets to attack and a few simple moves to develop an initiative. Chapter 5. 2...d5 - the Falkb Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) 5. fxe5 Qh4+ 6. Ng3 Nc6 7. c3 Nxe5 {7...d4  is just bad because of 8.Bb5 Bc5 9.d3 Nge7 10.Nd2⩲  with either Ne4 or Nf3 ideas.} 8. d4 {Alongside material parity, White has easy development and potential play against the black king and/or isolated pawn, as you will see in the corresponding chapter .} Ng4 9. Qf3 Bd6 (9... Be6 10. Bb5+ Kd8 11. Bf4 g5 12. Be5 Nxe5 13. dxe5 {White wants to castle (doesn't matter which side) and relies on piece activity. On the other hand, Black will struggle to put the king to safety.}) 10. Bf4 Qe7+ 11. Kd2 {With a good position. We shouldn't be worried about putting our King in the center as queens will get exchanged very quickly in this position.  Another important note is to not play 11.Be2?  because of 11...Bxf4 12.Qxf4 Ne3  and Black wins material.}) (3... exf4 4. Nf3 Nf6 {White has a wide choice here: 5.c4 , 5.Bc4 , 5.Be2 , 5.d4  and 5.Nc3 . I'd suggest to try} (4... Qxd5 {This also exists, but White is doing well after the text move.} 5. Nc3 {5.d4  is also possible.  5...Nf6 6.Bxf4 Qe4+ 7.Qe2 Qxe2+ 8.Bxe2 Nd5 9.Bd2⩲  1-0 Zvjaginsev, V. - Wang, H. Taiyuan 2007 .  5...Bd6 6.c4 Qe6+ 7.Be2 c6 8.Nc3 Nf6 9.O-O O-O 10.Bd3  is a bit unclear. But White has nothing to worry about. The idea is to develop via Qc2-Bd2-Rae1 .} Qd8 {Is just wasting time.} (5... Qe6+ 6. Be2 Bd6 7. O-O Ne7 8. d4 O-O 9. Ng5 Qh6 {A very clever move. Now White cannot play 10.Nge4  because of 10...g5!? .} 10. Nce4 Ng6 11. Bc4 Nc6 12. c3 Nd8 13. Qf3 {White regains the pawn and keeps the better chances.}) 6. d4 Bd6 7. Bc4 Nf6 8. Qe2+ {This is a very typical idea in the Falkbeer, to prevent Black from castling easily.} Qe7 9. Qxe7+ Kxe7 10. O-O {Once again, White is way ahead in development.}) (4... c6 {4...Bd6 5.d4 c6  transposes to the same line.} 5. d4 Bd6 6. Nc3 Ne7 {6...Nf6  transposes to 2...c6 chapter .} 7. dxc6 Nbxc6 8. Ne4 Bf5 (8... Bc7 9. c3 O-O 10. Bd3 {With good counterplay. The aim is to castle straight away and develop pieces via Qc2- Bd2 -Rae1 .}) 9. Bd3 Nb4 {Looks a bit challenging but after} 10. Nxd6+ Qxd6 11. Bxf5 Nxf5 12. Qe2+ Qe7 13. Qxe7+ Kxe7 14. Kd2 {White has dodged all tactical possibilities.} f6 15. c3 {Leads to another playable endgame. Both sides have good practical chances!}) (4... Bd6 5. Nc3 Ne7 6. d4 O-O 7. Bd3 Nd7 8. O-O h6 {Up to this point, both sides have played quite logically. And here Spassky spices up the position by sacrificing a pawn.} 9. Ne4 $1 Nxd5 10. c4 Ne3 11. Bxe3 fxe3 12. c5 Be7 13. Bc2 {White is going to build a battery on the b1 - h7 diagonal.} Re8 14. Qd3 e2 $2 {14...Nf8  Black desperately needed to cover the h7 square, but unfortunately Bronstein decided to buy himself some time...} 15. Nd6 $5 {Objectively this move does not win, but not everyone can decide to walk the black king to the center.} Nf8 $2 {15...exf1=Q+ 16.Rxf1 Bxd6 17.Qh7+ Kf8 18.cxd6 cxd6 19.Qh8+ Ke7 20.Re1+ Ne5 21.Qxg7 Rg8 22.Qxh6 Qb6 23.Kh1 Be6 24.dxe5 Qf2! 25.exd6+ Kxd6 26.Qf4+ Ke7  and White is forced to be content with a perpetual check.} 16. Nxf7 $3 {Now the attack is unstoppable.} exf1=Q+ 17. Rxf1 Bf5 {17...Qd5 18.Bb3 Qh5 19.Nxh6+ Kh8 20.Nf7+ Kg8 21.N7g5+ Kh8 22.Bf7+-} 18. Qxf5 Qd7 19. Qf4 Bf6 20. N3e5 Qe7 21. Bb3 Bxe5 22. Nxe5+ Kh7 23. Qe4+) 5. Bb5+ {Many famous players have tested this variation in the past. In particular, Nakamura, Zvjaginsev and Savchenko tried this move.} c6 {The main continuation.} (5... Bd7 {This is another continuation. White will have good chances to play for an advantage in this case.} 6. Bxd7+ Qxd7 7. O-O Qxd5 (7... Nxd5 8. c4 Nb6 9. d4 $1 Nxc4 10. Qd3 (10. Qe2+ {Is another try, but not as effective.} Qe6 11. Qd3 Ne3 $1 {Black needs to be precise. 11...Be7 12.Bxf4 Nb6 13.Bxc7  gives White a good position.} 12. Ng5 {Everything is now forced for both sides.} Qg6 13. Qb5+ c6 14. Qxb7 Qxg5 15. Bxe3 fxe3 16. Qxa8 Bd6 17. Nc3 Qh4 18. g3 Bxg3 19. hxg3 Qxg3+ 20. Kh1 {Leads to a perpetual.}) 10... Nd6 {Once again, a precise decision. 10...Nb6  isn't as effective because after 11.Ne5 Qe6 12.Nc3 Nc6 13.Nxc6 Qxc6 14.Bxf4 Be7 15.Rae1↑  Black will have practical problems.} 11. Bxf4 Be7 12. Nc3 O-O 13. Ne5 Qd8 14. Nd5 $1 Nd7 {14...Nc6  is also logical but after 15.Rae1 Nxe5 16.Rxe5  we will transpose to the main variation.} 15. Rae1 Nxe5 16. Rxe5 Re8 17. Rh5 g6 18. Rh3 {The calm retreat. We don't need to be super tactical here because Black has committed g6 and therefore has long-term problems pertaining to the dark squares.  A more direct approach here is 18.Be5  but, unfortunately, it ends with an equal position after 18...gxh5 19.Qg3+ Bg5 20.Nf6+ Qxf6 21.Rxf6 Ne4 22.Qf3 Bxf6 23.Bxf6 Nxf6 24.Qxf6 Re6 25.Qg5+= .} Qd7 19. Be5 {It must be scary to play this with Black, but somehow computer shows equality.}) 8. d4 Nc6 {8...Qf5  doesn't help as we can capture the pawn anyway via 9.Nh4 Qd7 10.Bxf4⩲ .} 9. Bxf4 O-O-O 10. c3 Nh5 {10...Bd6  is also possible but after 11.Bg5 Rhe8 12.Bxf6 gxf6 13.Nbd2 Bf4 14.Kh1⩲  White is slightly better thanks to Black's damaged pawn structure .} 11. Bd2 Be7 {11...Bd6 12.Qb3  doesn't change much .} 12. Qb3 Rhe8 13. Na3 {White has a slight pull. The idea should be to try and force Black to take ... Qxb3 which will guarantee us a better endgame.}) (5... Nbd7 {Is seen rarely in practice. White already has a good position.} 6. O-O Be7 7. Be2 {Black made a couple of passive moves, so we can step back with our bishop a little.} Nxd5 {And now we go for the same idea with} 8. c4 N5f6 9. d4 {Threatening to take the pawn and develop at the same time.} O-O 10. Nc3 Re8 11. Bxf4 Nf8 12. Bg3 {With a comfortable position. White will retain a small edge here because of space and central control, not to mention our superior coordination of pieces.}) 6. dxc6 Nxc6 (6... bxc6 $6 {A little bit strange move, which, nevertheless, is somewhat popular.} 7. Bc4 {White is doing quite good here.} Nd5 {Makes little sense.} (7... Bd6 {Seems like a logical move. But White needs to be precise here to get an advantage. Otherwise White is in huge trouble.} 8. Qe2+ $1 Qe7 9. Qxe7+ Kxe7 10. O-O Be6 11. Bxe6 fxe6 12. b3 {12.d4  was played between Illescas Cordoba vs Smyslov Seville 1987. The text move is an improvement over this game.} Nbd7 13. Na3 {With a fantastic position. Our Idea should be to go Nc4 - Bb2 - Rae1 and to put pressure on c6 ,e6 and f4 pawns.}) (7... Nbd7 8. O-O Nb6 9. Bb3 Be7 10. d4 {And we have an easy advantage. The next move is to take the pawn!}) 8. Nc3 {Simple developing move with a prophylactic purpose, we don't want to allow Black's bishop to develop to its ideal square on d6 anymore.} Be6 9. Qe2 {Once again, our idea is to not allow ...Bd6  and of course to put further pressure on the knight. 9.O-O??  loses the piece immediately via 9...Nxc3 .} Be7 10. Nd4 $1 {Wasting no time!} Nxc3 11. dxc3 Bxc4 12. Qxc4 {White seems to be almost winning here. But black has one resource:} Bh4+ {The point of all the exchanges. But we are fine here anyway thanks to a sneaky intermezzo.} 13. g3 $1 fxg3 14. Qe2+ $1 {The intermezzo. White is now clearly better.} Qe7 {If Black does not choose to exchange the queens and instead goes for 14...Kf8  we can simply play 15.O-O  and the kingside attack is too dangerous.} 15. Qxe7+ Bxe7 16. hxg3 {With a clearly better endgame. Our idea here is to, first, develop our pieces and O-O-O . Second, we want to put pressure on the c6 -pawn in the long run.}) (6... Qe7+ 7. Qe2 Qxe2+ 8. Kxe2 Nxc6 9. d4 {White has nothing at all against the exchange of queens.}) 7. Nc3 (7. d4 $6 Bd6 8. O-O O-O 9. c3 {This is objectively better for Black, but the position remains very complex.} h6 $1 {This is the best way to continue here.} 10. Nbd2 g5 11. Bd3 Re8 12. h3 Be6 13. Re1 Qd7 {And Black is clearly better.}) 7... Be6 $1 {Black prepares in advance for the check. An accurate reaction.  7...Bb4  is also possible but after 8.d4 O-O 9.O-O Bd6 10.Ne2 Nh5 11.c4⇆  we reach a similar position with an extra tempo as Black played Bb4-Bd6 .} (7... Bd6 {Is the most popular choice.} 8. Qe2+ $1 {White should try to prevent Black from castling too quickly.} Qe7 {8...Be6  transposes to the mainline .} 9. b3 O-O 10. Qxe7 Nxe7 11. Bb2 a6 12. Be2 b5 13. O-O-O Bb7 14. Rde1 {With an unclear game ahead. We should push a4 next to provoke b4 , followed by Nd1 - Nf2 ideas.}) (7... Qb6 $5 {Interesting choice. Black is in no hurry to decide on squares for bishops.} 8. a4 Be7 9. d4 {A double-edged position arises. We have another alternative here 9.a5 Qc7 10.O-O O-O 11.d4 Rd8 12.Ne2 Bd6 13.c3 a6 14.Bd3 h6 15.Qe1∞  with an unclear game ahead. We have attacking chances on the kingside starting with Qh4 .} O-O 10. O-O Bg4 11. Ne2 a6 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. h3 Bf5 {Probably the best response. Black should avoid simplifying the position too quickly. As we see in this variation: 13...Bxf3 14.Rxf3 c5 15.a5 Qb7 16.Bxf4  where White is quite comfortable .} 14. a5 Qb7 15. Nxf4 {We are a pawn up, but the position is far from clear. Black has a double bishop advantage and more active pieces at the moment, which is sufficient compensation for the pawn.   Our next few moves are pretty simple: Nd3 (to protect b2 ) followed by either Be3 or Bf4 . In the long run, we should aim to exchange pieces to simplify matters.}) 8. Qe2 $5 {White would be happy to castle queenside here and afterwards slowly build up in the center. Interesting idea. We are aiming to pose problems early on with Nd4 or Ng5 ideas. Black has plenty of decent moves here. I'll show some options to illuminate the alleged course of the game.} Bd6 (8... Bc5 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Ng5 Qe7 {Does not change our plans.} (10... O-O 11. Nxe6 fxe6 12. b3 Nd5 13. Bb2 Qh4+ 14. g3 $1 {Vital intermezzo, yet again!} Qf6 {14...fxg3  once again is not a good move because of 15.O-O-O Nf4 16.Qe1! Bf2 17.hxg3 Qxg3 18.Qe4↑  with tremendous attacking possibilities.} 15. O-O-O f3 16. Qc4 Bd4 17. Rhf1 f2 18. Kb1 {With a so-called dynamic equality.}) 11. Nxe6 Qxe6 12. d3 Rb8 13. Qxe6+ fxe6 14. Bxf4 Rxb2 15. Kd2 Bb4 16. Be5 {With a slightly better endgame because of Black's broken pawn structure.}) (8... Qb6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. b3 Bb4 {10...Bd6 11.Na4 Qb5 12.Bb2  brings a good position. As mentioned earlier, we should not be afraid to exchange queens in this variation because of Black's weak pawn structure.} 11. Na4 Qd8 12. Ng5 $5 O-O 13. Nxe6 Qd6 14. Bb2 Rae8 15. O-O-O Rxe6 16. Qf3 Rfe8 17. Rhf1 {With an equal position. We should remember to answer ...Re2 with Rf2 always and to not give our opponent the mighty 7th rank!}) (8... Bb4 {Is also interesting, but here we have an interesting response:} 9. a3 $5 Ba5 10. b4 $5 Bb6 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. Na4 O-O 13. Nxb6 Qxb6 14. Qf2 {Now White can easily castle. The plan would be to put pressure on the f4 and c6 -pawns in the future.}) (8... Be7 {This move is designed against Ng5 or Nd4 ideas.} 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. b3 {The queenside castle is one of the good ideas for White. The position remains unbalanced and the whole fight lies ahead.} O-O 11. Bb2 Re8 12. O-O-O Bd6 13. Qf2 Nd5 14. Rde1 {Once again, we reach dynamic equality. Our plan is to either try to exchange the queens (not on b6 , because it will connect Black's structure) and put pressure on the f4 and c6 -pawns or alternatively to generate a kingside attack.}) 9. Nd4 {The point behind Qe2 .} O-O (9... Qd7 10. Nxe6 fxe6 11. d4 {White's pawn structure is better, but the lack of development forces us to play carefully.} O-O 12. Bd2 Rae8 13. O-O-O {Another safe try is 13.Ne4 a6 14.Nxd6 Qxd6 15.Bxc6 Qxc6 16.Bxf4 Nd5 17.Be5 Rc8 18.c3 Nxc3 19.Qg4 Qe4+ 20.Qxe4 Nxe4 21.Ke2! Nf2 22.Rhc1 Ng4 23.Bg3=  with equality.} a6 14. Bxc6 Qxc6 15. Rhe1 b5 16. Ne4 Nxe4 17. Qxe4 Qxe4 18. Rxe4 {Gives us an equal, yet dynamic endgame. White should aim to put pressure on the e6 -pawn and of course like in all endgames, activate the king.}) 10. Nxe6 Qb6 $5 {Is a fun line.} (10... fxe6 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. b3 {White is planning to castle queenside.} Nd5 13. Bb2 Qh4+ 14. g3 $1 {This is how we get the required time to hide our king.} fxg3 $2 {This is a mistake as it allows us to castle with little consequences.} (14... Qh6 15. O-O-O f3 16. Qc4 {It's more comfortable to play with White here because Black has 3 weaknesses ( c6 , e6 , and f3 ) that can be exploited in the long run.}) 15. O-O-O Nf4 16. Qe1 {The attack is too dangerous. White is completely winning.}) 11. a4 Rae8 $4 {Black should be very careful when playing fun lines!} (11... Qa5 12. Bxc6 bxc6 13. Nxg7 $1 Rae8 {Of course not 13...Kxg7  as after 14.O-O  White is completely winning.} 14. Nxe8 Rxe8 15. Qxe8+ Nxe8 16. O-O {With good chances. We should aim to develop via d3 - Bxf4 .}) (11... fxe6 12. Qxe6+ Rf7 13. Qxd6 Re8+ 14. Be2 Rfe7 15. d3 Rxe2+ 16. Nxe2 Rxe2+ 17. Kd1 Rxg2 18. Qxf4 {With huge complications ahead.}) 12. a5 Nxa5 13. Na4 {Suddenly the queen is trapped!}) (3... Qxd5 4. Nc3 Qe6 5. fxe5 Qxe5+ 6. Be2 {White is already way ahead in development.} Bg4 {6...Nc6 7.Nf3  is clearly better for White.} 7. d4 {And the queen unfortunately has to move again. White simply has a huge development advantage in this position.}) 4. d3 $1 {Already gives White a solid advantage. The main chapter goes much further down the rabbit hole. Quickstarter Guide Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]} Nf6 {People normally choose this move in comparison with 4...Qxd5 , but the position is just sad for Black here.} (4... Qxd5 {The slightly lesser evil compared to 4...Nf6 .} 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Bd2 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 Ne7 {7...Nf6  is also possible, but after 8.Bxf6 gxf6 9.dxe4 Qxe4+ 10.Qe2+  White gets a clearly better endgame thanks to Black's weak doubled pawns.} 8. dxe4 Qxe4+ 9. Qe2 Qxf4 10. Bxg7 Rg8 11. Be5 {White is clearly better now.} Qa4 12. Bxc7 Nbc6 13. Nf3 Be6 (13... Bg4 14. Rd1 Qxa2 15. Qb5 Rc8 16. Bg3 Qe6+ 17. Kf2 a6 18. Qc4 {We are happy to enter an endgame. White will maintain a slight advantage regardless because of double bishops.}) 14. Qd2 Qe4+ 15. Kf2 {White consolidates its forces and keeps an advantage.}) 5. dxe4 Nxe4 6. Nf3 c6 (6... Bc5 7. Qe2 Bf5 {7...Qe7  can be met with 8.Be3  followed by Nbd2-O-O-O and White is clearly better thanks to the extra pawn.} 8. Nc3 Qe7 9. Be3 {We just want to exchange pieces as we are up in material.} Bb4 (9... Bxe3 10. Qxe3 Nxc3 11. Qxe7+ Kxe7 12. bxc3 Bxc2 {Black has recaptured the lost pawn, but the position still remains far from equal. However, White needs to be precise to achieve an advantage.} 13. Kd2 $1 Bf5 14. Re1+ Kf6 15. h3 h5 16. Ne5 {With a huge space and positional advantage. A simple developing move like 16...Nd7  can be very dangerous for Black because of 17.Bd3!}) (9... Nxc3 {Simplifying the position does not help Black.} 10. Bxc5 Qxe2+ 11. Bxe2 Nxd5 {Is a terrible move because White can now castle immediately and pose serious problems to Black's king.} (11... Nxe2 12. Kxe2 Na6 13. Ba3 Bxc2 14. Kd2 Be4 15. Ng5 {We should be careful to not confuse the move order with 15.Rhe1?!  as after 15...f5 16.Ng5 O-O-O  Black will get some breathing space.} Bxd5 {15...f5  does not work in this case because of 16.Nxe4 fxe4 17.Ke3  with a winning endgame.} 16. Rhe1+ Kd8 17. Re5 Bc4 18. b3 f6 19. Re7 fxg5 20. bxc4 gxf4 21. Rxg7 {With obvious winning chances thanks to complete domination on the 7th rank and the lack of coordination between Black's pieces.}) 12. O-O-O Nxf4 13. Rhe1 Nxe2+ 14. Rxe2+ Be6 15. Ng5 Nc6 16. Nxf7 $1 Kxf7 17. Rf1+ {And this is almost winning for White.}) 10. Bd4 Bxc3+ 11. Bxc3 Nxc3 12. Qxe7+ {Transposes to the 9... Bxe3 variation .}) 7. Bd3 {Development first.} cxd5 {Falls for a simple trick.} (7... Nf6 8. dxc6 Nxc6 9. Nc3 {Once again, Black has nothing for the pawn.}) 8. Bxe4 dxe4 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. Ng5 Ke8 11. Nxe4 {And White will enjoy an extra pawn in the endgame for no compensation.}) (2... Bc5 {One of the old classical moves and a really cheap trap. The Old Classical line. Black first develops the bishop to a better position, and only then plays d7 - d6 .} 3. Nf3 {ATTENTION! We NEVER want to blunder like this: 3.fxe5?? Qh4+-+ We are never in a hurry to capture the pawn on e5!  3.fxe5?? Qh4+-+ .} d6 (3... d5 {This is one more iteration of the Falkbeer Countergambit idea.} 4. Nc3 (4. exd5 {This is the calmer alternative.} e4 5. d4 $1 Bb4+ 6. Bd2 Bxd2+ 7. Nfxd2 Nf6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Be2 Re8 10. Nc4 {The position is roughly equal, but White can play for an advantage because his knight will reach the perfect blockading square e3 .} Nbd7 11. Ne3 {And this position is a little bit easier to play with White.}) 4... Nf6 (4... d4 5. Na4 {5.Ne2?!  may be interesting as well.} Be7 (5... Bd6 {This is more principled than 5...Be7 .} 6. fxe5 Bxe5 7. Bc4 {7.Nxe5  starts a long forced line which likely ends in a draw. 7...Qh4+ 8.g3 Qxe4+ 9.Qe2 Qxh1 10.b3 Nf6 11.Ng6+ Be6 12.Nxh8 Nbd7 13.Bb2 O-O-O 14.O-O-O Bg4 15.Bh3! Qxd1+ 16.Qxd1 Bxd1 17.Nxf7 Rf8 18.Ne5 Be2 19.Nc5 Bb5 20.a4 Re8 21.Bxd4 b6 22.axb5 bxc5 23.Bc3 Kd8 24.Nc6+ Kc8= .} Nc6 {7...Bg4  will never work because of the typical trick 8.Bxf7+ .} 8. Bb5 {White has many tempting continuations, but this move defines the structure and promises a lasting advantage. 8.b4!? , 8.O-O , and 8.d3  are all interesting too.} Bg4 {Black maintains his bishop on e5 by pinning the f3 -knight.} 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. d3 Ne7 11. O-O Qd6 (11... O-O 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 f5 {An attempt to create tension in the center while the white knight is out of play.} 14. Bg5 h6 15. Bxe7 Qxe7 16. exf5 {White is a pawn up and has by far the better pawn structure, but his dark squares are exposed and Black can cook up some dynamic play.} Rae8 17. Qxc6 Qg5 18. Qf3 Bd6 19. Nc5 $1 Bxc5 20. Qd5+ Kh7 21. Qxc5 Re2 22. Qd5 Rxc2 23. Rae1 {Black didn't equalize completely.}) 12. h3 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 O-O 14. Bf4 {This structure is really favorable for White. Black needed to play more dynamically so that the a4 -knight would be more problematic for us.}) 6. Nxe5 Nf6 {Now the e4 -pawn is weak and the knight on a4 is poorly placed.} 7. b3 {White doesn't protect the e4 -pawn and plays for development instead.} Nxe4 8. Qf3 Nf6 9. Bb2 O-O 10. O-O-O {The bishop can be developed to d3 or c4 and White has natural play: just throw the g- and h-pawns forward! Sometimes, we might even abandon the a4 -knight altogether.}) 5. Qe2 $1 {An important move! White can't lose the battle for the e4 -square.} d4 (5... exf4 6. d4 Be7 7. Nxd5 Nxd5 8. exd5 O-O {Black loses too much time in case of 8...Qxd5 9.Bxf4 .} 9. Bxf4 {A typical structure favorable for White arises.} Bb4+ 10. Kf2 {White is doing perfectly fine. His position is very harmonious after c2 - c3 followed by Qd2 , Bd3 and Rhe1 / Rhf1 and possibly Kg1 .  With the half-open f-file, there is always some potential for a kingside attack.}) (5... dxe4 {5...Nxe4 6.Nxe4 dxe4 7.Qxe4  transposes.} 6. Nxe4 Nxe4 7. Qxe4 Nc6 8. fxe5 {Now we win the pawn and it's not obvious if Black can find any decent compensation. The most important thing is to hold the center.} Nd4 {Forced, Black couldn't allow c3 + d4 .} 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Bd3 $1 {The check on f2 is really not a problem. 10.Qxd4 Bxd4  is just a draw.} Bd7 11. Rf1 Bc6 12. Qe2 {White needs to be precise, it's not so easy to complete the development.} O-O-O 13. Bf5+ Kb8 14. c3 Qd5 15. d4 Rhe8 16. Rf4 {With the idea of Be4 ! White's pawn chain is just perfect. Black's initiative will diminish with every exchange.}) 6. fxe5 dxc3 7. exf6 cxd2+ {Black must speed up our development in order not to lose a pawn.} 8. Bxd2 Qxf6 9. O-O-O {With excellent development and comfortable play. Most importantly, the bishop will come to c3 , attacking the queen and the g7 -pawn. White will win lots of tempi on the queen with moves like h2 - h4 - h5 . Chapter 2-2...Bc5 Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) (3... Nc6 $6 {This is a common inaccuracy we're always ready to punish with} 4. Nxe5 Nf6 {4...Nxe5 5.d4±  Typical trick! White has a solid pawn center and excellent development.} (4... Nxe5 5. d4 {A typical trick! White has a very strong center.}) 5. d3 O-O 6. Be2 Re8 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. Nc3 {We are left with a healthy extra pawn.}) 4. c3 Nc6 {Other move leads to pleasant and very playable endgames. White now has a pleasant choice between 5.b4  and 5.d4 .} (4... Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb6 {If 6...Bb4+  we just keep developing 7.Bd2  and with that massive center we can look confidently into the future.} (6... Bb4+ $6 7. Bd2 {Now we can protect the e4 -pawn by tactical means.} Bxd2+ 8. Nbxd2 O-O 9. Bd3 c5 10. d5 {White was already much better in 1-0 (47) Savchenko,B (2562) -Kobalia,M (2620) Sochi 2018 , but there is one trick you need to be aware of.} Qe7 11. Qe2 $1 {11.O-O ? Nxd5! 12.exd5 Qe3+-+} Re8 12. O-O-O {Black probably needs to agree to a slightly worse endgame now.} Na6 (12... Nxd5 13. exd5 Qxe2 14. Bxe2 Rxe2 15. Rhe1 Rxe1 16. Rxe1 {And the mate threat ( Re8 ) allows White to win the d6 -pawn.} Bd7 17. Nc4 Na6 18. Re7 Bg4 19. Nxd6 {White's central pawns are strong and more importantly, the d6 -knight and the e7 -rook are extremely well placed. Black is unlikely to defend this position.}) 13. Rhe1 Nb4 {If 13...c4  then 14.Nxc4 Nb4 15.e5±  and White is crushing through.} 14. a3 {We don't need to worship this bishop too much.} Nxd3+ 15. Qxd3 {Now e4 - e5 is inevitable.}) 7. Nc3 {White has a great pawn center, easy development, and a good choice of different plans.} O-O {8...Nc6 8.e5 dxe5 9.fxe5 O-O  is another possible move order.} (7... Nc6 8. e5 Nd7 9. Qe2 $1 {Preparing Be3 and O-O-O .  9.Be3?  would be a mistake in view of dxe5 10.fxe5 Ndxe5∓} dxe5 10. dxe5 Nc5 {Black relies on his activity.} 11. Be3 Bf5 {The threat of ... Nd3 + forces us to give up the bishop pair.} 12. Bxc5 Bxc5 13. Rd1 Qe7 14. a3 {Preventing Black's only counterplay. 14.Nd5  allows 14...Bb4+ .} Be6 (14... Qe6 15. Nd5 O-O-O 16. Qc4 Bb6 17. Nxb6+ axb6 18. Qxe6+ Bxe6 19. Rd3 {White is advancing on the kingside, he has a clear advantage.}) 15. f5 Bb3 16. Rd2 {The bishop on b3 is going to be attacked quite soon and it doesn't have any retreat squares.} O-O 17. Qb5 Nxe5 18. Nxe5 Qxe5+ 19. Re2 Qxf5 20. Qxb3 {The position is not so clear yet. Black has two pawns for the pieces and some play against our king in the center. Once we finish our development with g3 and Bg2 , or by trading rooks on e8 followed by Be2 , it should be close to winning.}) 8. e5 {Direct play looks good.} dxe5 (8... Re8 {8...dxe5 9.fxe5 Re8 10.Be2  transposes.} 9. Be2 dxe5 10. fxe5 Nd5 11. O-O Nc6 12. Kh1) (8... Ng4 {Black can keep the tension in the center too, so that his knight can go to ... h6 after h3 .} 9. h3 {And White doesn't have Bxh6 now.} Nh6 10. Be3 Nf5 11. Qd2 {As long as we can hold the d4 -pawn we shouldn't worry in this position.} dxe5 12. fxe5 Nc6 13. O-O-O Nxe3 14. Qxe3 {White has stabilized in the center and has a pleasant play.}) 9. fxe5 Nd5 (9... Nc6 $5 {This is probably the best way to play as Black.} 10. exf6 Bg4 11. Be2 Re8 12. Kf1 {Complex lines arise, where White is doing well:} Qxf6 13. Nd5 {It's important to eliminate the dark-squared bishop.} Qg6 14. Nxb6 Rad8 15. d5 $1 axb6 16. Qd2 {Defending from Be6 and Nb4 and keeping in mind Qg5 idea. Black still has some counterplay, but frankly speaking, it should never be enough for a piece.}) 10. Bc4 Be6 11. Qb3 c6 {11...Nxc3 12.bxc3+-  simply gives White a really strong center.} 12. O-O {White enjoys a very dominating position. Kh1 is a useful move to avoid any tactics on the b6 - g1 diagonal. Later Ne4 followed by Bg5 or Nfg5 are strong moves to start a direct attack against the black king.}) (4... Bb6 {This seems like a very natural move to me, anticipating d2 - d4 .} 5. fxe5 dxe5 6. Na3 $5 Nf6 7. Nc4 {Trading away a strong bishop.}) (4... Bg4 $6 {The second most popular move. Here White has an advantage of two bishops and an easy game, but Black is unable to join the battle for the center.} 5. h3 Bxf3 6. Qxf3 Nd7 7. d3 Ne7 {Black prepares ... f7 - f5 .} 8. Be2 O-O 9. f5 {We don't allow Black to play ... f7 - f5 and start a kingside attack ourselves! Our center is very strong and g2 - g4 - g5 and h2 - h4 - h5 are on the cards.}) (4... Qe7 $6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Qxe4+ $2 7. Kf2 {With a completely winning position. White aims to develop via Bb5 + and immediately threaten Re1 .}) (4... f5 $5 {This interesting move leads to a rather complex play.} 5. d4 $5 (5. exf5 Bxf5 6. fxe5 dxe5 7. d4 exd4 8. cxd4 Bb6 {Black is required to be precise here.} 9. Bg5 {9.Bc4 Nc6 10.O-O Qd6 11.Nc3 O-O-O 12.Nd5 Bg4 13.Bf4 Bxf3 14.Qxf3 Nxd4 15.Qg4+ Qd7 16.Nxb6+ axb6 17.Qxd7+ Rxd7 18.Bxc7 Kxc7 19.Rf8 Nc6  with a probable draw.} Ne7 10. Bb5+ Nbc6 11. Qe2 Qd6 12. Bxc6+ bxc6 13. a4 Ba5+ 14. Nbd2 Qe6 {Black is close to equality.}) 5... exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ (6... Bb6 {With this move Black accepts to play a worse position.} 7. e5 dxe5 8. fxe5 Be6 {Black tries to set up a blockade on the light squares.} 9. Nc3 Ne7 10. Bg5 {White fights for the control over the d5 -square by pinning the e7 -knight, or by simply capturing it later on.} Nbc6 11. Bb5 h6 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Qa4 O-O-O {Preventing d4 - d5 .} 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Qxc6 {White has an extra pawn for now and Black's king may very well be more insecure than White's.}) (6... fxe4 7. dxc5 exf3 8. Qxf3 Nf6 {White has the two bishops and he is a pawn up, but he has only developed his queen now.} 9. Nc3 Qe7+ {This allows White to stabilize his position.} (9... d5 $1 {The c5 -pawn becomes vulnerable and the light squares are weak. White needs to fight for equality.} 10. Bd3 O-O 11. O-O b6 12. f5 {Preparing Bg5 .} Nc6 {Black is constantly creating small threats.} 13. Qf2 {13.Bg5 Ne5 14.Qg3 Nxd3 15.Qxd3 c6=} Nb4 14. Be2 {The position is unclear, each side has its own advantages. Here's one of the options:} d4 15. a3 Nc6 16. Nb5 a6 17. Bf3 axb5 18. Bxc6 Rb8 19. Bxb5 bxc5 20. Bc4+ Kh8 21. a4 {White has the two bishops and potentially his a-pawn could be strong, but Black has a strong center and his pieces are slightly better placed. This is quite a playable line for Black, which is surprising in my opinion!}) 10. Be2 dxc5 11. O-O Nc6 12. Qf2 $1 {Regrouping pieces.} Bd7 13. Be3 b6 14. Rae1 O-O 15. Bc4+ Kh8 16. Bd4 Qd8 17. Be5 {White has a very nice initiative for the pawn and he is surely better here. Black's king lacks defenders.}) 7. Nbd2 Bxd2+ (7... fxe4 {A long forced line begins here.} 8. Qa4+ Bd7 (8... Nc6 9. d5 exf3 (9... b5 $5 {This is the only move for Black to keep the balance.} 10. Bxb5 exf3 11. gxf3 Kf8 12. Bxc6 Rb8 13. O-O Bh3 14. Ne4 Bxf1 15. a3 {15.Kxf1 Qh4∞} Bd3 16. axb4 a6 17. Qb3 Bxe4 18. fxe4 {It's White who sets the pace for the game.}) 10. dxc6 Bxd2+ 11. Bxd2 b5 12. Qe4+ Qe7 13. gxf3 {The bishop pair is very strong in this position. b5 is also hanging and g7 will definitely be a target too.}) 9. Qxb4 exf3 10. Nxf3 Nf6 {10....Bc6 11.Bd3⩲   10...Nc6 11.Qb3⩲} 11. Bd3) 8. Nxd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 {The bishop pair defines White's advantage.}) 5. b4 $5 (5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ (6... Bb6 7. Nc3 f5 {This is not going to solve Black's problem in this line, but at least gives a fresh direction to research.} 8. e5 dxe5 9. dxe5 Qxd1+ 10. Kxd1 {White retains a long-term advantage.} Be6 11. Ng5 Rd8+ (11... O-O-O+ 12. Ke1 Nd4 13. Nxe6 Nxe6 14. Bc4 Nd4 15. Rb1 {White gradually finishes development while keeping all of the trumps in the position.}) 12. Ke1 Bc8 13. Bc4 Nge7 14. Bd2 h6 15. Nf3) 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. Bd3 O-O 9. O-O Bg4 10. Be3 {White has reached the perfect alignment of pieces.}) 5... Bb6 6. a4 {White threatens a5 immediately. This line is further covered in the 2...Bc5 chapter.} a5 {This doesn't work; White will win a pawn.} (6... a6 7. fxe5 dxe5 8. Bc4 {You may also include 8.a5 Ba7  if you want, and then play 9.Bc4 . It shouldn't make too much of a difference.} Nf6 9. d3 {The position is very similar to those arising from the Italian Game/Giuoco Piano.} O-O 10. Qe2 {Preparing Be3 . White enjoys some space advantage on the queenside which is said to be very important in the Italian game.} Qd6 11. a5 Ba7 12. Be3 Bxe3 13. Qxe3 Be6 14. Nbd2 {This looks like a promising version of the Italian game. Black will have to work a while to completely equalize the game. For example,} Rad8 15. Rd1 Nh5 $5 16. O-O Nf4 17. Bxe6 Qxe6 18. d4 exd4 {You should avoid 19.Qxf4 dxc3  when White is suddenly under pressure.} 19. cxd4 Qh6 20. Kh1 f5 21. Qb3+ Kh8 22. exf5 Nxd4 23. Nxd4 Rxd4 24. Nf3 Rxd1 25. Rxd1 {With an equal position. White's idea is to push g3 and kick Black's active knight.}) 7. b5 Nce7 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Nxe5 Nf6 {Of course, White's advantage is not set in stone yet because he is behind in development. However, Black can't create any direct threats.} 10. d3 Be6 11. Nd2 O-O 12. Ndc4 {Gaining an important tempo on the dark-squared bishop.} c6 13. bxc6 bxc6 14. Be2 {Black has incomplete compensation for the pawn.}) (2... Nc6 {Another sideline. A fine developing move, which, however, prevents Black from capturing on f4 and therefore is not dangerous at all.} 3. Nf3 f5 {A fierce counterattack. Nakamura played this against Ivanchuk.} (3... exf4 {Not the best moment to capture on f4 .  3...d6  will probably just transpose to the 2...d6 chapter .} 4. d4 {Tranposes to the 2... Nc6 Chapter .} d5 (4... Nf6 5. Qe2 $1 {and it's hard to properly defend on f4 . For example,} g5 6. Nxg5 (6. e5 Nd5 7. Nc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 9. bxc3 g4 10. Qe4 gxf3 11. Qxd5 {With a double-edged position where we retain better chances of attack thanks to our active queen.}) 6... Nxd4 7. Qc4 Ne6 8. Nxe6 dxe6 9. Bxf4 {White has a slight advantage. The idea is to play Nd2-O-O-O . Although we should be careful to answer 9...Ng4  with 10.Be2! .}) (4... g5 {This looks strange with the knight on c6 .} 5. d5 g4 6. dxc6 gxf3 7. Qxf3 Qh4+ {Otherwise Black is in trouble: White has the better pawn structure and lead in development.} 8. g3 fxg3 9. hxg3 Qf6 {White doesn't have to trade queens straight away:} 10. Qb3 dxc6 (10... Qg6 11. cxb7 Qxe4+ 12. Be3 Bxb7 (12... Qxb7 13. Rh4 Bd6 14. Bf4 Bxf4 15. Rxf4 Qxb3 16. axb3 Bb7 17. Nc3 {White wins material back and starts attacking all the pawn weaknesses.}) 13. Rh4 Qf3 14. Bd4 Qxb3 15. axb3 f6 16. Nc3 {White is a pawn down, but he will easily win it back because of his lead in development - and perhaps pick up an extra pawn too!}) 11. Bf4 Bh6 12. Nd2 Bxf4 13. gxf4 Qxf4 14. O-O-O Bg4 15. Ba6 $1 {Now White is winning.} Bxd1 16. Rf1 Qh6 17. Qxf7+ Kd8 18. Bxb7 {Qf8+ will win a full rook, for example: 18...Be2 19.Qf8+ Qxf8 20.Rxf8+ Ke7 21.Rxa8  and White is close to winning because of the pressure on the back rank.}) 5. exd5 Qxd5 6. Nc3 Bb4 7. Bd3 Qe6+ {This is more or less the same, we need to move our king to f2 , but Black spent time on ... Qe6 + too.} (7... Nf6 8. O-O Qd8 9. Ne2 {We get the dream edition of the King's Gambit.}) 8. Kf2 {Followed by Re1-Bf4 , Black should also be careful of Nb5 ideas. We retain a slightly better position. Chapter 4-2...Nc6 Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) (3... d5 {Not as bad as it might seem.} 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. Nc3 Qa5 $2 {This is not the Scandinavian!} (5... Qe6 6. Be2 Bc5 {Black tries to hinder our development. This could work if it weren't for Nxe5 !} 7. Nxe5 $1 Nxe5 8. d4 {A good old trick!} Nf3+ $5 {Black needs to complicate the matters.} 9. gxf3 Be7 (9... Bb6 {Now including a4 -... a5 favors White, because the b5 -outpost could be used by the knight or in some cases the bishop.} 10. a4 a5 {favors White, because the b5 -outpost could be used to the knight or in some cases the bishop.} 11. Qd3 Ne7 12. Be3 Bd7 13. O-O-O {The white king is very safe on c1 .} O-O-O 14. Rhe1 Bc6 {Black obviously has some compensation, but the advantage of an extra doubled pawn is that it's not necessary to protect it.} 15. Bg1 Qd6 16. Ne4 Qb4 17. Ng5 Be8 18. Qc4 {White has a slight lead in development and can target the f7 -pawn or e6 -square after ... f6 or ... f5 .}) 10. O-O Bd7 {Black tries to castle queenside and get to our king on g1 .} 11. d5 Qb6+ 12. Kh1 O-O-O 13. Ne4 Nh6 14. a4 a5 15. b4 $5 {One of the tempting ways to develop initiative.} Bxb4 16. Rb1 {Our king is more or less safe at the moment. Meanwhile, White developed a nice initiative on the queenside. c2 - c3 is the first of many threats black needs to deal with.}) 6. Bb5 Bd7 (6... Bg4 7. Bxc6+ bxc6 8. fxe5 {White is a pawn up.}) 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 8. Nxe5 {Leaves White a pawn up. 8...Bxg2  doesn't work because of 9.Qg4 .}) 4. exf5 $5 {I'd suggest to choose this move, which enforces Black to play precisely.  4.d3  is also very nice and simple direction which probably gives White better chances. d6 5.Nc3 Nf6 6.g3 g6 7.Bg2 Bg7 8.fxe5 dxe5∞  as in 1-0 Ivanchuk, V. - Nakamura, H. Cap d'Agde 2010 .} e4 (4... exf4 $6 {This move indicates a complete misunderstanding of the opening ideas.} 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 Bd6 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. Qe2+ {White will get a favorable endgame now.} Qe7 9. Qxe7+ Nxe7 10. Nh4 {The f4 -pawn is more vulnerable than the f5 -pawn.} f3 11. g3 {White is close to winning after O-O and Rxf3 .}) 5. Ne5 Nf6 (5... Nxe5 $2 6. fxe5 Qe7 7. Qh5+ Kd8 8. d4 exd3 9. Qg5 {With such a space advantage the endgame will be nearly winning for White.  9.Bxd3 Qxe5+ 10.Kd1 Nf6⩲  was 0-1 Fier,A - Hammer, J. ICC INT 2009 .} dxc2 {This is never a good idea; we didn't care about this pawn anyway.} (9... Nf6 10. Qf4 Nd5 11. Qd4 Nb4 {We shouldn't be too scared with this jumping horsey.} 12. Bxd3 Nc6 13. Qe4 Nxe5 14. O-O {White is close to winning because of Black's king on d8 and his immense lag in development.}) 10. Nc3 Nf6 11. Qf4 d6 12. e6 g6 13. Bc4 c6 14. O-O {White's extra space, lead in development, and safer king's position more than compensate for the missing pawn.}) 6. d4 {It's always useful to grab space in the opening.} (6. Nc3 {This is a good try, but it allows the opponent to be brilliant!} Bc5 $1 7. Bc4 d6 $1 8. Nf7 Qe7 9. Nxh8 Bxf5 {A nice counter gambit. Black is OK here to say the least.} 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. Bxd5 Nb4 12. Bb3 Nd3+ 13. cxd3 exd3+ 14. Kf1 O-O-O 15. Qe1 Qd7) 6... d6 $5 {A calm alternative. Black accepts a slightly worse position.} (6... exd3 7. Bxd3 Bc5 {Now it's hard to castle, but the white king is comfortable in the center.} 8. Nc3 O-O (8... Qe7 {Provokes a fancy move:} 9. Kd2 $5 {An extremely odd-looking move, blocking in the c1 -bishop with your own king. However, White sidesteps Black's play on the e-file (he was threatening ... d6 ) and is in time with Re1 .} d6 (9... O-O 10. Re1 Bf2 11. Re2 Qc5 12. Nxc6 dxc6 (12... bxc6 13. Qf1 {White unwinds a ball of pieces: Nd1 , c3 , b4 and Nb2 - c4 , nearly all of these moves come with tempo!} Bd4 14. Nd1 Qb6 15. c3 Bc5 16. b4 Bd6 17. Nb2 {17.Ne3 Ba6 18.Nc4  is the same.} Ba6 18. Nc4 Bxc4 19. Bxc4+ Kh8 20. Kc2 {The white king is surrounded by its pieces.} a5 21. Be3 Qb7 22. a3 {White has an extra pawn and the bishop pair. Of course, there's much left to play for, but White should be confident in his chances.}) 13. Re5 {In this pawn structure, we are always willing to sacrifice the exchange in favor of quick development.} Qb4 14. Qf3 Bd4 15. a3 Qd6 16. Ne4 Nxe4+ (16... Qd8 17. Ng3 {We're still inviting Black to grab the exchange and repair our pawn structure. If Black makes any neutral move, c2 - c3 , Kc2 and Bd2 / e3 followed by Rd1 are on the agenda.}) 17. Qxe4 {This is close to winning for White. g2 - g4 is next, the pawns control many important squares and prevent Black from developing his pieces to active squares.}) 10. Ng4 {Maintains some advantage, because White can hang onto the f5 -pawn.}) 9. Qf3 {White can't castle, but is this a real problem if you play the King's Gambit?} Qe8 (9... Re8 10. Ne2 Nxe5 {Black can try this, but it won't help.} (10... Nb4 {Eliminating our dark-squared bishop.} 11. Bd2 Nxd3+ 12. Nxd3 d6 13. O-O-O Bxf5 14. Nxc5 dxc5 15. Bc3 {The bishop is very well placed on the long diagonal.} Qe7 16. Rhe1 (16. Bxf6 $1 {A deep move, trading one advantage for another.} Qxf6 (16... Qxe2 17. Qd5+ Qe6 18. Be5 {The bishop is here to stay.} c6 19. Qxe6+ Rxe6 20. Rhg1 {White is better because the bishop is extremely strong on e5 , although it is impossible to deny obvious drawish tendencies.}) 17. Nc3 {The knight is well placed on c3 too! And now we can restrict Black's bishop.} c6 18. g4 Bg6 19. f5 {White's pieces are slightly more active and he has an obvious attacking plan: Qf4 , h4 and g5 .}) 16... Qe4 17. Qxe4 Nxe4 18. Be5 Re7 19. Ng1 Nd6) 11. fxe5 Rxe5 12. Bf4 {Followed by g2 - g4 - g5 simply wins for White.}) (9... d6 10. Nxc6 Qe8+ 11. Kf1 {This time, the king makes room for the other rook!} Qxc6 12. Qxc6 bxc6 13. h3 h5 14. g3 {A pawn is a pawn. This position is much easier to play than the complications arising after 14.g4!? .} (14. g4 $5 hxg4 15. hxg4 Nxg4 16. Bc4+ Rf7 {16...d5?  gives White another pawn for no reason. 17.Nxd5+-} 17. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {Black has some compensation for an exchange.})) 10. Ne4 Nxe5 {This is just unpleasant for Black:} (10... Bd4 11. Kd1 $1 Bxe5 (11... Nxe4 12. Bxe4 Bxe5 13. Re1 {Transposes to 11...Bxe5 .} d6 (13... Kh8 14. Bxc6 dxc6 15. Rxe5 Qd8+ 16. Bd2 Bxf5 17. Kc1 {White has a nice and simple plan here, and Black should play carefully not to get into even bigger trouble.}) 14. g4 {Our idea is to play h3- c3 -Kc2 and of course we should take the bishop when we are forced to.}) 12. Re1 $1 {After a few accurate moves White will retain a good advantage.} d6 13. Nxf6+ Rxf6 14. g4 {Black's light-squared bishop is severely restricted and we can win back the dark-squared bishop at any desirable moment. We may reach another position with opposite-colored bishops after 14...Qf8 15.fxe5 Nxe5 16.Qg3  followed by ... Nxd3 at some point. Such middlegames, with opposite-colored bishops and the safer king, are very pleasant to play and allow you to slowly build up a strong attack.}) 11. Nxf6+ $1 Kh8 (11... Rxf6 12. fxe5 Qxe5+ 13. Kd1 {Following up with Re1 , Bg5 , and g2 - g4 gives White an excellent initiative.}) (11... gxf6 12. fxe5 Qxe5+ 13. Kd1 {The f5 -pawn restricts Black's play considerably. White's king is stuck in the center, but it's much safer on d1 .}) 12. fxe5 Qxe5+ 13. Kd1 Qxf6 {Now that Black's queen is safe on f6 , he won't lose anymore time.} 14. Rf1 {The situation has stabilized. White now starts rolling the g-pawn forward.} d5 15. g4 {The position remains complex, but White has natural play: c3 , Kc2 followed by g4 - g5 , Bf4 and Rae1 . So White's attacking chances are obvious, while it's much more difficult for Black to find harmony.}) 7. Bb5 $1 dxe5 8. fxe5 Bxf5 {Now we should equalize the material and finish development.} 9. exf6 Qxf6 10. O-O Bd6 {Thanks to this move Black can complete development without adventures.} (10... O-O-O $2 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. g4 {Black has no compensation for the piece whatsoever.}) (10... Qg6 11. Bf4 O-O-O 12. Bxc6 Qxc6 13. c3 {White has a really solid center and the e4 -pawn may become weak. White should have a long-term advantage.}) 11. Nc3 O-O 12. Bc4+ $1 Kh8 13. Nb5 {With two bishops White will enjoy a small advantage in a calm position. We are happy to eliminate the dangerous bishop.} Qg6 14. Be3 Rad8 15. Nxd6 cxd6 16. Qd2 d5 17. Bb3 {White enjoys a small but long-term advantage.}) (2... d6 3. Nc3 {3.Nf3 exf4  would transpose to arguably the best line at Black's disposal, so it is reasonable to dodge that bullet.   On the other hand, 3.d3  is a good way to flee from the field of theoretical battle.} (3. d4 $5 {Is another interesting alternative.} exd4 {3...Nf6 4.Nc3 exd4 5.Qxd4 Nc6 6.Qe3  will transpose into the main line.} 4. Qxd4 Nc6 5. Qe3 {Looks like the Center Game. With f4 &amp; d6 included it seems to be playable:} Nf6 {5...g6  is also possible, but after 6.Nc3 Bg7 7.Bd2 Nf6 8.O-O-O O-O 9.Nf3 Re8 10.Bc4  threatening ideas like Ng5 . 10...Na5 11.Bd3 c6 12.Qe1⩲  White has a very comfortable position.} 6. Nc3 Be7 7. Bc4 Be6 {Black can also try 7...O-O  But White has a comfortable position after 8.Nf3 a6 9.a3 b5 10.Ba2 Be6 11.Bxe6 fxe6 12.O-O  ⩲ .} 8. Bxe6 fxe6 9. Nf3 d5 10. Qe2 Bb4 11. Bd2 Bxc3 12. Bxc3 Nxe4 13. Bxg7 Rg8 14. Bc3 {White seems to be fine here. Note that Black will probably take on c3 after playing ... Qe7 or ... Qd7 , so we should castle on the short side.}) 3... exf4 (3... Nc6 4. Nf3 {When the knight is already on c6 , we will always have d4 - d5 push.} Nd4 {Does not make too much sense.} (4... Nf6 {The most popular move according to the database.} 5. Bb5 exf4 (5... Bd7 6. d3 exf4 7. Bxf4 {Just another dream position.}) 6. d4 a6 7. Be2 Nh5 {7...d5  Black lost some time on d7-d6-d5 , so White is just better now. 8.exd5 Nxd5 9.Nxd5 Qxd5 10.O-O⩲  with ideas like c3 or c4 followed by Bxf4 .} 8. O-O g6 9. Ne1 Bg7 10. d5 $1 Ne5 11. Bxh5 gxh5 12. Bxf4 Bg4 13. Qc1 {White trades away active pieces and starts collecting weak pawns. We avoid Qd2 to stay away from Nc4 ideas.}) (4... g6 $6 {This is too slow and too passive.} 5. fxe5 dxe5 {5...Nxe5  does not offer much hope either as after 6.d4 Nxf3+ 7.Qxf3 Bg7 8.Be3±  White is clearly better.} 6. Bb5 {With a big advantage. White's idea is to take on c6 and play against the weak doubled pawns.}) (4... Be7 {Is also quite harmless.} 5. fxe5 Nxe5 6. d4 Ng6 7. Bd3 {White is better thanks to their active pieces and central domination.}) (4... Bg4 5. Bb5 {White quickly gets a comfortable advantage.} Nge7 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 a6 8. Ba4 b5 9. Bb3 Nd4 10. Qf2 Nxb3 11. axb3 {With a slight advantage because of Black's weak pawn on a6 combined with our kingside play.}) (4... exf4 5. d4 g5 {Usually Black is happy to get this setup, but here it seems White has a direct solution: Possible move, but 5...g4!  is stronger: 6.Bxf4 gxf3 7.Qxf3 Nc6 8.Bb5  White has insufficient compensation. The initiative has yet to be proven.} 6. d5 (6. g3 g4 {A bold decision. Black goes for a fairly stereotypical structure, in which White is clearly ahead in development and Black needs to play very accurately in order to get into a bad position.  As you might have learnt from the course, the correct move is  6...Bg7 7.d5 Ne5 8.gxf4 gxf4 9.Bxf4 Bg4 10.Be2 Bxf3 11.Bxf3 Qf6 12.Bxe5 Qxe5⩱} 7. Nh4 f3 8. Be3 Nge7 $6 {The beginning of Black's troubles.  8...Nf6 9.Bd3 Be7 10.a3 Nh5 11.Nxf3 gxf3 12.Qxf3 Ng7 13.O-O-O Bg5 14.Kb1 O-O 15.Nd5  Compensation is obvious, but Black is still able to fight back.} 9. h3 Bg7 10. Qd2 d5 $6 {Black opens the center without decent development.} 11. O-O-O dxe4 12. d5 $1 Ne5 13. Nxe4 {White has reached a dream position.} f5 14. Bb5+ Bd7 {14...Kf8 15.d6 fxe4 16.dxe7+ Qxe7 17.Bg5 Bf6 18.Bxf6 Qxf6 19.Rhe1  could have prolonged the resistance, but the result of the game would hardly have changed.} 15. Bxd7+ Nxd7 16. Ng5 Nf8 17. Bc5 Bf6 18. Rhe1 f2 19. Bxf2 Rg8 20. Ne6 Nxe6 21. Rxe6 Kf7 22. Rxf6+ Kxf6 23. Qh6+) 6... Ne5 7. Bb5+ Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Nxd7 9. h4 Bg7 {Other moves are less fortunate. For example, if 9...g4  then 10.Nd4  gives White an advantage.  Another interesting try is 9...Ne7 10.hxg5 Ng6 11.Qd4 Nde5 12.Bxf4! Nxf4 13.Nxe5 Bg7 14.Qa4+ Kf8 15.Nd7+ Ke7 16.Nf6 h6 17.e5±  and White is almost winning .} 10. hxg5 Qe7 11. Qe2 O-O-O 12. Bxf4 {Black is doomed to fight for equality.}) 5. d3 c6 {5...Bg4  can be met by 6.Be2 .} 6. Be2 g6 7. O-O Bg7 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Nb1 {This is a typical idea. We are aiming to reroute the knight via Nd2 - c4 or f3 depending on the situation.} Nh6 11. Bf4 {With comfortable play ahead for White. Chapter 3-2...d6 Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) (3... Nf6 {Generally Black wants to take the f4 -pawn right away or not at all.} 4. Nf3 Be7 {4...exf4  is not very good here because of 5.d4 Nh5 6.Nd5  with an advantage for White due to the greater central control.} 5. d4 exd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 {6...O-O  is also possible. But after 7.Be3 Nc6 8.Qd2  White has a slight pull.} 7. Qd1 {This is a better square for the queen than on e3 as 7.Qe3  allows the beautiful and effective 7...d5! .} O-O 8. a3 Re8 9. Bd3 {Gives White a very comfortable position.}) (3... g6 $6 {Is just bad because of} 4. fxe5 dxe5 5. Nf3 Bg7 {If 5...Nc6  then 6.Bb5!} 6. Bc4 {And White got a clearly better position in Howell, D. vs Moroni, L. Chess.com INT 2021 .}) (3... c6 $6 {Is also dubious because of} 4. d4 Nd7 5. Nf3 {Now this looks like some Pirc or Philidor lines.}) 4. Qf3 {Almost a novelty. As you can see, White is serious about winning back a pawn! Black has many options here 4...Nc6 , 4...g5 , 4...Nf6 , 4...Qh4+  all of which are covered in the corresponding chapter .} g5 (4... Nf6 5. d4 Nc6 6. Bb5 Bd7 7. Nge2 {White wins back a pawn and enjoys all the benefits of active play.} Nb4 8. O-O {We shouldn't be afraid on 8...Nxc2  as after 9.Rb1  we have many attacking possibilities on the kingside. Our idea should be to push e5 at the right time.}) (4... Qh4+ 5. g3 fxg3 6. hxg3 Qf6 {6...Qd8  is met by 7.d4 .} 7. Nd5 Qd8 {7...Qxf3  just loses on the spot 8.Nxf3 Kd7 9.Ng5 Nh6 10.Rxh6 gxh6 11.Nxf7+- .} 8. d4 c6 9. Nc3 {White has amazing development and various ideas.}) (4... Nc6 {Defending the pawn with 4...g5  can be met with 5.h4 Nc6  threatening Nd4 . 6.d3 g4 7.Qf2!  with a slight advantage for White.} 5. Qxf4 {5.Bb5  doesn't promise anything in this case. 5...Bd7 6.Bxc6 Bxc6 7.Qxf4 d5= .} g6 (5... Nf6 6. Nf3 $5 Be7 {6...Nb4  will eventually transpose to the main variation after 7.Bb5+ Bd7 8.Bxd7+ Qxd7 9.O-O Nxc2 10.Rb1 Nb4 11.d4 Be7 12.Nh4 .} 7. d4 Nb4 8. Bb5+ Bd7 9. Bxd7+ Qxd7 10. O-O Nxc2 11. Rb1 Nb4 12. Nh4 g6 {12...Nd3  is also possible. 13.Qf3 Nxc1 14.Nf5!?  gives White sufficient initiative .} 13. Qf3 Qg4 $1 14. Qxg4 Nxg4 15. Nf3 {White has a full compensation for a pawn, but it's not clear if we can seek for more.}) (5... Nb4 {A principled move. Another logical try is 5...Nd4 6.Bd3 Nf6 7.Nge2 Ne6 8.Qg3 g6 9.Bc4 Bg7 10.Qh4=  and it's more or less equal. White should first castle followed by d3 and perhaps Bh6 in the future.} 6. Kd1 {Our king always plays an important role!} g6 (6... Be7 {Doesn't pose any problems either.} 7. d4 Nf6 8. a3 Nc6 9. Nf3 O-O 10. Bd3 Bg4 11. Qe3 Bh5 12. h3 {Even though our king is in the center, we will retain a slight advantage because of central control and some kingside attacking ideas via g4 , our king can always secure itself via Bd2-Kc1 .}) (6... c5 {Black establishes control over d4 square.} 7. b3 Be7 8. Bb2 Nf6 9. a3 Nc6 10. Nd5 $5 Nxd5 {Another alternative here is 10...Ne5  but now White can simplify with 11.Bb5+ Bd7 12.Bxd7+ Qxd7 13.Nxe7 Qxe7 14.Nf3  and White is quite comfortable.} 11. exd5 Ne5 12. Nf3 O-O 13. Nxe5 dxe5 14. Bxe5 Qxd5 15. Kc1 {White has a lot of simple improving moves, while our king will be completely safe on b2 .}) (6... Nf6 7. Nf3 g6 (7... d5 {This is topical, but White is also ready to play in the center.} 8. Nb5 Na6 9. exd5 Be7 10. Bc4 O-O 11. Re1 {With the idea of Nc3-d3 followed by developing our bishop.}) (7... h6 $5 {Black wants to kick the white queen from a very sound position. 7...c5  eventually leads to a transposition 8.b3 Be7 9.Bb2 O-O  9...Be6 10.Bc4⩲ .  10.a3 Nc6 11.Nd5 Nxd5 12.exd5 Ne5 13.Nxe5 dxe5 14.Bxe5 .} 8. Bc4 g5 9. Qe3 Bg7 10. h4 g4 {It's better to keep the kingside closed. Worse is 10...Ng4 11.Qe1 gxh4 12.Rxh4 Nc6 13.d3 h5 14.Bg5 f6 15.Bd2⩲  with a slight advantage.} 11. e5 {The only move but sufficient.} dxe5 12. Qxe5+ Kf8 {A very chaotic position. As usual, engines recommend us to repeat moves.} 13. Nd4 Nfd5 14. Qe4 Nf6 {With a repetition. 14...Nb6  doesn't promise an easy life 15.Nde2 Nxc4 16.Qxc4 Nc6 17.Rf1⩲ .}) 8. Bc4 $1 Be6 {8...Nc6  can be met with 9.d4 Bg7 10.Rf1  with a nice attacking game ahead.} 9. Bxe6 fxe6 10. e5 Nfd5 11. Qg4 Qd7 12. Re1 O-O-O 13. Nxd5 Nxd5 14. d3 {White is better here. The only question is how to bring the queen's rook into play.} Re8 15. a4 {For example, like this!} Bg7 16. exd6 cxd6 17. Ra3 Rhf8 18. Rb3 Rf5 19. Bd2 {Rooks are completely in play. Next stop is to transfer our king to safety via Kc1 - b1 .}) 7. Bb5+ $5 {This position is really imbalanced. Which is sort of the point, isn't it?} c6 (7... Nc6 8. Nge2 Bg7 9. Rf1 Nh6 10. d4 O-O 11. Qg5 $1 {Black’s queen does a nice job protecting c7 , so we're happy to trade it away.} f5 12. Nd5 Bd7 13. Qxd8 Raxd8 14. Bg5 Rde8 15. c3 {With a slight advantage.}) (7... Bd7 8. e5 Nc6 (8... c6 9. Be2 Nd5 10. Nxd5 cxd5 11. exd6 Bg7 12. Qe3+ Be6 13. Bg4 {We have to create a target in the opponent's camp.} Qxd6 14. Bxe6 fxe6 15. Nf3 Ne7 16. Re1 e5 $1 {Only move.} 17. Nxe5 O-O 18. Nf3 Nf5 19. Qe6+ Qxe6 20. Rxe6 {Black has to play vigorously to maintain equality.}) 9. Nf3 {Black has to be creative and sacrifice a pawn not to get into big trouble.} Bh6 {9...dxe5  makes no sense. 10.Nxe5 Qf6 11.Qxf6 Nxf6 12.Nxd7 Kxd7 13.Rf1  with a great position.} 10. Qg3 Nge7 {10...a6  can be answered with the simple 11.Bc4  and White is slightly better.} 11. exd6 cxd6 12. Qxd6 O-O 13. Ne4 {A pawn is a pawn, and our king is not so unsafe after all.}) 8. a3 $1 Na6 {8...Nxc2?!  is not great because of 9.Bc4 d5 10.exd5 Nxa1 11.Qe5+±  and White has a clearly better position.} 9. Bxa6 bxa6 10. Nf3 Bg7 11. Rf1 Ne7 12. b3 O-O 13. Bb2 c5 14. Kc1 {White should trade a pair of bishops, connect rooks and stabilize the pawn structure in order to get a serious advantage.}) 6. Bb5 Bg7 7. d3 Nge7 8. Nf3 {We are in time to develop harmoniously.}) 5. h4 Nc6 6. d3 g4 7. Qf2 {White is slightly better as we take the f4 -pawn on the next move.}) (2... f5 $2 {Gioachino Greco tried this in 1620, but even that won't make this move better.} 3. exf5 {Black can't develop properly because of Qh5 + threat.} Qh4+ (3... Qe7 {Makes a bit more sense than 3...Qh4+ . Again, we simply play for development.} 4. Nf3 e4 {Probably the best attempt for Black.} 5. Ng5 Nf6 6. Be2 {One of our ideas is to use the h5 - e8 diagonal to give this very nasty check.} d5 7. Bh5+ Kd7 8. O-O c6 9. d3 {Open the center, close the game. Black's king is exposed and he is a pawn down.}) 4. g3 Qe7 5. Bg2 {Our plan is simple: develop pieces and win. 5.Qh5+  is also fine, but for the educational purposes I'd like to leave the full game here. 5...Kd8 6.fxe5 Qxe5+ 7.Be2 Nf6 8.Qf3 d5 9.g4 h5 10.h3 hxg4 11.hxg4 Rxh1 12.Qxh1 Qg3+ 13.Kd1 Nxg4 14.Qxd5+ Bd7 15.Nf3 Nf2+ 16.Ke1 Nd3+ 17.Kd1 Qe1+ 18.Nxe1 Nf2#  0-1 (18) NN-Greco,G Europe 1620 .} exf4+ 6. Ne2 fxg3 7. O-O {This is completely unplayable for Black. Not only hasn't he developed a single piece yet, his queen even impedes his development. Chapter 1-2...Nf6,2 Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) (2... Qh4+ $2 {This simply can't be a good move.} 3. g3 Qe7 4. Nc3 exf4 5. d4 {One of the main concepts of any gambit - don't care too much about material and develop the pieces.} fxg3 6. hxg3 d5 7. Bf4 Be6 (7... c6 8. Qd2 Nf6 9. O-O-O {We shouldn't close the center with  9.d5  when we are this far ahead in development.} Nxe4 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. d5 {Black didn't develop any pieces and therefore finds himselft on a verge of defeat.}) 8. Rh2 $1 {A useful rook lift.} dxe4 9. d5 Bd7 10. Qd2 {We finish our development and simply crush our opponent in the center. O-O-O , d6 and Nxe4 are coming. You may also consider simply taking Bxc7 .}) (2... c6 {This is a possible move, but looks more like another attempt to provoke White for 3.fxe5?? Qh4+ .} 3. Nf3 {Another possible concept is 3.d3!? exf4 4.Bxf4 d5 5.Nd2  1-0 Gufeld, E. - Saidy, A. Honolulu 1996 .} d5 {This breaktrough is now late.} (3... exf4 4. d4 d5 {This is quite similar to the Falkbeer Countergambit, but here Black has less options: Black can't support the f4 -pawn with ... g5 .} 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. exd5 cxd5 (6... Bd6 7. Qe2+ {It's vital to prevent Black from castling.} Kf8 (7... Qe7 8. Qxe7+ Kxe7 {This endgame is equal, but it's White who is trying to pose problems.} 9. Bc4 {Ideally, we'd like to have two connected passed pawns on the queenside, but Black can prevent this with accurate play.} b5 $1 10. Bb3 b4 11. Ne2 Nxd5 {White has several options now, but in general, the analysis suggests that Black can hold this position with precise play.} 12. Bxd5 cxd5 13. Bxf4 Nc6 14. a3 Ba6 $5 {This is good enough for Black to make a draw, but he still needs to be precise.} 15. axb4 Nxb4 16. Bxd6+ Kxd6 17. Kd2 Bxe2 18. Kxe2 Nxc2 19. Ra6+ Ke7 20. Rc1 Rhc8 21. Kd2 Nb4 22. Rxa7+ Rxa7 23. Rxc8 {The game should end in a draw, but Black had plenty of moments where he could have gone wrong.}) 8. Ne5 g5 $2 {This was played by Anand once, but it is nearly losing:} (8... Nxd5 9. Nxd5 Qh4+ 10. Qf2 {We don't have to do anything special, the endgame is more than fine for us!} Qxf2+ 11. Kxf2 cxd5 12. Bxf4 Nc6 13. Rd1 {One idea is Ng6 + to obtain the bishop pair. Generally, the d5 -pawn remains weak and we will get the two bishops advantage. g3 followed by Bg2 is a pleasant maneuver for White too. We won't deliver a checkmate anytime soon, but a good endgame is a very decent outcome of the opening.}) 9. h4 Nxd5 10. Bd2 {Or 10.Nxd5 cxd5 11.Qh5 Qe7 12.Qh6+ Ke8 13.Qg7±  as in 1/2 Nunn, J. - Anand, V. Monte Carlo 1995 .} Nd7 11. O-O-O {This position is nowhere near playable for Black: we simply open files on the kingside with g3 and go for the direct attack. The king is really poor on f8 and Black has no counterplay.}) 7. Bxf4 Bd6 8. Bxd6 Qxd6 9. Qe2+ {In general, our idea is to exchange queens because of Black's isolated queen's pawn ( d5 ).} Be6 10. Qe5 {We don't have anything against endgames.} Qb6 11. O-O-O Nc6 12. Qf4 O-O 13. a3 Rac8 14. Bd3 {It's really hard for Black to find any counterplay. Our plan is very simple though: just Rde1 and Rhf1 , Ng5 , g2 - g4 and something like this should certainly work out well.}) 4. Nxe5 dxe4 5. Bc4 Nh6 6. Nc3 f6 7. d3 {Our knight is a symbolic price for such activity. 7.d4 Bf5 8.O-O Nd7 9.Kh1∞  is very promising too.} b5 8. Bb3 fxe5 9. Qh5+ Kd7 10. fxe5 {Black's king is too exposed, White dominates the game.} Kc7 11. e6 exd3 12. Qe5+ Qd6 13. Qe4 {White is perfectly centralized. Perhaps, Black can hold, but it's hard to find someone who'll play like this voluntarily.}) (2... Nf6 {A solid move, after which White can fight for a small advantage.} 3. fxe5 Nxe4 4. Nf3 d5 {This gives White too many tempi.} (4... Ng5 {This leads to another pleasant endgame for White.} 5. d4 Nxf3+ 6. Qxf3 Qh4+ 7. Qf2 {The King's Gambit is an interesting opening, we sacrifice some pawns and try to play for an attack. But when the opportunity presents itself, we gladly agree to playing a slightly better endgame.} Qxf2+ 8. Kxf2 Nc6 9. c3 d6 10. exd6 Bxd6 11. Nd2 Be6 12. Be2 {This is a very typical and pleasant structure for White. Nc4 and Ne4 are both possible, depending on Black's actions. a4 - a5 is one of the ideas too. Try to torture your opponent for as long as possible in these positions!}) (4... Be7 5. d3 {5.d4  is also possible: 5...d5 6.exd6 cxd6  6...Nxd6 7.Bd3  is also decent.  7.Bd3 d5 8.O-O Nc6 9.c4 Nb4 10.Be2  looks like a well-known Petroff line without the c7 and f2 pawns.} Ng5 6. d4 O-O 7. Bd3 {Putting the bishop onto the optimal square and looking closely at the opponent's king.} Nxf3+ 8. Qxf3 d6 9. Nc3 {Our next moves are easy and natural.} Nc6 (9... dxe5 10. dxe5 Nc6 11. Bf4 {White has better development and is likely to be the first to create threats.} Nb4 {This doesn't change the script much.} (11... Nd4 12. Qf2 Bg4 {Black gives up a few tempi attempting to prevent us from castling queenside.} 13. h3 Bh5 14. g4 Bg6 (14... Bh4 {This is more precise than 14... Bg6 ; Black immediately simplifies the position.} 15. Bg3 Bxg3 16. Qxg3 Bg6 {Black obvioulsy should reduce piece quantity in order not to get into trouble.} 17. Bxg6 hxg6 18. O-O-O Qd7 19. h4 {Black is probably in time to centralize and repel threats, but it's easier to play as White here.}) 15. O-O-O c5 16. Kb1 Qd7 17. Qg2 Rad8 18. h4 {White has a nice initiative. Our attack on the dark squares with ideas like h4 - h5 - h6 and Ne4 - f6 + can never be underestimated.}) 12. O-O-O Nxd3+ 13. Rxd3 Qe8 14. Nd5 {Black shouldn't be in time to create any counterplay. White can put pressure on the dark squares and march his h-pawn forward.}) 10. Bf4 {Preparing O-O-O , the d4 -pawn is poisoned.} Nxd4 $2 {The pawn is poisoned.} (10... Nb4 11. O-O-O Nxd3+ 12. Rxd3 Bf5 13. exd6 cxd6 14. Re3 Bf6 15. Nd5 {Our pieces are slightly more active and our king should be safe after c2-c3 . Black's bishop pair is nothing to worry about, we can capture on f6 at any given time. 15...Bxd4  loses to 16.Ne7+ .}) 11. Qe4 Nf5 12. g4 Bh4+ 13. Bg3 {Black should be happy not to resign immideately.} Re8 14. O-O-O Bxg3 15. hxg3 Qg5+ 16. Qf4 {Again, White reaches a favorable endgame.} Qxf4+ 17. gxf4 Ne3 {Black needs to try this because Nb5 and Nd5 were unpleasant threats.} 18. Rde1 Ng2 19. Bxh7+ Kf8 20. Ref1 {Black still needs to complete his development and his knight is poorly placed on g2 . Our bishop can retreat to e4 with the gain of tempo and Nb5 / Nd5 are still difficult to meet. Black may not be lost yet, but we're getting there.}) 5. d3 Nc5 6. d4 Ne6 7. Nc3 {White has space and a development advantage. 7.c4  is also possible.}) (2... Qf6 {One of the very strange variations, which was not mentioned in the course.} 3. Nf3 {After this precise move, Black already has a difficult position.} d6 {3...Qxf4 4.Nc3 Qf6 5.Bc4±} 4. Nc3 exf4 5. Nd5 Qd8 6. Nxf4 Nf6 7. d4 $5 {White is not afraid to sacrifice a pawn for a few tempi.} Nxe4 8. Bd3 Nf6 9. O-O Be7 10. Ng5 O-O 11. Qf3 h6 12. Ne4 Nbd7 13. Ng3 $5 {We don't want to exchange pieces while attacking unless we can profit from it directly.} Nb6 14. b3 Bg4 15. Qf2 d5 16. h3 Bd7 17. c4 c5 18. a4 $1 {This move helps us to start a very important plan!} dxc4 19. bxc4 Nxa4 20. d5 b5 21. Ra3 $1 {Our idea becomes clear. Bishop retreats to b1 to be joined by the queen from f2 . Our rook will be placed perfectly on f3 or g3 .} Qc7 22. Bb1 Rab8 23. Re3 Bd6 24. Qc2 bxc4 25. Ngh5 {White's attack has reached its peak, position is absolutely winning and the logical result is inevitable.} Rxb1 26. Nxf6+ gxf6 27. Nh5 f5 28. Qxb1 Be5 29. Rxe5 Qxe5 30. Bf4 Qd4+ 31. Kh2 Nc3 32. Qb7 Nxd5 33. Qxd5 Qxd5 34. Nf6+ Kg7 35. Nxd5 Re8 36. Rf3 Re6 37. Rg3+ Rg6 38. Ra3 a6 39. Be3 Rd6 40. Nf4 Rc6 41. Kg1 Kh7 42. Kh2 Kg7 43. h4 Be8 44. Rc3 Rc8 45. Rxc4 a5 46. Rc3 a4 47. Nd5 f6 48. Ne7 Ra8 49. Bxc5 Bd7 50. Ba3 Ra7 51. h5 f4 52. Nd5 Kf7 53. Nxf4 Rb7 54. Rc4 Ra7 55. Rd4 Bb5 56. Rd6 Ra6 57. Rd8 Be8 58. Ng6 Bc6 59. g4 Ra5 60. Bb2 Ke6 61. Rf8 Bb5 62. Rxf6+ Kd7 63. Ne5+ Kc8 64. Rxh6 a3 65. Rh8+ Kc7 {Model Games &amp; Afterword Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) 3. Nf3 {I suggest we choose the King's Knight Gambit. This healthy developing move protects us from an unpleasant check. 3.Bc4 , 3.Nc3 , and even 3.d4  also deserve attention, but probably cannot withstand the onslaught of chess engines. The good news is that in any case, everything is decided over the board. Black has many choices again, but the most challenging moves are:} (3. Bc4 Qh4+ (3... Nf6 4. Nc3 c6 5. Bb3 d5 6. exd5 cxd5 7. d4 Bd6 (7... Bb4 8. Nf3 O-O 9. O-O Bxc3 10. bxc3 Qc7 11. Qe1 {White pulls his pieces towards the kingside. In this regard the h4 -square looks perfect for the queen.} Nc6 12. Qh4 Ne7 {Anand prepares to repel the siege.} 13. Bxf4 Qxc3 14. Bd2 Qc7 15. Ne5 Nf5 16. Qf4 Be6 17. Bb4 Rfc8 {Black repels the first wave of the attack, so Alexander begins to pull up those pieces that are not yet involved.} 18. g4 Nd6 19. Rae1 {The position is very complex and double-edged, and here Vishy did not feel the main threat of White.} Nfe4 {19...Nc4 20.g5 Nd7 21.Bxc4 Nxe5 22.Bxd5 Bxd5 23.Rxe5 Qc4 24.a3∞} 20. c4 $1 {Wonderful move! The light-squared bishop joins the attack too.} dxc4 21. Bc2 Nf6 {21...Qb6 22.Rxe4 Qxb4 23.Re3  and the white pieces look menacingly towards the black king.} 22. g5 Nh5 $2 {22...Nd5! 23.Bxh7+ Kxh7 24.Qh4+ Kg8 25.Bxd6 Qxd6 26.g6 fxg6 27.Nxg6 Bf5 28.Qh8+ Kf7 29.Rxf5+ Nf6 30.Qh5 Qxd4+ 31.Kg2 Qd2+ 32.Re2 Qd5+! , but to go for this line you must have absolutely steel nerves and indestructible faith in yourself.} 23. Qf3 g6 24. Nxg6 $1 {Blowing up Black's defenses!} hxg6 25. Bxg6 fxg6 26. Rxe6 Qf7 27. Qd5 Nf5 28. Rxf5 $1) 8. Nge2 f3 $2 {Very unfortunate move.} 9. gxf3 O-O 10. Bg5 {White's play develops by itself, which Ivanchuk uses masterly.} Be6 11. Qd2 Be7 12. O-O-O Nc6 13. Nf4 Na5 14. Rhe1 {Forcing Black to capture on b3 .} Nxb3+ {14...Qd6 15.Bxd5+-} 15. axb3 h6 16. Bh4 Qa5 17. Kb1 {So, with very natural moves, Vasyl Mykhaylovych managed to get an absolutely winning position.} Ne4 18. fxe4 Bxh4 19. Rg1 Bg5 20. h4 $1 {Removing the last obstacle before a direct attack.} Bxf4 21. Qxf4 Kh8 22. exd5 Bd7 23. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 24. Rg1+ Kh7 25. Ne4 f6 26. Nxf6+ Rxf6 27. Qxf6 Bg4 28. Qe7+) 4. Kf1 b5 5. Bxb5 Nf6 6. Nf3 Qh6 7. d3 Nh5 $6 {Up to this moment, boths sides played well, but here Black could not resist to set a simple trap.} 8. Nh4 $6 {8.Rg1 c6 9.Bc4±  and g4 - g5 grants White huge advantage.} Qg5 9. Nf5 {After an exchange of pleasantries, the position remained very complex.} c6 {9...g6 10.h4 Qf6 11.Nc3 c6 12.Ba4∞} 10. g4 $5 {Objectively not the best move, but Anderssen foresaw the beautiful idea.} Nf6 {10...g6 11.gxh5 gxf5 12.Rg1 Qh4 13.Qf3 cxb5 14.exf5 b4!  The first line the computer gives looks roughly like this and the last move just leaves me speechless.} 11. Rg1 cxb5 {11...h5 12.h4 Qg6 13.g5 Ng4 14.Ba4∞  was the last chance to complicate the fight.} 12. h4 $1 Qg6 13. h5 Qg5 14. Qf3 $1 Ng8 15. Bxf4 Qf6 16. Nc3 Bc5 17. Nd5 {White is obviously dominating.} Qxb2 18. Bd6 $1 {?!! White misses the victory, but this move is an introduction to an incredibly beautiful checkmate combination.} Bxg1 {18...Qxa1+ 19.Ke2 Qb2! 20.Bxc5 Qxc2+ 21.Kf1 Qxc5 22.d4  and even here, having an extra rook and piece, Black should be very careful not to lose quickly.} 19. e5 $1 {Black is defenseless.} Qxa1+ 20. Ke2 Na6 21. Nxg7+ Kd8 22. Qf6+ $3 Nxf6 23. Be7#) 3... d6 {not revealing their cards and remaining flexible (covered in Chapter 7 ), and 3...g5  protecting the extra pawn and preparing for further conquests, which we cover in Chapter 8 . While working on the course, I was disappointed and fascinated again and again. I can claim that the King's Gambit is decent for rapid or blitz and can also be rarely used as a surprise weapon in classical chess. Today I'll be your guide. Welcome to the King's Gambit Museum! The most flexible move. Black doesn't give up on the g5 idea while continues normal development.} (3... g5 {This move is being played more often than others. Black protects on f4 and prepares further advancing of the pawn.} 4. h4 {It is important to undermine the pawn structure and prevent the queen from reaching h4 .} g4 (4... d5 $5 {Almost no one played like this, but it's an interesting move which deserves serious attention.} 5. d4 $1 {Development first.} dxe4 6. Nxg5 Nf6 {If Black tries to be greedy with 6...e3  then we respond with 7.Bc4  followed by castle.} 7. Nc3 {Unfortunately 7.Bc4  does not work in this case as Black can respond with 7...Bg4! .} Bb4 8. Bxf4 h6 9. Bc4 $1 {The knight was a lost cause anyway, as this is a typical theme in the King's Gambit.} hxg5 10. Bxg5 {We can already see the compensation for the sacrificed piece. Few engine lines would help to understand what's going on:} Bxc3+ (10... Nc6 11. Qd2 {Castling on the short side is also a possibility. But in this case our queenside pawn structure is intact. Therefore, it makes more sense to castle long. Get ready for another crazy line, Black has to find only moves to survive here.} Be7 {If Black tries to stop us from castling long through 11...Bg4 , we can go to the other side with 12.O-O .} 12. O-O-O Na5 {Only move once again. Black has to be precise in order to equalise.} 13. Bb5+ c6 14. d5 {Offering a second piece for activity.} cxb5 15. d6 Nc4 {Black can also try the safer option 15...Qxd6 16.Qxd6 Bxd6 17.Rxd6 Nd7 18.Nxb5  where the position remains double edged.} 16. Qe1 Nxd6 {Now the following sequence is more or less forced.} 17. Bxf6 Bxf6 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Rxd8+ Bxd8 20. Qxe4+ Be7 21. Re1 Be6 22. Qxb7 Rd8 23. Qxb5+ Kf8 24. g3 {The position remains unclear.}) (10... Be7 11. O-O {Threatening Nxe4 .} Nc6 12. Nxe4 {Another fun line is 12.Qd2 Be6 13.d5 e3 14.Qd1 Bg4 15.dxc6! Bxd1 16.Raxd1 Qb8 17.Bxf6 Bxf6 18.Ne4 Bxh4 19.Bxf7+ Kf8 20.Bc4+ Kg7 21.Rd7+ Kh6 22.Rf4 Bg5 23.Nxg5 Kxg5 24.Rf3  with a draw in six lines!} Nxe4 13. Bxf7+ Kf8 14. Bd5+ Nf6 {Another interesting yet daring try for Black is 14...Kg7 15.Rf7+ Kg6 16.h5+ Kxg5 17.Rg7+ Kh6 18.Rg6+ Kh7 19.Bxe4 Qxd4+ 20.Qxd4 Nxd4 21.Re6+ Kg7 22.Rxe7+ Kh6 23.Rxc7  which leads to more or less equal endgame.} 15. Bxc6 bxc6 16. Qd3 {Threatening the deadly Rxf6+ followed by Raf1 .} Kg7 {16...Ba6 17.c4  will transpose into our main variation.} 17. Rae1 Ba6 18. c4 {White has enough firepower to secure a draw. Black should be reasonably careful here. For example: 18...Rf8 19.h5 Kh8  19...Bxc4 20.Qxc4 Nd5 21.Bxe7 Nxe7 22.h6+ Kh8 23.d5 Nxd5 24.Qd4+ Nf6 25.Qc3 Qd6 26.Re5 Rf7 27.Ref5 Raf8 28.g4 Kh7 29.g5 Rg8 30.Kh1=  20.Re6 Ng8 21.Bxe7 Rxf1+ 22.Kxf1 Nxe7 23.Rh6+ Kg7 24.Qh7+ Kf8 25.Rf6+ Ke8 26.Qf7+ Kd7 27.Qe6+=}) 11. bxc3 Qd6 {Getting out of the dreaded pin.} 12. O-O Nd5 13. Qe1 Nc6 {Black can also try the logical 13...f5  where we go all in with 14.g4!  The position is still quite complicated. However, practically Black will struggle to defend.} 14. Qxe4+ Be6 15. Qd3 {We are not in a rush. The idea is to protect our pawn on c3 and slowly improve with Bb3 - c4 .} Kd7 $1 {Black is forcing us to take the knight with this move. As we shall see:} 16. Bxd5 {Unfortunately 16.Bb3  does not work in this case as Black can carry out the plan with 16...Rag8 17.c4 Rxg5!  with a winning position.} Qxd5 17. c4 $1 Qxc4 18. d5 Qxd5 19. Qxd5+ Bxd5 20. Rad1 {We have recovered our sacrificed piece, or have we?} Ke6 21. g4 {Now we can happily give a perpetual with Rf6-Rf5+ . 21...Rag8 22.Rf6+ Ke5 23.Rf5+ Ke6 24.Rf6+= Chapter 8-King's Gambit Accepted - 3... Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) 5. Ne5 Nf6 (5... d6 {Also a very important line.} 6. Nxg4 Be7 (6... Nc6 7. Nf2 Nf6 (7... Qf6 $5 {An interesting way for Black to fight for advantage.} 8. c3 Bd7 9. d4 {The center looks pretty, but if White won't develop in time, it will be painful.} h5 (9... O-O-O 10. Qf3 Bh6 11. Bc4 {It's important to control the g8 -square in some lines. So it goes.} Qg7 {Other interesting lines include: 11...Qg6 12.Bxf4 f5 13.O-O Nge7 14.h5 Qg7 15.Na3 fxe4 16.Nxe4 d5 17.Nb5! dxe4 18.Bxh6 Qxh6 19.Qg3 Kb8 20.Nxc7 Rc8 21.Na6+ Ka8 22.Nc7+=  with a perpetual.  11...Re8 12.Na3 Qg7 13.Bxf4 f5 14.O-O Nge7 15.Rae1 Ng6 16.Bxh6 Qxh6 17.h5 Nh4 18.Qh3∞ .} 12. Bxf4 f5 13. Nd2 {A long computer line ends with a slightly worse endgame, but White has decent counterplay to make a draw. 13...Nge7 14.Bxh6 Qxh6 15.exf5 Nxf5 16.g4 Rde8+ 17.Nfe4 Qe3+ 18.Qe2 Qxe2+ 19.Bxe2 Nfxd4 20.cxd4 Nxd4 21.g5 Bf5 22.Bh5 Bxe4 23.Nxe4 Rxe4+ 24.Kd2  Obviously one doesn't simply memorize all the computer lines, but we also don't expect our opponents to play like Stockfish or Leela.}) 10. Nd2 $5 O-O-O 11. Nf3 Re8 12. Ng5 Nge7 {It is also possible to play 12...d5 13.e5 Qe7 14.Be2 f6 15.exf6 Nxf6 16.Bxf4 Bh6 17.g3 Na5 18.Nd3 Nc4 19.Ne5∞ .} 13. Qf3 Qg7 14. Bxf4 f6 15. Ngh3 Bg4 16. Qd3 d5 {Incredible adventures await us...} 17. g3 Ng6 18. Kd2 {Getting out of the pin.} dxe4 19. Nxe4 Nce5 $1 20. dxe5 Rd8 21. exf6 Rxd3+ 22. Bxd3 Qd7 23. Nhf2 {Despite the fact Stockfish is rather skeptical, I'd say there's a lot of play ahead.}) 8. d4 Bh6 9. Be2 Qe7 {It's important to meet 9...Rg8  with 10.Bf3 .  Note that 10.O-O  allows 10...f3! 11.Bxf3 Bxc1 12.Qxc1 Nxd4 .} 10. Nc3 Bd7 11. Nd5 {11.a3!?  would be a great idea, but after 11...O-O-O 12.Qd3 Kb8 13.Bd2  Black has Be6! 14.O-O-O d5⩱ .} Nxd5 12. exd5 Nb4 13. c4 {I honestly have no idea what's going on, but the computer shows equality in every sensible variation.} Bf5 14. O-O Rg8 {Is another logical possibility. But as mentioned earlier, we will be fine with a little precision.} (14... Nc2 15. Bd3 Bxd3 {15...Ne3  is quite harmless as after 16.Bxe3 fxe3 17.Ng4 e2 18.Qxe2 Qxe2 19.Nf6+ Ke7 20.Bxe2 Kxf6 21.Bg4  we maintain a small edge. Although with correct play Black can equalise.} 16. Nxd3 Ne3 17. Bxe3 Qxe3+ {17...fxe3  allows 18.Qg4  and White suddenly has an almost winning position.} 18. Kh1 O-O-O {Black should not be greedy with 18...Qxd4  because now Black wont be able to castle. 19.Qe2+ Qe3 20.Qh5! Qxd3 21.Rae1+  with a great position.} 19. Nxf4 Rde8 20. Qh5 Bxf4 21. Qf5+ Kb8 22. Qxf4 {We have an extra pawn, but Black's activity compensates for that.}) 15. Ng4 Bxg4 16. Bxg4 Nc2 17. Bh3 Qe4 {If 17...Qxh4  then 18.Qxc2 Qxh3 19.Bxf4 O-O-O 20.Rae1= .} 18. Rf2 Ne3 19. Qf3 Qxd4 20. Re2 Kf8 21. Bxe3 fxe3 22. Rf1 {And we have equalised. However, practically it is still quite unclear.}) (6... Nf6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 8. Nc3 {8.d4  is an interesting alternative, but sadly it does not fully equalise. 8...Nc6 9.Nc3 Qxd4 10.Bxf4 Bg7 11.Qxd4 Nxd4 12.O-O-O  This endgame is slightly worse for White.} Nc6 9. Nd5 Qg7 10. d3 Qg3+ (10... Nb4 $5 11. Bxf4 {The king's journey doesn't promise an attractive position: 11.Nxb4? Qg3+ 12.Kd2 Qe3+ 13.Kc3 Bg7+ 14.Kb3 Qb6∓ .} Nxd5 12. exd5 Qxb2 13. Qc1 {White has equalised. With correct play from both sides we will reach the following position: 13...Qc3+ 14.Kf2 Bg7 15.Rb1 O-O 16.Be2 Qd4+ 17.Be3 Qxd5 18.a3 Re8 19.Bf3 Qe6 20.Re1=  where piece activity compensates the missing pawn.}) 11. Kd2 Nd4 {This is for the gourmets. Another try is 11...Nb4 12.Qf3 Qxf3 13.gxf3 Nxd5 14.exd5= .} 12. c3 c6 13. Nf6+ Ke7 14. cxd4 Kxf6 15. Be2 {With counterplay as both kings are exposed currently.}) 7. d4 {The pawn on h4 is usually sacrificed without a shadow of doubt.} Bxh4+ 8. Nf2 Bg3 9. Qf3 {If Black plays well here, we should be precise to secure a draw.} Nc6 (9... Qf6 10. Nc3 Qxd4 11. Nb5 Qc5 {It is also possible to play 11...Qb6 12.Bxf4 Bxf4 13.Qxf4 a6 14.Qg3 Nf6  Note that 14...Ne7  is simply a blunder in view of the beautiful 15.Qc3!  15.Qg7 Rg8 16.Nxc7+ Qxc7 17.Qxf6 Qa5+ 18.Qc3 Qxc3+ 19.bxc3= .} 12. Bxf4 Bxf4 13. Qxf4 a6 14. Nc3 Be6 15. Nd5 Nd7 {Black can also take 15...Bxd5  but it allows an important resource as you will see. 16.exd5 Nd7 17.O-O-O O-O-O 18.Ne4 Qb6 19.Rh3!  And this rook lift can be dangerous for Black.} 16. b4 $5 Qc6 17. b5 Qc5 18. Nd3 {The position remains unclear with both sides having chances.}) 10. Bxf4 Bxf4 11. Qxf4 Nxd4 12. Qd2 Ne6 13. Nc3 Qg5 {We have to accept this endgame, where we equalize with precise play.} 14. Qxg5 Nxg5 15. Nd5 Kd8 16. O-O-O Ne7 {Black can also try 16...c6 17.Ne3 Kc7 18.Nc4=  and we have equalised thanks to the weak pawn on d6 .} 17. Rh5 Ne6 18. Ne3 {White has a lot of compensation in this position thanks to the activity of our pieces.}) (5... h5 $6 6. Bc4 Rh7 {Too much, isn't it?} (6... Nh6 7. d4 d6 8. Nd3 f3 9. gxf3 Be7 {We literally don't care about the h4 -pawn.} (9... Nc6 10. Be3 Na5 {If 10...Be7  then the position will transpose after 11.Nc3 Bxh4+ 12.Kd2 .} 11. Bb3 gxf3 12. Qxf3 Bg4 13. Qf1 $5 Nxb3 14. axb3 Bg7 15. Nc3 O-O 16. Nf4 {... f5 is very annoying here, so it's good to set up the blockade in advance.} Qd7 17. Kd2 f5 18. Qc4+ Rf7 19. Rhg1 Kh8 20. Rae1 {With an unclear game ahead. We should try to exchange the dark-squared bishop through d5 - Bd4 as mentioned in the arrows.}) 10. Be3 Bxh4+ 11. Kd2 Nc6 12. Nc3 Na5 13. Bb3) 7. d4 f3 {If Black goes for 7...d6  then after 8.Nxf7 Rxf7 9.Bxf7+ Kxf7 10.Bxf4 Kg7 11.Nc3 Be6 12.Qd2  White is clearly better.} (7... Bh6 {As we know from Chapter 8 , 7...f3 8.gxf3 d6  is stronger.} 8. Nc3 Nc6 9. Nxf7 $1 {White’s pieces are ready to join the attack very quickly.} Rxf7 10. Bxf7+ Kxf7 11. Bxf4 Bxf4 12. O-O $1 {White's idea is based on this move.} Qxh4 13. Rxf4+ Kg7 14. Qd2 d6 15. Raf1 Nd8 16. Nd5 Bd7 17. e5 {All of White’s pieces are involved in the attack.} dxe5 18. dxe5 Bc6 19. e6 $1 {The black king is open to all winds now.} Bxd5 20. Rf7+ Nxf7 21. Rxf7+ Kh8 22. Qc3+ Nf6 23. Rxf6 Qxf6 24. Qxf6+ Kh7 25. Qf5+ Kh6 26. Qxd5 Kg6 27. Qd7) 8. gxf3 d6 9. Nxf7 $5 {9.Ng6  is also quite interesting.} Rxf7 10. Bg5 Be7 11. f4 {Very stylish and objectively very strong.} Rg7 12. Nc3 {Now we develop.} c6 13. Qd3 Kf8 14. O-O-O a5 15. Rhf1 b5 16. Bxg8 Kxg8 17. Ne2 {With a great position for us.}) (5... Qe7 $5 {This forces the transition to a roughly equal endgame.} 6. d4 d6 7. Nxg4 Qxe4+ 8. Qe2 Qxe2+ {Immediately goes to an equal endgame.} (8... Qe7 9. Qxe7+ Bxe7 10. Nf2 Nc6 11. c3 Nf6 {11...Be6 12.Bxf4  is just equal.} 12. Be2 {12.Bxf4 Nh5 13.Bh2  is also good.} Rg8 13. Bf3 {Perhaps the best square for our bishop.} Be6 14. Bxf4 Bd5 15. Ke2 O-O-O {Our plan is to bring the b1 -knight to a very nice square.} 16. Nd2 Rde8 17. Nf1 Bd8+ 18. Ne3) 9. Bxe2 h5 10. Nf2 Nc6 11. c3 {We will take the f4 pawn while developing.} Bh6 12. Nd3 Bf5 13. O-O O-O-O 14. Nxf4 Nce7 15. Na3 Nf6 16. Bd3 {White's idea should be to develop through Bd2-Rae1 , perhaps bring the knight into play with Nc2-Ne3 .}) (5... d5 $5 {Once a Falkbeer fan - always a Falkbeer fan!} 6. d4 $1 Nf6 7. Bxf4 Nh5 {7...Nxe4  is just bad because of 8.Nd2  with clear advantage for White.} 8. Bg5 f6 9. Be2 Ng3 {Black should be careful not to take 9...fxg5  as after 10.Bxg4 Bxg4 11.Qxg4 Ng7 12.Nc3  White should be winning because of piece activity and weak king in the center.} 10. Bxg4 {Black king has to retreat.} Bb4+ 11. c3 O-O 12. Rh3 Nxe4 13. Nd2 {We should be happy when there is such chaos on the board, shouldn't we?} Nxg5 14. hxg5 fxe5 15. Bxc8 Qxc8 16. Qb3 Qd7 17. Qxb4 Nc6 18. Qb5 exd4 19. O-O-O {We have equalised according to the computer. White's idea should be to go Rdh1 and put pressure on the h7 -pawn.}) (5... Nc6 $5 {A rare move, but a decent possibility.} 6. d4 {6.Nxg4  is just bad because of 6...d5 .} Nxe5 (6... Qf6 7. Nc3 Bb4 {Not good enough.} (7... Nxe5 8. dxe5 Qxe5 9. Qxg4 Bh6 {9...d6 10.Qf3 Bh6  transposes to the mainline.} 10. Qf3 d6 11. Be2 Be6 12. Nb5 {12.Rf1  is also possible, but boring: 12...O-O-O 13.Bxf4 Qxf4 14.Qxf4 Bxf4 15.Rxf4 Ne7 16.Rf6 Rdg8 17.Kf2 Rg6 18.Rxg6 fxg6 19.Ke3= .} O-O-O 13. Bd2 d5 14. exd5 Bxd5 15. Qh3+ Be6 16. Bc3 {Looks exciting, but will likely end in a draw as usual.} Qc5 17. Qf3 Ne7 $5 (17... f6 18. b4 Qe7 19. O-O Bd5 20. Qh3+ Kb8 21. Rae1 {With decent counterplay. Black should exchange the queens right away, which should equalise matters.}) 18. Bxh8 Rxh8 19. Qc3 Qxc3+ 20. Nxc3 Nf5 21. Bg4 Rg8 22. Bxf5 Bxf5 23. Kf2 {White is up an exchange, but Black has more than enough compensation to make up for this.}) 8. Nd3 Qxd4 9. Nxb4 Qxd1+ {9...Nxb4?  is just bad because of 10.Bxf4 Qxd1+ 11.Rxd1 d6 12.a3+-  and White is winning. This happened in Nunn, J. - Piket, J. Monte Carlo 1995 .} 10. Kxd1 Nxb4 11. Bxf4 d6 12. e5 {With advantage thanks to the double bishops and our precious pawn on e5 .}) 7. dxe5 Ne7 {7...d6?! 8.Bxf4 Bg7 9.Bc4 dxe5 10.Bxf7+±  leaves White with a clear advantage. This happened in Grischuk, A vs Yandemirov, V Ubeda 2000.   Another dubious try is 7...f3?! 8.Nc3 Be7 9.Bf4±  with a clearly better position.} 8. Bxf4 Ng6 9. Bg3 Bg7 10. Nc3 Nxe5 11. h5 c6 12. Qd2 Bf6 $5 {Preventing O-O-O .} 13. Be2 d6 14. Rd1 Be6 15. Qxd6 Qxd6 16. Rxd6) (5... Bg7 {One of the sidelines.} 6. d4 d6 7. Nxg4 Bxg4 {This is also possible, but doesn't do any harm.} (7... Nc6 8. Bxf4 {8.c3  is met by 8...f5 9.Nf2 Nf6  with initiative.} Bxd4 9. Nc3 Qe7 10. Bd3 Bxg4 11. Qxg4 Nf6 12. Qh3 $5 {We cannot allow Black to castle just yet.} Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 Nd7 {This is also possible.} (13... Qe6 14. Rb1 Qxh3 15. Rxh3 {With equality. White has a very bad pawn structure but the double bishops compensate for that.}) 14. O-O f6 15. Bc4 O-O-O 16. Rab1 {Objectively equal, but White has decent attacking possibilities.}) 8. Qxg4 Bxd4 9. Nc3 Nf6 10. Qf3 {Black is already in some danger, and therefore should be precise.} Qe7 11. Bxf4 Bxc3+ 12. Qxc3 Qxe4+ 13. Be3 Nc6 14. O-O-O Qe5 15. Qb3 O-O-O 16. g3 {White has double bishops which sufficiently compensates for the pawn.}) 6. Nxg4 {We have to be ready to enter the complications. 6.Bc4 d5  is simply not good enough.} Nxe4 (6... d5 $5 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 8. Nc3 Qg7 {This is the idea of 6... d5 .} 9. Qf3 Bg4 {Is not as dangerous anymore, but we still have to be precise.} (9... Nc6 10. Qxf4 Nd4 11. Nxd5 Nxc2+ $2 {Quite reckless!} (11... Bd6 12. Nf6+ Qxf6 {Anything else besides this is immediately lost because of e4-e5 in the next move.} 13. Qxf6 Bg3+ 14. Qf2 Nxc2+ 15. Kd1 Bxf2 16. Kxc2 {Once again, Black has sufficient compensation for the pawn. White should try to develop here with b3 - Bb2 .}) 12. Kd1 Nxa1 13. Nxc7+ Kd8 14. Be2 $1 {We are in no hurry to take the rook as it is not going anywhere.} Rb8 15. Nd5 {White is completely dominating.}) 10. Qxf4 Bd6 11. Bb5+ $1 {Nice intermezzo that allows our king to escape to f1 .} Nc6 12. Qg5 Bg3+ 13. Kf1 Qd4 14. Qe3 Qxe3 15. dxe3 dxe4 16. Nxe4 Be5 17. Nf2 {White likely won't have enough time to consolidate even without queens. Black has decent counterplay for the lost pawn.}) (6... Nc6 7. Nc3 Nd4 $5 {7...d6 8.Nxf6+ Qxf6  will transpose into 5... d6 6.Nxg4 Nf6 .} 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 9. d3 c6 10. Ne2 Ne6 {Peculiar way to protect the pawn.} 11. c3 $5 {11.Qd2  looks worse than it is 11...d5 12.exd5 cxd5 13.Nxf4 Bh6 14.g3 O-O 15.Qf2 Nxf4 16.gxf4 Re8+ 17.Be2∞ .} Rg8 12. Bd2 Rg4 13. Rh3 {Interesting idea. Our plan is to meet Rxh4 with Rf3 .} d6 14. Qc2 Rxh4 15. Rf3 {Who said that a rook is a bad blocker?} Bh6 16. O-O-O Qg6 17. Re1 Bd7 18. d4 O-O-O 19. d5 {With an unclear game ahead with decent chances for both sides.}) 7. d3 Ng3 8. Bxf4 Nxh1 (8... Qe7+ 9. Be2 Nxh1 $2 {Is quite reckless.} (9... Rg8 10. Bxg3 Rxg4 11. Bf2 Rxg2 12. Nc3 {White is about to finish development. The position remains unclear.} Nc6 {Is less precise.} (12... d5 $1 13. Qd2 (13. Nxd5 {Optically, this gives Black a very dangerous initiative, but the position remains unclear.} Qe5 14. c4 Rxf2 $5 15. Kxf2 Bc5+ 16. Kf1 Qd4 {16...Qg3  forces perpetual check 17.d4 Bh3+ 18.Rxh3 Qxh3+ 19.Kg1 Qg3+ .} 17. Ke1 Na6 18. a3 Be6 19. Qb3 O-O-O 20. Qc3) 13... Rxf2 (13... c6 14. Be3 Be6 (14... Bg4 15. Kf1 Rg3 {Taking the light-squared bishop is less valuable for Black. Here is a sample variation to demonstrate why: 15...Rxe2 16.Nxe2 Nd7 17.Re1 O-O-O 18.Nd4 Ne5 19.Bh6 Qf6+ 20.Qf4 Qxh6 21.Qxh6 Bxh6 22.Rxe5 Bc1 23.Re7 Rf8 24.Rg1 h5 25.Rxg4 hxg4 26.Nf5⩲  and White is quite happy thanks to active rook and knight and, of course, our h-pawn.} 16. Bg5 Qe6 17. Kf2 {This is probably what Steinitz had in mind!} Bd6 18. Bf4 Bxe2 19. Kxg3 {19.Bxg3 Bxg3+ 20.Kg2!? Nd7 21.Nxe2 Bc7 22.Rae1 O-O-O 23.Ng3∞  is also possible if you don't want to save your nerves.} Qg4+ 20. Kf2 Qf3+ 21. Kg1 Qg4+ 22. Kf2 {Leads to a perpetual. A very fighting line in which both sides must show great resourcefulness.}) 15. O-O-O Nd7 16. Bg5 {White has many ideas to pose problems for Black as demonstrated on the board.}) 14. Kxf2 Be6 15. Bf3 Nc6 {Another good-looking draw ahead.} 16. Nxd5 Qc5+ 17. d4 Nxd4 18. Nf6+ Ke7 19. Ng8+ Ke8 20. Nf6+ {If Black goes for 20...Kd8  then 21.c3  will be winning.}) 13. Qd2 Qe6 14. Qf4 Bh6 15. Qf3 Rg7 16. Nb5 Qe5 {Black can also try 16...Kd8  which pretty much forces 17.Nd4 Nxd4 18.Bxd4 Rg8 19.Rf1  leading to a more or less equal endgame.} 17. d4 Qf4 18. d5 Nb4 19. Rd1 Nxc2+ 20. Kf1 Kf8 21. d6 {Gives us more than enough initiative. Black's bishop and (and by extension, the rook) will have serious problems getting out.}) (9... Qb4+ 10. Bd2 Qb6 {10...Qxb2?!  is too careless 11.Nc3 Nxh1 12.Rb1 Qa3 13.Rb3 Qd6 14.Ne4 Qd4 15.Nef6+ Kd8 16.Be3 Qa4 17.Qa1  with a strong attack.} 11. Bg5 Bg7 12. Rh3 Nf5 13. Nc3 h6 14. Bd2 {We can quote Tartakower once again.}) (9... Qxe2+ {This gives White a slightly better endgame.} 10. Qxe2+ Nxe2 11. Kxe2 Bg7 12. Nc3 d6 13. Ne3 Be6 14. g4 {With a nice endgame. Our idea should be to push h5- Kd2 -Raf1 .}) 10. Bg5 Qb4+ 11. c3 Qxb2 12. Nf6+ {With a completely winning position. If Black goes 12...Kd8  then 13.Nd5+  seals the deal.} Ke7 13. Nd5+ Kd6 14. Nd2 {I guess Steinitz had something else in mind when he said the king must defend himself.}) 9. Qe2+ Qe7 10. Nf6+ Kd8 11. Bxc7+ Kxc7 12. Nd5+ {And we reach an unclear position where the material balance is breached, although it is always nice to have a queen! This line is further annotated in the 3...g5 chapter.} Kd8 13. Nxe7 Bxe7 14. Qf3 Bxh4+ 15. Kd2 {Material balance is irretrievably violated. Black has two light pieces and a rook for a queen, but its forces are too disunited. It's nearly impossible to cover all the opportunities which both sides have, so let's check few sample lines.} Nc6 $2 (15... Bg5+ 16. Kc3 Nc6 17. Na3 Be7 18. b4 $5 Rg8 {Black should not take the bait with 18...Bxb4+  as after 19.Kb2 Rg8 20.g4  the knight on h1 is dead.} 19. g4 Ng3 {19...Bxb4+  will transpose after 20.Kb2 .} 20. Qxg3 Bf6+ 21. Kd2 Bxa1 22. c3 {With a slight advantage for us thanks to the weakened dark squares in Black's territory combined with passive coordination of pieces.}) (15... Re8 16. Na3 $5 {The c3 -square belongs to the king!} Bg5+ 17. Kc3 Nc6 18. b3 Ne5 {Here's a crazy line: 18...Rb8!? 19.Kb2 b5 20.g4 Re3 21.Qxf7 h6 22.d4 Ng3 23.d5 Ne4 24.dxc6 Bf6+ 25.c3 Rxc3 26.Qg8+ Kc7 27.Nxb5+ Rxb5 28.Bxb5 Rc5+ 29.Ka3 Rxb5 30.Rc1 dxc6 31.Rxc6+ Kxc6 32.Qxc8+ Kd6 33.b4= .} 19. Qd5 Bf6 20. d4 Nc6 21. Kb2 Bxd4+ 22. c3 Be5 23. Nc4 Ng3 24. Bd3 {To summarize this line, we can quote the great Tartakower: 'The winner is the one who makes the penultimate mistake.'}) 16. Qh5 Bf6 17. Qh6 $1 Be5 {17...Bxb2  does not work because of 18.Nc3! Ne7 19.Re1 Bxc3+ 20.Kxc3 Ng3 21.Rxe7 Kxe7 22.Qg5++- .} 18. d4 Bxd4 19. Nc3 Nf2 20. Re1) (3... Be7 {Black plays this move to prepare a very annoying check from h4 while keeping normal development.} 4. Bc4 {4...d6  would transpose to the 3... d6 system .} Bh4+ (4... Nf6 5. e5 Ng4 (5... Nh5 $5 {This move is seldom played, but important regardless.} 6. Nc3 O-O 7. d4 {As usual, we take the center.} d6 8. O-O Bg4 {Black wants to provoke a weakening of the g3 -square.} 9. h3 Be6 10. d5 Bf5 $2 {Is a mistake, allowing:} (10... Bc8 {Only move} 11. e6 fxe6 12. dxe6 c6 {Only move. Otherwise Nd5 - Ng5 is quite dangerous.} 13. Nd4 d5 14. Qxh5 dxc4 15. Nce2 Qe8 16. Nxf4 Qxh5 17. Nxh5 Rxf1+ 18. Kxf1 {With an equal endgame. Black should be able to take the e6 -pawn with correct play, but in the meantime we can pose practical problems.}) 11. Nd4 $1 Bg6 12. e6 {With a great position thanks to piece activity and space.}) (5... Ne4 {The most solid approach.} 6. Nc3 $5 {6.O-O ?!  is a bit slow at the moment in view of 6...d5 7.exd6 Nxd6 8.Bb3 g5∓  And Black is just a pawn up here. 6.Nc3 is interesting, but probably not enough after:} (6. Bd5 {Important to prevent Black from pushing ... d7 - d5 .} Bh4+ (6... Ng5 {6...Nc5 7.d4 Ne6  transposes to the main line.} 7. d4 Ne6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. O-O d6 10. exd6 Bxd6 11. Ne4 c6 (11... Be7 12. Bb3 Nd7 13. Qd3 {Both sides have developed quite harmoniously, the fight is ahead. For example,} b6 {Is not as challenging.} (13... a5 14. a4 b6 15. Bd5 Ba6 16. c4 Rb8 17. Bd2 {With a complicated game ahead. White's idea is to develop through Rae1 and put pressure on the knight on e6 .}) 14. Bd2 Bb7 15. Rae1 {White has sufficient counterplay for material deficit.}) 12. Bxe6 Bxe6 13. Nfg5 Bf5 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. Bxf4 {With equality.}) 7. Kf1 Nc5 {7...Nf2? 8.Qe1±  gives away a piece.} 8. d4 Ne6 9. g3 $1 {A common idea to open files for a rook.} fxg3 $2 (9... Be7 10. Kg2 $5 c6 11. Bxe6 dxe6 12. c4 c5 13. d5 exd5 14. cxd5 Qd7 {Threatening Qh3 +.} 15. h3 O-O 16. gxf4 b5 17. d6 Bd8 18. Nc3 Bb7 19. Rf1 Qc6 20. Kh2 Nd7 21. Be3 {With an extremely double-edged position where Black has a monster bishop on b7 and we have monster pawn chain as compensation.}) 10. hxg3 Be7 {Obviously not 10...Bxg3  as here White just wins a piece 11.Bxe6 dxe6 12.Rh3+-  and the bishop has nowhere to go.} 11. Kg2 {White is clearly better now because Black's king is quite unsafe since O-O is not possible thanks to the open h-file.}) 6... d5 $5 7. Bxd5 Bh4+ 8. Ke2 Nf2 9. Qe1 Bg4 10. g3 fxg3 11. hxg3 Nxh1 12. gxh4 c6 13. Be4 O-O 14. Qxh1 f5 15. exf6 Nd7 {Gives Black too much counterplay.}) 6. d4 d5 {6...Bh4+ 7.Kf1  is fine for White as shown in the previous variation.} 7. exd6 Bh4+ $2 {Is just bad now because of} (7... Qxd6 8. O-O O-O 9. Nc3 {Preparing to recapture the lost pawn on f4 .} Be6 10. Ne4 Qb6 11. Bxe6 Qxe6 (11... fxe6 12. h3 Nh6 {Black does not want to give away the f4 -pawn so easily.  12...Nf6 13.Qd3⩲  Once again, White has a slight advantage because of Black's weak pawn on e6 .} 13. c3 {We are in no rush, as the pawn will be taken eventually anyway.} c5 {The most accurate response according to computer, Black has problems in this position otherwise.} 14. d5 c4+ 15. Kh1 Nd7 16. d6 Bd8 17. Qe2 Rc8 18. g3 {With an unclear game ahead. However, White has some decent attacking resources on the kingside. If Black takes on g3 then the g-file would get open very quickly proving to be dangerous for Black's king and if not then we will just capture Bxf4 - Rad1 and the monster pawn on d6 will be problematic.}) 12. Nfg5 Qd7 13. Rxf4 {Gives White a slight advantage thanks to active pieces combined with some attacking chances on the kingside.}) (7... Bxd6 8. Qe2+ Qe7 9. Qxe7+ Kxe7 {Capturing with the bishop allows Bxf4 , so it makes no sense.} 10. O-O Be6 11. Na3 $5 {We are ready to create some imbalance in the spirit of King's Gambit!} Bxa3 12. Bxe6 fxe6 13. bxa3 Rf8 14. Re1 {And White will retain some initiative in exchange for the pawn. The idea is to first go for h3 and ask the knight where it wants to go, maybe afterward we can follow up with Ng5 .}) 8. g3 $1 fxg3 9. Qe2+ Kf8 10. O-O {Castling with purpose!} Bf6 11. Ne5 {White has a deadly attack.}) 5. Kf1 d5 $6 {This was played by Garry in the Kasparov - Short match in 1993, but modern theory claims it's not a good idea.} (5... d6 6. d4 {transposes to 3...Be7 .} Bg4 {This is not a move we should worry about. We get back our pawn and keep an extremely solid setup. Simply} (6... Be7 7. Bxf4 Nf6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Qe2 {With an interesting game ahead. White has attacking possibilities through the e4 - e5 push. But before that is possible, we should regroup through Bd3 - Re1 .}) 7. Bxf4 Nc6 {Another possible try is 7...Qf6  where we can quietly retreat 8.Be3 Ne7 9.h3 Bxf3 10.Qxf3  and White is slightly better because of the bishop pair.} 8. c3 Qf6 {Black can also try 8...f5 9.exf5 Nge7 10.Nbd2 d5 11.Be2 Bf6 12.h3 Bxf5 13.g4⩲  and we aim to regroup with Kg2-Rf1 .} 9. Be3 O-O-O 10. Nbd2 {Currently White is threatening g2-g3 . If Black defends with 10...h6 , we can go for 11.h3  followed by Kg1 to put our king to safety. We can even go for b4 - b5 afterwards and give it all.}) (5... c6 $5 {The idea is to have ... d7 - d5 resource at hand.} 6. Nc3 Be7 7. d4 d5 8. exd5 Nf6 9. dxc6 O-O {Danish Gambit reversed!} 10. cxb7 Bxb7 11. Bxf4 Nc6 12. a3 Na5 13. Ba2 {Leaves White with two extra pawns, although Black has enough compensation. White's plan is to try and develop quickly even if it means giving away a pawn back for activity.}) (5... Be7 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. e5 Nh5 8. Qe2 $1 {Clever move. The idea is to counter 8...d6  with 9.exd6  and now after 9...cxd6  we have a nice tactic 10.Ng5  which is immediately winning.} O-O 9. g4 $1 fxg3 10. d4 {Leading to an extremely complicated yet explosive position where both sides have many different possibilities. For example: 10...d5 11.Nxd5 Bh3+ 12.Ke1 g2 13.Rg1 Nc6 14.c3 b5 15.Bb3 Bh4+ 16.Nxh4 Qxh4+ 17.Qf2 Qe4+ 18.Qe2=  with a perpetual check.}) 6. Bxd5 Nf6 7. Nxh4 {In their game, Short went 7.Bb3 .} Nxd5 8. exd5 Qxh4 9. Qe2+ Kd8 10. Qf2 Qxf2+ 11. Kxf2 {Leads to a very pleasant endgame for White. The aim is to develop pieces on the queenside via c4 - Nc3 - d3 and target the f4 -pawn, if Black tries for g5 we have h4 ideas. Chapter 6-King's Gambit Accepted - Othe Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) (3... Ne7 {A favorite move of Etienne Bacrot. Black wants to protect the f4 -pawn with the knight from g6 or d5 .} 4. d4 Ng6 $6 {This simply provokes us for a good move!} (4... d5 5. Bd3 dxe4 6. Bxe4 Ng6 7. Qe2 Be7 8. h4 {Trying to use the awkward placement of the black knight.} Nxh4 {Looks almost like a suicide.} (8... h5 {Is another logical response, stopping h4 - h5 ideas. However, the downside of doing this move is that Black has exposed his kingside and will have to be careful before castling short.} 9. Bxg6 fxg6 10. Bxf4 O-O (10... Nc6 11. c3 O-O 12. Bg3 {Gives White a slight advantage. The idea is to develop with Nbd2 followed by O-O . White is practically a pawn up thanks to Black's doubled pawns. Additionally, White should try to occupy the g5 -square with one of the knights, as they say, knights belong on outposts!}) 11. Qc4+ {11.Bg3  allows 11...Bb4+ .} Kh7 12. Qxc7 Nc6 13. Qxd8 Bxd8 14. Be3 Nb4 15. Kd2 Bf5 16. Na3 {It's true that Black has some compensation. But playing without two pawns is not very tempting.}) (8... f5 {Can hardly be recommended.} 9. Bd3 O-O 10. h5 Nh4 11. Bxf4 {White is already quite comfortable. The idea is to develop the knight and go long castling followed by attacking on the kingside.} Re8 {Is another sample variation.} (11... Nxf3+ 12. Qxf3 Re8 13. Nc3 {13.Nd2  transposes to the clickable in the previous line .} Bf6+ 14. Kf1 Nc6 {Once again, being greedy is not a good idea in these situations. 14...Qxd4 15.Rd1  followed by Nd5 gives White an almost winning advantage.} 15. Qd5+ Kf8 16. Qxd8 Rxd8 17. Bxc7 Rd7 18. Bg3 Bxd4 19. Re1 {White enjoys a slight advantage thanks to the lack of development as well as coordination in Black's camp. White should aim to take the h1 -rook out as quickly as possible, perhaps through Rh4 .}) 12. Bc4+ $1 Kf8 13. Nbd2 Bf6 {Black can also try the more direct 13...Nxf3+ 14.Qxf3 Bf6+ 15.Kd1 Qxd4 16.c3 Qb6 17.Kc2  followed by Rae1 . Black's King is left alone and we should aim to play sharp lines to exploit this.} 14. Be5 Nc6 15. O-O-O Nxd4 {Only move. Black is in huge trouble otherwise.} 16. Qf2 Nhxf3 17. Nxf3 Bxe5 18. Nxe5 Rxe5 19. Rxd4 Qg5+ 20. Kb1 Be6 21. h6 {The dust has settled, but not really. White still has massive attacking possibilities!}) (8... O-O 9. h5 Nh4 10. Bxf4 Nxf3+ 11. Qxf3 Qxd4 {Possible but risky.} (11... Nd7 {Better to hurry up with the development.} 12. h6 g6 13. Nc3 Nf6 14. Qd3 Nxe4 {14...Bg4  can be answered with 15.O-O .} 15. Nxe4 Bf5 16. O-O-O Re8 {16...Qd5 17.Rhe1 Rae8 18.c4 Qd7 19.d5  followed by Qd4 ideas, although the position is far from clear.} 17. Rde1 Bf8 18. g4 $5 Bxe4 {18...Bxg4??  allows a beautiful punishment with 19.Bxc7!  with a winning position.} 19. Rxe4 {Gives an equal position. Practically speaking White can try to push a bit with g5 followed by Rhe1 .}) 12. Nc3 Nc6 13. Rd1 {We don't need to castle anyway.} Qc5 14. h6 g6 15. Be3 {15.Nd5 f5 16.Qb3 Rf7 17.Bf3 Be6 18.Qxb7 Re8  does not promise much.} Qe5 16. Nd5 f5 {Black's best move according to the computer. If 16...Bg4  then 17.Qxg4 f5 18.Qf4 Qxf4 19.Bxf4 fxe4 20.Ke2  gives White a slight advantage as the pawn can be recovered at any moment on c7 .} 17. Nxe7+ Nxe7 18. Bd5+ Nxd5 19. Rxd5 Qe4 {19...Qxb2  is quite risky. 20.O-O  And Black should be quite careful here to not get mated.} 20. Qxe4 {With equality as we reach opposite-colored bishops.}) 9. Nc3 {Simple developing move, our idea is to take on f4 next.} Nxf3+ 10. Qxf3 g5 {Looks extremely risky as Black's King wont be able to castle anytime soon. We will just continue developing.} (10... Qxd4 11. Bd2 {Another fun line is 11.Nd5 Bg4 12.Qxf4 Qd1+ 13.Kf2 Qe2+ 14.Kg3 Bd6 15.Bd3 Bxf4+ 16.Bxf4 Qe6 17.Nxc7+ Ke7 18.Rae1= .} Nd7 12. O-O-O c6 {Doesn't change our plan much, if at all} (12... Nf6 13. Bxf4 Qb6 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. Bxd5 Bf6 {15...O-O  is just a blunder because of 16.Qe4 .} 16. Be5 O-O 17. Qf4 Re8 18. Bb3 {With attack. Although Black should be able to defend with correct play.}) 13. Rhe1 Qf6 14. Bxf4 O-O 15. Kb1 {With tremendous attack on the kingside. Black should be very careful here.}) 11. Bd2 Nd7 12. O-O-O Nf6 13. Rde1 Kf8 14. g3 {Is just great for White. Black's king remains in a dangerous position.}) 5. h4 $1 h5 {5...d5  is not as precise anymore because of 6.h5 Ne7 7.e5⩲  followed by Bxf4 - Nc3 .} 6. Nc3 Be7 {6...Bb4  can be met with 7.Bc4± .} 7. Nd5 d6 8. Nxf4 Nxh4 9. Bc4 Bg4 10. Qd3 {Getting rid of the annoying pin.} Bxf3 11. gxf3 Bg5 12. Kf2 {With a slight advantage for White thanks to the central control. White's idea is to develop through Bd2 or Be3 and put one of the rooks on g1 . On the other hand, Black has a serious question to answer - where to castle?}) (3... h6 $5 {A rare move, which was played by many strong grandmasters: Leko, Almasi, McShane, Ganguly, Alekseev and so on. The main idea is to prevent 3...g5 4.h4  line. But now White is in time to grab the center.} 4. d4 g5 5. g3 {Using the fact that Black can't reply with the usual ... g5 - g4 in this case.} fxg3 {5...g4?  allows 6.Ne5± .} 6. hxg3 Bg7 7. Nc3 d6 8. Be3 Bg4 (8... Nf6 9. Qd2 Bg4 (9... d5 $5 {A very inhuman move which fortunately doesn't guarantee Black any safety.} 10. exd5 Nxd5 11. Nxg5 $5 hxg5 {11...Nxc3  can be met with the beautiful 12.Nxf7 Kxf7 13.Qxc3  with ideas of Bc4+ and O-O .} 12. Bxg5 {It's a pity this fancy sacrifice doesn't work. One computer line shows:} Qd6 13. Rxh8+ Bxh8 14. Ne4 Qg6 15. O-O-O Nc6 16. Bd3 f5 17. Re1 Kf8 18. g4 Ncb4 19. Rf1 Nxd3+ 20. Qxd3 Kg7 21. Qf3 f4 22. Rh1 {With an unclear game ahead. Although practically speaking, this will be tougher for Black to play as the king is in potential danger.}) (9... Ng4 10. O-O-O {Ivanchuk chose 10.Bg1  and emerged victorious after a fierce battle Nc6 11.O-O-O O-O 12.Nh2 Nxh2 13.Rxh2 Bg4 14.Re1 Ne7 15.Bg2 Qd7 16.Be3 Ng6 17.Reh1 c6 18.Rxh6 Bxh6 19.Rxh6 Kg7 20.Bxg5 f6 21.Be3  Ivanchuk,V (2678) Leko,P (2663) chess24.com INT 2020 .} (10. Bg1 {10.O-O-O  is correct as we have learned in the relevant section !} Nc6 11. O-O-O O-O 12. Nh2 {With a sound idea to disturb the cementing position of the black knight. The main weakness of the black king position is the h6 -pawn, and our plan is to get to it.} Nxh2 $6 {Leko did not feel the danger. Had Black avoided the exchange with 12...Nf6  it would have been very difficult for White to organize an attack.} 13. Rxh2 Bg4 14. Re1 Ne7 15. Bg2 $6 {The idea here is that White's rook will not be disturbed on the h1 square with Bf3 . But this is superfluous since with a rook on h1 we are always ready to capture on h6 .  15.Bd3! Qd7 16.Be3  and White's attack is not easy to repel. The idea of ​​ e5 and Bg5 works everywhere.} Qd7 16. Be3 Ng6 $2 {Now Black can't defend h6 -pawn with his queen.  16...Rae8! 17.Reh1 Qe6  and due to the unfortunate position of the bishop, White's attack is not easy to develop.} 17. Reh1 c6 18. Rxh6 Bxh6 19. Rxh6 Kg7 20. Bxg5 f6 21. Be3 Rh8 22. b3 Rxh6 23. Bxh6+ Kh7 24. Bf1 $1 {Finally the bishop returns to the perfect square.} Bh3 25. Be2 Re8 26. Kb2 b5 27. Bd3 {Objectively, everything is not so bad for black, but it is very difficult to play under the constant pressure, and black begins to make mistakes.} Qg4 28. Be3 b4 {28...Qxg3 29.e5 dxe5 30.Ne4 Qf3 31.Bg5±} 29. Nd1 Qxg3 30. Qxb4 Bg4 31. e5 $1 {Nice punch!} f5 {31...Bxd1 32.Qb7+ Kh8  32...Kg8 33.exf6+-  33.Qxc6! Rg8 34.Qh1+ Qh4 35.Qxd1+-} 32. Qxd6 Bxd1 33. Bxf5 Rg8 34. Bd2 $1 {A quiet move that exposes all the shortcomings of Black's position. Despite the extra rook, their pieces are constrained, and White, combining attack with pushing pawns on the queenside, should win easily.} Bg4 $2 35. Qe7+ $1 {Apparently Black blundered this.} Rg7 36. Qg5 Qh2 37. Bxg4 Ne7 38. Qh5+ Qxh5 39. Bxh5 Rg2 40. Bb4 Nd5 41. Bf3 Rf2 42. Bxd5 cxd5 43. Bc5 a6 44. b4 Kg6 45. a4 Kf5 46. b5 axb5 47. axb5 Ke6 48. b6 Kd7 49. Kb3 Kc6 50. c4 dxc4+ 51. Kxc4 Rf4 52. e6 Re4 53. e7 Kd7 54. b7 Kc7 55. Bd6+ Kxb7 56. Be5) 10... Nd7 (10... Nc6 11. Bc4 Na5 12. Bd3 Nc6 13. Bg1 {With a pleasant position. Once again, Black will have to think twice before deciding to castle.}) (10... c6 11. Bc4 {11.e5 d5 12.Bd3 Nxe3 13.Qxe3 Be6  and Black is solid.} Nd7 12. Bb3 Nb6 {This allows a tempting piece sacrifice:} (12... Qe7 13. Rde1 Nxe3 14. Rxe3 Nb6 15. a4 a5 {After a5 has been played, Black never wants to castle queenside.} 16. Ree1 O-O 17. Nd1 {With such a dominant center, White will have time for all necessary maneuvers.}) 13. Nxg5 hxg5 14. Bxg5 f6 15. Rxh8+ Bxh8 16. Bf4 {And we reach a complicated position where once again White is willing to give up a piece for dynamic compensation. White should try to aim for a4 - a5 and also Rh1 ideas. One sample line is:} Qe7 17. a4 Bg7 18. a5 Nd7 19. Qe2 Nf8 20. a6 {With very nice compensation. Some forced lines, as usual, lead to a draw:} b6 21. Ba4 b5 22. Nxb5 cxb5 23. Qxb5+ Bd7 24. Qh5+ Kd8 {24...Qf7  is not as delightful as after 25.Bxd7+ Nxd7 26.Qxg4  White enjoys a small edge.} 25. Qa5+ {Leading to a perpetual.}) 11. e5 {Our general concept here is to open the center as soon as possible} Nb6 {11...dxe5?? 12.dxe5  is winning for White.} 12. Bd3 d5 13. Bxg5 $5 hxg5 14. Rxh8+ Bxh8 15. Rh1 Bg7 16. Rh7 Kf8 17. Nxg5 Qe7 18. Qf4 {With massive compensation for the material deficit. The idea here should be to put pressure on the f7 -pawn by all means necessary!}) 10. Be2 Nc6 11. O-O-O Qe7 {This is another logical setup.} 12. Rde1 {12.Nd5  leads to massive exchanges 12...Nxd5 13.exd5 Qe4 14.Nxg5 hxg5 15.Rxh8+ Bxh8 16.dxc6 Bxe2 17.Re1 O-O-O 18.cxb7+ Kxb7 19.Qxe2=  with an equal endgame .} O-O-O 13. d5 Bxf3 (13... Nxe4 14. Nd4 $1 Bxe2 {Is not very good because of} (14... Nxd2 15. Bxg4+ Kb8 16. Nxc6+ bxc6 17. Bxa7+ Kxa7 18. Rxe7 {Looks very promising for White. But unfortunately with correct play Black will hold.} Bxc3 19. bxc3 Nc4 20. Rxc7+ Kb8 21. Rxc6 Ne5 22. Rb6+ Kc7 23. Rb4 {With an equal endgame. Black has enough compensation, specially because of the knight.}) 15. Nxc6 bxc6 16. Qxe2 {Black's king is quite unsafe. The threat of Qa6 + is not easy to deal with.}) 14. Bxf3 Ne5 15. Be2 {Can be quite dangerous for Black.}) 9. Be2 Nc6 10. Qd2 Nge7 11. O-O-O Qd7 {Black prepares to castle long. We can use the unguarded b5 -square to our advantage.} (11... a6 {Interesting move. Black doesn't want White to play Bb5 in the future and prepares for a safe escape for the king on the queenside.} 12. Nxg5 $5 {Very nice resource! We just wont give Black enough time to develop.} Bxe2 13. Nxf7 $5 Kxf7 14. Qxe2 Qd7 15. Rdf1+ Kg8 16. Qc4+ d5 {Of course not 16...Kh7??  because of 17.Rxh6+  and I will let you figure out the rest.} 17. exd5 Na5 18. Qe2 {Needless to say the computer defends this, but from practical point of view White has very fine compensation.}) 12. d5 Ne5 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. Bd4 Ng6 {Best move. If Black plays 14...O-O-O  then 15.Bxe5 dxe5 16.Rhf1  is strong with pressure on the f-file. After something like 16...Bxe2 17.Qxe2 Rhf8 18.Rf6  the f- and h-pawns are both weak, White is clearly pushing.} 15. Bb5 c6 16. Be2 {Now the black king doesn't have a safe harbour.}) (3... Nf6 {One of the modern ways to fight the King's Gambit and recommended by Jan Gustafsson in his Lifetime Repertoire here on Chessable. Black doesn't want to mess with all sorts of ... g7 - g5 ideas and plays for development instead.} 4. e5 {Probably forced, as 4.Nc3  allows Black to illustrate one of the ideas behind the 3rd move, by playing 4...d5  with an easy game.} Nd5 $6 {Pours water into our mill, on d5 the knight is vulnerable.} (4... Ne4 {A reasonable move for those aiming for a solid game.} 5. d3 {Immediately hitting the knight.} Ng5 {5...Nc5 6.d4  would costs Black additional time.} 6. Bxf4 {Having won back our sacrificed pawn, including a small lead in development, we can already be satisfied with the outcome of the opening. Having said that,} Ne6 7. Be3 c5 $5 {Is a worthy try to prevent d3 - d4 , and keeps White's advantage to a minimum.} 8. Nc3 {8.d4?! cxd4 9.Nxd4 Qa5+  is followed by capture on e5 .} Nc6 9. g3 {Preparing to develop the bishop on the long diagonal, where it is much more effective than on e2 .} d5 10. exd6 Bxd6 11. Bg2 O-O 12. O-O {This looks more like a position from the King's Indian Defence and is approximately equal. White can continue with moves like Qd2 , Rae1 and perhaps even Nh4 - f5 .}) (4... Nh5 {Famously, a knight on the rim is dim... or is it? From h5 the knight simply keeps the pawn on f4 alive and challenges White to prove compensation.} 5. d4 {The move I recommend, we take the centre and support our e5 -pawn. Magnus Carlsen tried 5.Qe2  but was slightly worse after 5...Be7 6.d4 O-O 7.Nc3 d6  preparing ... Bc8 - g4 as seen in Carlsen, M. - Ding Liren, Chess24.com rapid 2020 . Ding went on to win that game in straightforward fashion.} Be7 {Not the most accurate move.} (5... d5 {Krishnan Sasikiran used to play like this in the 2000s.} 6. c4 $1 {We have to strike swiftly.} Nc6 $2 {Both Sasikiran and Mikhalevski tried this move but it backfired.} (6... Bg4 {Not a very effective pin here as Black will lose grip on the centre.} 7. cxd5 Qxd5 8. Nc3 Bb4 9. Be2 {White holds a large advantage as the king will seek refuge on the kingside next, which will force the queen on d5 to move again.}) (6... Be6 7. Nc3 {Adding more fuel to the fire that is the d5 -square. 7.cxd5 Bxd5 8.Nc3  also deserves attention.} dxc4 8. d5 {I like the coordination.} Bg4 9. Bxc4 Be7 10. Qa4+ {10.O-O!?  isn't bad either.} Qd7 (10... c6 11. Be2 {Black now has to find a bunch of strong moves to stay in the game.} b5 12. Qe4 b4 13. Nb5 $5 {The most interesting option we have at our disposal.} f5 {The knight is untouchable, as 13...cxb5 14.d6! Bh4+ 15.g3!  costs Black material.} 14. Qd4 cxb5 15. Bxb5+ Kf7 16. d6 Bh4+ 17. Kf1 {The alternative 17.Nxh4 Qxh4+ 18.Qf2 Qxf2+ 19.Kxf2  is an interesting endgame, but it objectively shouldn't promise White much. For instance 19...Rc8 20.Re1 Ke6 21.Ba4 Na6  preparing Nc5 , basically forcing the repetition, as neither side can sidestep from the moves 22.Bb3+ Kd7 23.Ba4+  with a draw.} Bxf3 18. gxf3 g6 19. Bxf4 Nxf4 20. Qxf4 {A rather long and forced line leads to this position, which is equal according to engines, but, one can't deny it, it looks really tasty!}) 11. Qb3 {White's idea is that this leaves the queen misplaced on d7 and b7 vulnerable.}) (6... c6 {This peaceful move won't help equalizing, though it should be said that safeguarding the d5 -pawn certainly is very logical.} 7. Nc3 g5 8. Ng1 $5 {This creative resource immediately challenges the knight on h5 !} g4 9. Nh3 {White should be doing well and is about to recapture the pawn on f4 .}) (6... g5 7. g4 $1 {It turns out that capturing the brave pawn is not good at all, 7...fxg3 8.Nxg5!  and Black's position is collapsing. Mind-boggling complications are ahead. Just enjoy the chaos!} Bxg4 (7... dxc4 $5 {It is quite remarkable that this move isn't that bad.} 8. Bxc4 {8.gxh5 g4!  is actually better for Black, as we can't properly hold on to the extra piece.} Bxg4 (8... Ng7 {Not a pretty square for the knight, but potentially it is not forced to stay there and may jump to e6 in the future.} 9. Nc3 {Sacrificing yet another pawn, which should no longer come as any surprise!} Bxg4 10. h3 {We first lure the bishop to h5 . Why this is useful is seen in the line 10.h4? Nc6 11.Nd5 Be7 12.hxg5 Ne6  where White's centre is collapsing. The same line would be quite different with the bishop on h5 though, as that would enable our h1 -rook to simply capture it right now!} Bxf3 {Playable for Black as well, though it's hard to part with the strong bishop.} (10... Bh5 {It makes sense to keep the powerful bishop alive.} 11. h4 g4 $5 (11... Nc6 12. Nd5 Be7 13. hxg5 Bg4 {As mentioned earlier, the bishop being on h5 Black can't get ... Ng7 - e6 in now and needs to lose additional time.} 14. Bxf4 Qd7 15. Ne3 O-O-O 16. Nxg4 Qxg4 17. Rh4 Qd7 {This is also possible.} (17... Qg2 {By taking away some squares from the king, Black boldly threatens to give a check on b4 .} 18. Qe2 {We are obliged to enter the endgame.} Qxe2+ 19. Kxe2 Nf5 {The dormant knight comes alive with a show of force, forking the rook on h4 and pawn on d4 .} 20. Rg4 Ncxd4+ 21. Nxd4 Nxd4+ 22. Kf2 Ne6 {Our pair of bishops and pawns on e5 and g5 , which gives Black's position a somewhat cramped feel, give sufficient compensation for the pawn, but not more than that.} 23. Kf3 b5 {Our great contemporaries played the following game: 23...Rhg8 24.Bxe6+ fxe6 25.Rh1 Rdf8 26.Ke4 Rf7 27.Rh6 Kd7 28.Be3 Rg6 29.Rxg6 hxg6 30.Rh4 Rg7 31.Rh8 a6 32.Rb8 b6 33.Rh8 Kc6 34.a4 Kd7 35.Bd2 Bd8 36.Be3 Be7 37.Bc1 Bc5 38.Bf4 Kc6 39.Rb8  1/2 Stockfish - LCZero, INT 2021 .} 24. Bxe6+ fxe6 25. Rh1 {This will also likely end in a draw, e6 and h7 are weaknesses that are easy to probe.}) 18. d5 Nf5 19. Rh2 Bb4+ 20. Kf1 {Leading to irrational play.}) 12. Nd5 {Initiating yet another mess! 12.Ng5!?  also deserves attention.} Be7 13. Nxf4 gxf3 14. Nxh5 Bxh4+ 15. Kf1 Nxh5 16. Bxf7+ {Pulling the break.} Kxf7 17. Qxf3+ Ke7 {Black's only move! Instead 17...Ke6 18.Qg4+ Kd5 19.Rxh4  is too dangerous. Indeed it's rarely a good idea to centralize the king in the middlegame!} 18. Qxh5 Qxd4 (18... Rf8+ 19. Ke2 Qxd4 20. Qxh4+ Qxh4 21. Rxh4 Rf7 {Only Black can be better here, but in reality, the position is just drawn with a few precise moves.} 22. Rh6 Nc6 23. Bg5+ Kf8 24. e6 Re8 25. Ke3 Rf5 26. Rg1 {White has enough activity.}) 19. Bg5+ Bxg5 20. Qxg5+ Kd7 {20...Ke8 21.Qh5+  repeats.} 21. Qf5+ Kc6 22. Qf6+ Kb5 23. Qxh8 {Forces Black to give a perpetual. 23...Qf4+  White's king can't escape.}) 11. Qxf3 Qxd4 {The ambitious continuation.} (11... Nc6 12. Bb5 Bb4 {This looks bad because of White's next move, but Black has enough resources.} 13. d5 a6 $1 14. dxc6 {14.Ba4 b5!  unpins the knight and simply wins.} axb5 15. cxb7 Rb8 16. Bd2 {Intending to castle queenside in order to create pressure along the d-file.} Bxc3 17. Bxc3 O-O 18. h4 $1 {It's very important to prevent Black from stabilizing the position, otherwise, the extra pawn may start counting.} Qd7 19. hxg5 Rfd8 20. Qxf4 Qd3 21. Rf1 Qg6 22. Qg4 {There are usually no winners in such a shootout. This will likely end in a draw as well.}) 12. Qxb7 Qxc4 13. Qxa8 {Unfortunately if Black now finds 13...Bb4!  this sets up a perpetual check. 14.Qxb8+ Ke7 15.Qxh8 Bxc3+ 16.bxc3 Qxc3+  and our king won't be able to escape the checks.}) 9. Rg1 Bxf3 (9... Qd7 {Black's most ambitious try, leading to typical King's Gambit type complications.} 10. Rxg4 $1 Qxg4 11. Qb3 {This opening is all about development and the initiative - material is of secondary importance! A sample line now is 11...Nc6 12.Bxf7+ Ke7 13.Bd2 Rd8 14.Na3 Ng7 15.Bc4 Nxd4 16.Bb4+ Kd7 17.O-O-O Bxb4 18.Nxd4 a5 19.h3 Qg2 20.Bd5 Qg3 21.Bf3  with very unclear play. Clearly, the player who has mastered the art of the initiative has a practical edge here.}) 10. Qxf3 Qxd4 11. Bxf7+ Kxf7 12. Qxh5+ Ke6 {At first glance, this looks very scary for Black.} 13. Qg4+ Kf7 {One way or another, repetition seems to be incoming.}) (7... Ng7 8. Nc3 {Not just a simple developing move, but one that adds additional pressure to the important d5 -square.} dxc4 (8... c6 {Giving support to the d5 -pawn.} 9. h4 {We see that with every move White tries to destabilize Black's position. Having sacrificed a pawn there must always be a sense of urgency in our play. Black can't be allowed to simply finish development and enjoy the extra pawn!} Bxg4 10. Be2 Bb4 (10... dxc4 {Perhaps not a very logical follow-up, but logic these days is often defined in terms of something either working or not.} 11. Ne4 Nd7 12. Nfxg5 Be6 13. Bxf4 {Black is a pawn up but not yet fully mobilized. An illustration of how play may continue is 13...Qb6 14.O-O O-O-O 15.Kh2 Nf5 16.Bh5  with another round of crazy complications.}) 11. Bd2 {Setting a nice trap!} Nf5 {A strong looking knight move. The threat is to jump to g3 which looks hard to face.} 12. Nxg5 $1 {This is the trap, Black is suddenly busted.} Bxe2 13. Qxe2 Ng3 14. Qg4 $1 Nxh1 15. Nxf7 $1 {Easy to miss from afar.} Kxf7 16. e6+ Ke7 {Loses beautifully. 16...Kf6 17.Bxf4  isn't saving Black either.} 17. Nxd5+ $1 cxd5 18. Bxb4+ Kf6 {18...Ke8 19.Qh5#  is mate!} 19. Kd2 $1 {Bringing the a1 -rook into the game. White doesn't have many pieces left, but it is enough to mate the lone black king!}) 9. Bxc4 Bxg4 {Transposes to the 7...dxc4 move order.}) 8. Rg1 {Making use of the open g-file.} Bf5 {8...Bxf3 9.Qxf3  leaves both d5 and h5 hanging.} 9. Rxg5 Bg6 10. cxd5 Be7 {10...Qxd5 11.Nc3  is also great for White.} 11. Rg2 Qxd5 12. Nc3 {We get a position White can be happy about. Our center is still intact and our pieces more harmonious. The knight on h5 makes an especially sad impression.}) 7. cxd5 Qxd5 8. Nc3 Bb4 9. Be2 {And after O-O on the next move, White is nearly winning.}) (5... g6 {A rare but curious move. Black wants to outwit us and then decide whether he wants to push the d-pawn to d6 or d5 .} 6. Qe2 Bb4+ (6... Be7 7. g4 $1 {This typical pawn thrust again.} fxg3 {Having opened the diagonal for our bishop, we simply continue development.} 8. Nc3 gxh2 {Very greedy, but it needs to be addressed!} (8... d5 9. exd6 {Forced, as with the knight being on c3 , blocking the pawn on c2 , White could not add pressure on d5 by playing c2 - c4 .} Qxd6 10. Bh6 Bg4 {Now that kingside castling is out of the window, Black will try to castle on the other side of the board. In order to do so, both the bishop and knight need to get out of the way.} 11. O-O-O Nd7 12. h3 Nf4 {Starting a tactical skirmish.} (12... Bxf3 13. Qxf3 O-O-O 14. Bg2 c6 15. Rhe1 Bf6 {This allows us to push ahead!} (15... Rhe8 16. Qxf7 {Black is consolidated, so we need to collect material back.} Qf6 17. Qxh7 g5 18. Ne4 {In the endgame White should show a certain neatness. For instance 18...Qh8! 19.Qxh8 Rxh8 20.Bxg5 Bxg5+ 21.Nxg5 Nf4 22.Bf3 Rdg8 23.Re4 Nd5 24.Nf7 Rh7 25.Nd6+ Kc7 26.Nf5 N7f6 27.Rh4 Rxh4 28.Nxh4 Nf4 29.Kd2 Nxh3 30.Rf1  and White should hold without much difficulty.}) 16. d5 Bxc3 {16...Qc7 17.h4!?  with an unclear game.} 17. Qxc3 c5 18. Bg5 f6 {18...Rde8 19.Be7!  makes use of the fact that h8 will be hanging if Black takes on e7 .} 19. Re6 Qb8 20. d6 $1 fxg5 21. Re5 b6 22. Re7 {White has fantastic compensation as Black is almost paralyzed.}) 13. Qe3 g2 14. Bxf4 gxh1=Q 15. Bxd6 O-O {Obviously!} 16. hxg4 Bxd6 17. Bb5 Rae8 18. Ne5 Qh2 19. Bxd7 Bxe5 20. Nd5 c6 21. Bxe8 cxd5 22. Bxf7+ Rxf7 23. dxe5 Qf4 {The endgame is approximately equal.}) 9. Bh6 {9.Nd5!?  is interesting too.} Ng3 (9... d5 10. exd6 Qxd6 11. Ne4 {For practical purposes this is more than enough, yet the engine of course always persists and also manages to hold Black's position together here.} Qa6 12. Qxh2 Qa5+ 13. Nc3 {13.c3 Bf5  followed by Nb8 - d7 allows Black to catch up in development.} Nc6 14. d5 Bd6 15. Qg2 Ba3 $1 16. O-O-O Qxc3 17. bxa3 Bf5 18. Rxh5 $1 {The position, as so often, is a complete mess.}) 10. Qxh2 Nxh1 11. Qxh1 {A picture of harmony − Black will never be able to catch up in development and is likely to succumb somewhere over the next 10 moves.}) 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. g4 {A nice, and typical, idea to open up our dark-squared bishop.} fxg3 9. Bh6 {Don't let Black castle too quickly! Even at the cost of a few pawns.} Ne7 10. O-O-O Bxc3 11. d5 {That's the spirit of the King's Gambit!} c6 {Trying to hold on to the extra piece is a bad idea. That is shown in the variation 11...Ba5 12.d6! Nf5 13.Bg5  where the queen is cornered on d8 .} 12. bxc3 Qa5 {It looks like Black may actually survive this way.} 13. d6 Nf5 14. Qd2 g2 15. Bxg2 Nxh6 16. Qxh6 b6 $1 {With double-edged play. The greedy 16...Qxc3  backfires after 17.Rd3 Qa5 18.Ng5  with a winning attack.}) (5... d6 6. Qe2 d5 {Leads to quite a normal (comparing to other lines) position.} (6... Be7 $4 {It's a trap!} 7. exd6 Qxd6 8. Qb5+ {Winning a piece in rather surprising fashion.}) (6... dxe5 7. Nxe5 {A curious forced line begins here.} Qh4+ {Black needs to accept the challenge, there is simply no viable alternative.} 8. g3 {8.Kd1 Be7  doesn't lead anywhere. Our king is quite awkward on d1 .} Nxg3 9. hxg3 Qxh1 10. Bxf4 {This is not the time to get greedy. After 10.Ng6+ Be6 11.Nxh8  White is a piece up, but will soon get overrun after 11...Nc6  followed by castles queenside. The king on e1 will be feeling the heat soon.} Bb4+ {The idea is to deny the b1 -knight the square c3 . Yet, it won't clarify things even a little.} (10... Bd6 $5 11. Nc3 {Here too, 11.Ng6+ Be6 12.Nxh8 Bxf4 13.gxf4 Nc6  followed by O-O-O is plain bad for White.} O-O 12. O-O-O Bh3 {Black can bail out with 12...Bxe5 13.dxe5 Bh3  when White has nothing better but to play 14.Ne4 Nc6 15.Nf6+! gxf6 16.Qh5 Bg2 17.Qg4+ Kh8 18.Qf5  and Black has nothing better but to repeat with 18...Kg8 19.Qg4+  with a draw.} 13. Nxf7 Bxf4+ 14. gxf4 Bxf1 {Not the most precise continuation.} (14... Nd7 {Very precise.} 15. Qc4 {We have to temper our ambitions. This move aims for a slightly worse endgame.} Rxf7 16. Qxf7+ Kxf7 17. Bc4+ Be6 18. Bxe6+ Kxe6 19. Rxh1 Nf6 {This is where Jan Gustafsson ends his line, stating Black is better.} 20. Kd2 {One of the main rules in the endgame: bringing the king into action.} h5 21. Ke3 {My opinion though is that it's Black who's trying to squeeze something out of this endgame, but it should also be closer to a draw.}) 15. Rxf1 Qc6 {This looks solid, but now Black has to find a sequence of only moves not to lose immediately.} (15... Qh4 16. Ng5 Nc6 17. Qe6+ Kh8 18. Nf7+ Rxf7 19. Qxf7 {The position is dynamically equal, where the active queen compensates for the slightly weakened structure.}) 16. Ne5 Qe6 17. Ne4 Nd7 18. Ng5 Qd5 19. Qd3 Nf6 20. c4 Qg2 21. Nd7 g6 22. Nxf8 Rxf8 23. Ne6 Re8 24. f5 {Even in the final position White still has some initiative.}) 11. c3 Be7 12. Nd2 g5 13. Nxf7 $1 Kxf7 14. Be5 Rf8 15. O-O-O {Despite being a rook down, with the bishop coming to g2 , and, potentially, d5 , White has all the fun.}) 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Bd2 Bf5 9. O-O-O Qd7 10. Qf2 {White has decent play for the pawn.}) 6. Be2 Bh4+ {This check may look attractive, to force the king to move.} (6... d6 7. O-O {Not only bringing the king to safety but preparing to embarrass the knight on h5 by playing Nf3 - e1 next.} Bg4 8. Nc3 O-O 9. Ne1 {Even with the bishop on g4 , this is still the best way to go.} Bxe2 10. Qxe2 g6 11. Bxf4 {Restoring the material equilibrium. The space advantage makes it slightly better for White.} Nc6 12. Rd1 {Black faces serious pressure.}) 7. g3 $1 fxg3 8. hxg3 Bxg3+ 9. Kf1 {Black's pieces on the kingside are in trouble.} g6 10. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 11. Bg5 $1 {Black loses the queen in broad daylight!}) 5. Nc3 Nxc3 (5... c6 {Black's setup looks very artificial.} 6. Nxd5 cxd5 7. d4 {It's already obvious White is doing more than OK, but it's never a bad idea to check few sample lines:} g5 {7...d6 8.Bxf4  gives White a large advantage in development.} 8. h4 g4 9. Ng5 {In keeping with the spirit of King's Gambit, the knight is headed for f7 !} (9. Ng1 {Black is more or less forced to play f3 .} f3 {Now a sample line is} 10. gxf3 d6 11. e6 $5 {11.exd6 Bxd6 12.Qe2+ Be6 13.Bg5  is possible too.} Bxe6 12. fxg4 h6 {Trying to prevent g4 - g5 , is well met by White's next move.} (12... Nc6 13. c3 {Not only giving comfort to the d4 -pawn but essentially making sure the c6 -knight is a very ineffective piece; it has no good squares to jump to.} Be7 14. g5 h6 15. Nf3 {Giving White a slightly better position. The better pawn structure counts for something!}) 13. Nh3 $5 Qxh4+ (13... Be7 14. g5 Nc6 15. c3 {White secures a positional edge.} Qd7 16. gxh6 O-O-O {Or 16...Bxh4+ 17.Nf2  when White has a big advantage as 17...Bxf2+ 18.Kxf2  doesn't lead anywhere, Black has no way to make use of the slightly exposed king on f2 .} 17. Be3 Rde8 18. Kd2 $1 {The pawn structure make White's position somewhat preferable. Note that our king is perfectly safe on d2 .}) 14. Nf2 Qe7 15. Be3 {White has excellent play for the pawn. The queen will go to d2 / f3 and, as usual, we will castle queenside.}) 9... h6 10. Nxf7 {Of course!} Kxf7 11. Qxg4 d6 12. Qh5+ Kg8 13. Bxf4 {A typical position for the opening we are discussing. Black will have a tough time facing the long-term initiative that White has. White intends to castle queenside and bring the a1 -rook into the game via d1 - d3 to g3 . It is very hard for Black to counter that plan.}) 6. dxc3 {Perhaps a puzzling decision for players who are starting out learning the game. Don't we always capture towards the centre to increase our influence in it? Here development is more important, and 6.bxc3 d6  would give Black an easy game. By capturing with the d-pawn, White is ready to capture on f4 on the next game.} d5 7. Bxf4 Be7 8. Qd2 {White has a clear advantage and will prepare queenside castling next. Our king is very safe on c1 , because of the 4 pawns covering it, and the rook appearing on d1 will add additional pressure on the d5 -pawn. Another typical idea will be to launch our h-pawn forward, creating discomfort on the kingside where it is likely Black's king will end up. Chapter 9-King's Gambit Accepted - 3... Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) 4. Bc4 (4. d4 {This seems to be a risky choice for White.} g5 5. g3 Bg7 6. Nc3 {6.gxf4 g4 7.Rg1 Kf8∓  White is forced to sacrifice a piece.} Nc6 $1 7. d5 Ne5 {White can win the pawn back.} 8. gxf4 gxf4 9. Bxf4 Bg4 {But now White's dark squares will be too weak.} 10. Be2 Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Qf6 12. Bxe5 Qxe5 {Since White is nearly forced to castle queenside, the g7 -bishop will become an absolute beast. Chapter 7-King's Gambit Accepted - 3... Chessable 2023 [mdb.sok@gmail.com]}) 4... h6 {Another flexible useful move. 4...g5  is a little bit premature because of 5.h4  with pressure on both the g- and f-pawns.} (4... g5 {Leads to drawish positions with minor chances for White.} 5. h4 gxh4 (5... g4 $2 {Allows White to regain his pawn:} 6. Ng5 Nh6 7. d4 Nc6 8. Bxf4 Bg7 9. Be3 {White is nearly winning already with such a brilliant development.}) (5... h6 6. hxg5 Bg7 7. gxh6 Rxh6 8. Rxh6 Bxh6 {Surprisingly, despite the material is equal and White's stronger center, Black is right in time with his response.} 9. Nc3 Nc6 10. Qe2 Bg4 11. Qf2 Bg7 {Black is in time to prevent d4 and create counterplay.} 12. Bb3 $5 {Preparing Ne2 and d4 as an extra option, but after the precise} (12. d3 Bxf3 13. gxf3 {13.Qxf3 Bxc3+! 14.bxc3 Ne5 15.Qxf4 Nxc4 16.dxc4  leads to a highly compromised pawn structure, although the position is still equal because of White's extra pawn.} Bd4 14. Qh2 {This is more logical, to cover the h4 -square.  14.Qg2!?  White can even win an exchange, but, alas, this is only enough for a draw. 14...Qh4+ 15.Ke2 Nf6  15...Nge7 16.Bd2 Kd7 17.Rh1 Rg8 18.Qf1 Qf6  is equal too.  16.Be3!  is a beautiful idea to trap the black queen, but sadly, it's not enough: 16...fxe3 17.Rh1 Qf2+  17...Qf4 18.Rh8+ Ke7 19.Rxa8 Ne5 20.Nd5+ Nxd5 21.Bxd5 c6 22.Bb3 Ng6 23.c3 Qh4 24.Kd1 Nf4 25.Qg8 Qh1+ 26.Kc2 Qh2+ 27.Kb1 Qh1+=  18.Qxf2 exf2 19.Rh8+ Kd7 20.Rxa8 Nh5!  White can't avoid a very deep repetition after 21.Rg8 Ne5 22.Nd1 Nf4+ 23.Kf1 Nxf3 24.Nxf2 Nh2+ 25.Ke1 Nf3+=  A lovely book draw.} Ne5 15. Qxf4 Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Nxc4 17. dxc4 {Our pawns on the c-file are too weak to play for an advantage.} Ne7 18. c5 Ng6 19. Qg5 {White can't avoid the trade of queens to due the constant threat of ... Qh4 +.} dxc5 20. Qxd8+ Rxd8 21. Be3 Ne5 22. Ke2 Nc4 23. Rh1 {The pawn structure is similar to the Berlin endgame. Black basically did everything right.}) 12... a5 $1 {The knight is tied to the c3 -square (to prevent ... a4 , trapping our bishop).} 13. d3 {13.a3  or 13.a4  would leave the b3 -bishop vulnerable to ... Nd4 - Nxb3 ideas.} Bxc3+ 14. bxc3 Qf6 15. Bd2 Bxf3 16. gxf3 a4 17. Bd5 Nge7 {White can't maintain the bishop pair.} 18. O-O-O {The position is playable. There are only pawns on the queenside left, because even the pawn(s) on e4 and on the f-file could be said to be part of the same pawn island in this pawn structure. This makes it difficult for White to create any realistic winning chances.}) 6. d4 {Black is two pawns up but his position is in ruins: he didn't develop a single piece yet and White can regain the f4 - and h4 -pawns at any desirable moment.}) (4... Be6 {The evaulation does not go beyond equality, but the move is obviously not the most principled.} 5. Qe2 {5.Bxe6 fxe6 6.d4  is the option I recommend instead.} (5. Bxe6 fxe6 6. d4 e5 (6... Qf6 7. Nc3 {With the idea to attack the c7 -pawn, which is a common idea when the queen is not on d8 .} Nd7 8. Nb5 O-O-O {Black is forced to challenge White to a pawn race: who will be able to find inroads to the enemy king's position first?} 9. Nxa7+ Kb8 10. Nb5 {White is likely the favorite in a pawn race.} g5 11. O-O c6 12. Nc3 g4 13. Ne1 {It's not so easy for Black to open our king since it's defended by our rook and knight.} e5 14. a4 Qg6 15. a5 {The e4 -pawn is of course weak, but we don't care and play for an attack. White has the initiative.}) 7. dxe5 Nc6 8. e6 {This move will spice the game up!} (8. Bxf4 dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Rxd8 10. Bxe5 Nxe5 11. Nxe5 {The e4 -pawn is too weak.} Nf6 12. Nd2 Bb4 {This is probably enough for a draw.} 13. c3 Bd6 14. Nec4 Bf4 15. g3 {Black will eventually regain the e4 -pawn and equalize.}) 8... Qf6 9. Nc3 O-O-O 10. O-O {We've reached a position with opposite-side castling. However, it's not at all easy to get to the black king. Both sides must play energetically.} g5 11. Nd5 Qg6 12. b4 Bg7 13. b5 Ne5 14. b6 cxb6 15. Nd4 {In my opinion, this is a risky approach, but it makes more sense than going for a more of less obvious draw. It's up to you, but my personal preference is obviously the b4 - b5 - b6 idea.}) 5... g5 6. Bxe6 fxe6 7. h4 Nc6 8. hxg5 Bg7 9. d4 Bxd4 {9...Nxd4 10.Nxd4 Bxd4 11.Bxf4 Ne7 12.Nd2→} 10. Qc4 d5 11. exd5 exd5 12. Qe2+ Qe7 13. Nc3 Bxc3+ 14. bxc3 Qxe2+ 15. Kxe2 Nge7 16. Bxf4) 5. h4 {We will have to fight against the g7 - g5 plan, otherwise we will have to face the consequences.} Nf6 (5... Be7 6. d4 Bg4 7. Bxf4 {Losing the h4 -pawn is the least of our concerns.} Bxh4+ 8. g3 Bf6 9. Qd3 {A nice move; now we harmoniously finish development with Nbd2 and O-O-O .} Bxf3 (9... Nc6 10. Nbd2 Qd7 11. O-O-O O-O-O 12. d5 {A strong attacking idea:} Ne5 13. Nxe5 dxe5 14. Be3 Bxd1 {We don't even need to recapture the bishop.} 15. Qa3 {With a very strong attack. The computer manages to defend this position with Black, but our opponents are extremely unlikely to!}) 10. Qxf3 Bxd4 $4 11. Be5 {Hopefully one of your opponents will fall into this trap!}) (5... Be6 {With h4 &amp; h6 inclusion this move becomes more sensible.} 6. Qe2 {Kingside castling isn't an option anymore, so White prepares castling queenside.} Nf6 (6... Nd7 {A very flexible move by Black; he wants to keep the option of castling queenside open.  Note that 6...Bxc4  will lead to the same position as the main line here: 7.Qxc4 Nf6 8.Nc3 Nbd7 9.d4 Nh5  and we reach the same position as here after move  9.} 7. d4 Bxc4 8. Qxc4 Ngf6 9. Nc3 Nh5 10. Bd2 c6 11. O-O-O Be7 12. Ne2 {The e4 -pawn will be weak, but White has a simple attacking plan: regain the f4 -pawn with Nxf4 and open files on the kingside with g2 - g4 - g5 .}) (6... Nc6 7. Nc3 Bg4 {Pretentious but sensible attempt: ... Nd4 comes with tempo and White has no d2 - d4 .} 8. Qf2 Nf6 9. d4 Qe7 {It's hard to hang on to the e4 -pawn, so we simply sacrifice it and play for development.} 10. O-O Bxf3 11. Nd5 $5 {11.Qxf3  loses the d4 -pawn while 11.gxf3  weakens our kingside too much.} Nxd5 12. exd5 Na5 (12... Be4 13. dxc6 bxc6 14. Bxf4 f5 {This bishop is called to shield Black from trouble.} 15. d5 $1 {White complicates the position even more. He may not allow Black to set up a blockade with ... d5 .} cxd5 (15... g5 16. Bd2 Bg7 17. Rae1 {I have no idea what's going on here, but it must be fun to play this position!} O-O 18. dxc6+ Kh7 19. hxg5 hxg5 20. g4 {With a completely crazy position. I don't know who's better and why. We should be ready to face such positions as King's Gambit players!}) 16. Bb5+ Kd8 17. Bc6 Rc8 18. Rae1 {White is two pawns down, but he is very active and it's not easy for Black to untangle. I would enjoy playing this position from the White side, even though it's highly unclear.}) 13. Bb5+ c6 14. gxf3 {Followed by Re1 gives White a strong initiative. The knight on a5 is sidelined and Black still has to spend a tempo on ... O-O-O before he can develop his bishop.}) 7. Bxe6 fxe6 8. d4 Be7 9. Bxf4 O-O 10. Bh2 {A nice prophylactic move.} Nh5 11. g3 {To be followed up by Nbd2 and O-O-O . White has the safer king.}) 6. Nc3 Nc6 {Both sides are trying to develop harmoniously.} (6... Be7 {Black can also try to put pressure on the h4 -pawn this way.} 7. d4 Nh5 8. Ne2 Bg4 {Again, we can't be bothered counting pawns.} (8... Nc6 9. Bxf4 Bg4 10. O-O {We can't save the h4 -pawn - as usual.} O-O 11. Qd2 Bxh4 12. c3 {White has an undeniable initiative. Black has to be precise here because many of his pieces are loose.}) 9. Qd3 Nc6 10. Bd2 O-O 11. O-O-O {The f4 -pawn will fall soon and White has ideas of g2 - g3 too, in order to open files to the black king.}) (6... c6 {This is logical, but playing d5 in two steps shouldn't be too dangerous.} 7. d4 d5 8. exd5 cxd5 9. Bb5+ {An accurate move; rapid development is key.} Nc6 10. Qe2+ {We are usually happy to give the check on e2 , because many of the arising endgames are pleasant for White.} Be7 11. Ne5 Bd7 12. Bxc6 {We give up the bishop pair in favor of quick development.} bxc6 13. Bxf4 O-O 14. g4 $5 {We castle at a right moment - according to the plan!} Bb4 (14... c5 15. O-O-O {To the queenside. How else?} cxd4 16. Rxd4 Bc5 17. Rd3 d4 18. g5 dxc3 19. Qf3 {The knight on f6 is not going anywhere. The queen controls many important squares and protects our bishop. gxf6 is next and in case of ... cxb2 + we always play Kb1 . White's king is safer.}) 15. g5 Ne4 {Black's pieces moved to the queenside, so where do you think we should castle?!} 16. gxh6 g6 17. O-O {Obviously to the kingside: the king is surprisingly safe on g1 !} Bxc3 18. bxc3 Kh7 19. Qg2 Bf5 {Threatening ... f6 .} 20. Bc1 Qc8 21. Rb1 {In my opinion, White has a nice initiative.}) 7. d4 Nh5 8. Nd5 {White is ready to win the pawn back and finish development at the same time.} g5 $6 {This is overoptimistic.} (8... Be6 {A very serious move, and I'd say White is a bit lucky to get away with it.} 9. Qd3 Na5 $2 {Looks like it will be great for Black, but here we have an important trick...} (9... Ng3 10. Rh2 Na5 11. Bb5+ {11.Nxc7+  is somewhat less promising as can be seen in the line 11...Qxc7 12.Bxe6 fxe6 13.Bxf4 Qb6! 14.Bxg3 Qxb2∓ .} c6 12. Nxf4 cxb5 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. Rh3) (9... g5 10. hxg5 hxg5 11. Bd2 {Black needs to be very precise.} Bg7 (11... g4 12. Ng5 {Into the deep end.} Bxd5 (12... Bg7 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. e5 $5 exd5 15. Qg6+ Kf8 16. Bxd5 Qe8 17. Qf5+ Nf6 18. Rxh8+ Bxh8 19. Bxc6 Qxc6 20. exf6 Re8+ {It is worth noting that Black also has to play well to reach this position.} 21. Kf2 {We have to provoke g3 .} g3+ 22. Kf1 Qc4+ 23. Qd3 Qxd3+ 24. cxd3 {All's well that ends well. The bishop will collect the f4 - and g3 -pawns.} Re6 (24... Bxf6 25. Bxf4 Bxd4 26. Bxg3 Re3 $2 (26... Re7 27. Rc1 Ke8 28. Rc4 Bxb2 29. Rb4 {The position is very drawish.}) 27. Bf2 Rxd3 28. Ke2) 25. Rc1 Bxf6 26. Bxf4) 13. exd5 Qxg5 14. dxc6 bxc6 15. O-O-O Be7 (15... d5 16. Bb3 Kd7 17. c4 Bd6 18. Rhe1 Qg6 19. Qc3 Nf6 20. Qa5) 16. Bb3) 12. O-O-O g4 13. Nh4) 10. Nxc7+ $1 Qxc7 11. Bxe6 Ng3 12. Rh3 fxe6 13. Bxf4 Qb6 14. Rxg3 Qxb2 15. Rc1) (8... Ng3 9. Rh2 {I can't guarantee your safety after 9.Rg1 .} Ne7 $1 {This is the move order that really tests White. 9...Nxe4? 10.Qe2+-  followed by Bxf4 and O-O-O is the dream of every King's Gambit player.} 10. Nxf4 Nxe4 11. Qe2 Ng3 {The text move shows why this is not so good.} (11... d5 12. Nxd5 Nxd5 13. Qxe4+ Be6 {The material is equal, but Black has active pieces and no weaknesses. A draw will be our pleasure.} 14. Bd2 Bd6 15. Bxd5 {Alas, there is nothing better than agreeing to an unpleasant endgame.} Bg3+ 16. Kf1 Qxd5 17. Qxd5 Bxd5 18. Rh3 Bd6 {Smoke cleared. Black has two bishops, but White is fortunately in time with the adequate counterplay.} 19. Re1+ Kd8 20. Ne5 f6 (20... b5 21. b3 Re8 22. Bf4 f6 23. Nd3 {Now an opposite-color bishop endgame will appear. Not so much fun, indeed, but draw is surely OK when we play King's Gambit for White!} Bf8 24. Rhe3 Rxe3 25. Rxe3 g5 26. Bg3 Kd7 27. Nc5+ Bxc5 28. dxc5 {For perfectionists, I can add one of the logical forcing lines: 28...Rg8 29.Rd3 Kc6 30.Bxc7 gxh4 31.Rd2 Bxg2+  31...Kxc5 32.Bf4=  32.Rxg2 Rxg2 33.Kxg2 Kxc7 34.b4 f5 35.Kh3 f4 36.Kxh4 h5 37.Kh3=}) 21. Nd3 Re8 22. b3 {Preparing c2 - c4 . White wants to trade off one of the bishops with Nf4 or Bf4 .  Play could continue: 22...Kd7 23.c4 Rxe1+ 24.Bxe1 Be6 25.Re3 Bf5 26.Nc5+ Bxc5 27.dxc5=  Black is slightly better, but the game is extremely likely to end in a draw because of the opposite-colored bishops.}) 12. Qe3 Ngf5 (12... c6 13. Bd2 d5 14. Bd3 {Followed by O-O-O when White has good compensation for the pawn.}) 13. Qg1 {White can continue with logical developing moves: Bd2 , O-O-O and Rg1 . Black needs to watch out for g2 - g4 as well. Basically White will gain lots of time kicking Black's pieces around and he is at least 3 moves away from castling.}) 9. hxg5 hxg5 10. Ng1 $1 g4 11. Ne2 Rh6 12. Qd3 {White regains the f4 -pawn with a large advantage.} *

